      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>STORIES OF THE WAR.</head>
        <p>STORIES OF THE WAR.</p>
        <p>A GUARD'S REFLECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Conscientious Objectors And now To</p>
        <p>Strafe Them.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frank Kay, a member of "The Examiner" reporting staff, now on active ser- vice in Egypt, writes:— Whilst all are keen on returning for the primary reason that England is home and that therein lies, their whole source of happiness, there are others who have various secondary reasons for desiring an early return as evidence a guard I met the other day who wants to get home to " strafe " a certain conscientious objector. I cannot help but say, after he had told his story th,at I sympathised with him, for he appeared to have a very strong case. Or course, opinions diger, so I will relate the story as I got it. I was sitting in a matting and lath hut which we use as an office, trying to catch the mail, which went out the next day, with a letter to well, never mind. It was very neces- sary to save myself from a strafing that I-should catch that mail. I was writing by ,candle- light and had just written a few lines when 1 heard' the sound of a' rifle being brought to the ground from the "slope." " Only the guard having a rest," I said to myself,' and -was pre- paring to continue my letter when the guard accosted me in the familiar fashion so peculiar to all out here, "Well, how's she go?" "Not so bad," I retorted, and being eager to catch that mail I added as a hint for him to leave me alone, "I'm just writing a, letter for to-morrow's mail." That did it. I couldn't have said a worse thing, for it, gave him' the cue which he evidently wanted to enable him to relieve him- self of something which was troubling him very</p>
        <p>much. "Mail!" he ejaculated. "Yes, and I'm goin' to catch that mail, too," he continued, "when I'm relieved of this job." 'Are you married?" he next blurted out in broad Lancashire dialect. The question asked in such a peremptory fashion without anything having happened to lead up to it, made me laugh as I replied in the affirmative, and asked good h.umcruredly, "Why do you want to know?" " Between My Girl And Me."</p>
        <p>"Well," he said, " Yrou'll quite understand my feelings then when I tell you about an affair between my girl and me, well not me, but one o' them ` conscious ' objectors. What's thiak about them fellas? Never ow t to breathe pure air o' England." I motioned impatiently to my letter, but the hint was wasted. My acquaintance was just warming to his subject and, not being a regular "Tommy," I couldn't find the heart to tell him to go to , well, a place not unlike Egypt for heat; so, with a sigh, I meekly submitted to another spasm, hoping in my heart that very, very soon the sound of someone approaching would take him to his duty to give the chal- lenge. But Fate was unkind to me, no one came. .</p>
        <p>Leaning on his rifle, his eyes glistening with enthusiasm and incidentally reading the only two lines of letter I had written—and the letter waAn't to a fellow soldier—he settled down to his story. "It's like this" he continued. "I've been out here and Gallipolly ' for nigh on eighteen month and me and the girl 'as corresponded reglar'. Now her mother is up agen me and doesn't take a fancy to me, so if awt goes wrong she allus makes th' most of it agen M 3. Well, we were up in line for a spell and I missed several mails. Not my fault you know, but anyhow it upset my girl. I suppose her mother had a lot to. do wi' it, and she wrote me a tell- in' off letter. Natural like as I'd bin feighten and couldn't write I were fair raw and vexed. Lads thowt nr gone mad wi' way I cursed. Anyhow, I wrote next day and lowd my girl if she thowt one o' them;' conscience objectors could do better nor me who'd better hey him. I waited for months for a reply to that letter, and at last it come by t' last mail and, such a letter."</p>
        <p>The mention of letter again reminded me of my obligation and I dipped my pen in the ink- pot viciously. This hint did not-go unobserved, but it did not have the desired effect, for With another shuffle to get a more comfortable position against the rifle the guard excused his interruption by saying " I'se not be long and then you can finish your letter, but I want to get this off my chest." •</p>
        <p>"This letter,"- he continued, "is a corker. It appears that some bloke from my town who was at Gallipolly ' has had leave home, and my girl's mother saw him. However he got home I don't know. I know well I 'can't get home and I've tried to 'swing it with varicose veins, but no use. Well, here my girl tells me she had a tiff with her mother and left home. She says that in a row she told her mother that I'd said she'd better hey a f con- scious ' objector, and it appears her mother re- plied, 'Well, perhaps one o' them would do thee more good nor yon neglectful soldier who goes galavantin' round Turkey. and Egypt wi' all their harems and sich like, and never tries to get a pass home to see thee." I laughed, but my guard was quite serious. Suggested Treatment For Objectors.</p>
        <p>"Hard luck," I saki; by way of consolation. "Hard luck," he sneered contemptuously. " think it's do with ' conscious ' objectors—you know them as wants no combatant work?"</p>
        <p>I told him I'd never considered the matter, being content to leave fools, hypocrites and cowards to their own combats in the battle of life. But he'd thought of punishments for them both quaint and humorous.</p>
        <p>"I -wish I were a platoon commander and</p>
        <p>could get one of them objector fellas under me. I'd give him a pair of wire cutters and give him a leg over t' parapet tellin' him to go and cut th' enemy's barbed wire. That would be non-combatant work, but I'll bet that chap would soon be back and say 'Give us a rifle quick.' " "But to put them on cutting trench barbed wire would be combatant work," I said to rectify his error. Quite erroneously the guard jumped to the conclusion that I was not at one with him in his condemnation of those who have a conscientious objection to fighting for the country in wllich they are content to live, and of which they enjoy the privileges of free- dom and equitable justice. "Well," he stammered, as he put his rifle to I his shoulder and prepared to go his rounds, "I'd I get 'era in a front line trench and put their I grub about three yards beyond the parapet to- wards the enemy lines and forbid anyone but them to fetch it. And I'd keep 'em like that until their stomach, which they can feel, beat their conscience, which they can neither feel nor see. I think they'd say 'Give us our grub, we'll tek up a rifle." I *should have liked very much to argue with him as to whether a man can feel his conscience or 'not; bet he was making for the entrance and I—well, I had my letter to finish. I will not add any morei personal comments on such a controversial subject, for I am quite satisfied with the knowledge that I, at any rate, am doing my bit. And when all is over I shall feel happier no doubt than the man who in- curred the guard's anger.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>ME FIGHTING,</head>
        <p>ME FIGHTING,</p>
        <p>As It Is Seen By The Men In The</p>
        <p>Ranks.</p>
        <p>Writing home about a week before he was killed, CorPorai J. E. Hill, of the Royal Welsh Fusiliers, 44, John-street, describes how his regiment went to the attack. He was wounded, retunied to the Front, and was killed. "You will see," he wrote, "I have been wounded, and I know you are getting a little anxious. Cheer up, it is only slight, although it could have been very serious.. You see our division have been doing some hard fighting the last week, and you can consider that I have been very lucky. Since leaving the trenches last we have caught it pretty hot, doing noth- ing but long night marches, and by the time we got to the division's fighting sector it was time for us to go into action. We did, too. You see a division had captured their objective and we had to go for ours the next morning, which was a.great wood. Well, everything went on all right that night, and we got relieved by the other half of our brigade, who were making a charge, the following -morning, and with us taking the line over our battalion was put in reserve. We had hardly got to reserve 'when we were called back again. We got there, and my word, what a surprise we had. We got sorted out for the third attempt, and it was my luck to be left-hand section of the rear line. We had 15ayonets fixed and every- thing was ready, when the Adjutant came and we got the order to unfix bayonets. The charge was cancelled and, my word, I was glad. Many times I prayed to get out of it safe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Germans Bayoneted Our Wounded.</head>
        <p>Germans Bayoneted Our Wounded.</p>
        <p>"By this time night had come on, and our stretcher-bearers, who, were collecting wounded, say that they saw the Germans come out of the wood and start bayoneting our wounded. Well, that made my blood run cold. Often I wished then we had gone over and stuck the lot of them. I don't suppose you will hear about that bit of German work. The reason the thing was cancelled wfis that plans had been rearranged and, of course, we went back to _reserve for a short rest. From our reserve we could see all that was going on in the front line. I saw a division go over, and, my word, what a sight it was. I Ichow you will read about the steadiness of our troops, but you can't realise—seeing is believing—and, my word, they were fine, no running and going mad over the thing. They walked quite calmly. I can't tell you how they did go. It was fine, and to make things better our artillery played 'havoc with the German position. While watching this I did not think for one moment that our division were attack- ing almost at the same time, and with our brigade having sá many casualties we were reserve brigade for the division. Well, things went on fine all that day, and we were not called upon until 2 o'clock the following morning. We got up the line about six. Of course, we went all round for the sake of dodging the shells, and it took us all that time to get. about 500 yards. We assembled in a deep valley to form up into artillery formation, as shells were dropping like hail. One company got over the ridge all right, and, of course, it came to our turn. We divided into sections, and when I got up on to to see when our best time to go was, a 'coal box' came about 20 yards from me, and, of course, a piece about the size of an egg landed me full bang in the left kidney. Down I went with a bang, and I remembergd nothing until I found myself all of a heap at the bottom oi the ridge. My word,,talk about Joe Wads- worth hitting me in the kidneys, it was not in it, I thought a steam hammer had caught me. Luckily enough, ,it only made a slight bruise, but it is the shock I am suffering from. That even is not much, but take it from me, I um having a good rest ,and four' meals a day. It seemed a treat to see bread here after six days on biscuits and bully. While here I have met plenty of ,the boys out of my f3latoon, and, ray word, what a tale ,they had. They got to the wood all right. They say it is worse than Loos. You see the Germans had it like a palace. I The prisoners, say that , they go there from Verdun on rest, in fact in one dug-out there was a piano and plenty of grub—don't say the Germans are short. Anyhow, when our boys got there they had to quit and be smart about it, too.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Germans Surrendering.</head>
        <p>Germans Surrendering.</p>
        <p>"You ought to see the prisoners giving them- selves up, old men and young lads, too, but mind you, the size of them is wonderful, and Fü is the 'stupidness of them. I believed , nearly 100 of them gave _themselves up' to _six chaps. _ _</p>
        <p>"Our lot meant to take that woo il and take it they did in fine style, but at what a cost:"</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
