      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Butter or Margarine? At last week's meeting of the Board of Guardians, Mr. W. J. Shaw seemed anxious that the inmates of the Workhouse should be supplied with butter inffead of _margarine, and he alleged that existing conditions caused uneasiness outside. I suppose it will hardly be pretended that the consumption of margarine •causits uneasiness inside. What, quality of butter 'Would Mr. Shaw and his friends sub- stitute for the really good margarine now used? I have a relative who has for many years been sister in a Yorkshire Union Hos- pital, and she longs for the very change that has evidently lead to such heartsearching on the part of Mr. Shaw and some others. She says the strong butter now served in the insti- tution is simply horrible, and all concerned would greatly prefer margarine—the lingering misgiving :as to which in some quarters is due to unreasoning preiudice.</p>
        <p>The 'Mayoralty. - We are all glad to be assured that Alderman Peter Peacock is to continue Mayor of War- rington -for 'a foid.th year. Apart from personal considerations, one could not at present con- temPlate with equanimity the possibility of supercession in the exalted ;office which the woithy "holder has so admirably 'filled and adorned over a term already equal to that for Which his most honoured predecessors have served. Thus his Worships • experience is destined to ;constitute a record. The -case is somewhat similar to that of an East Lanca- shire borough in which I am ,nrerestetl, having formerly spent some years there. Eight along from -incorporation to the time When I 'left, it I appeared to have been the rule for the Mayor for the time being to serve for three years, but I I learn that it was broken owing to the war, and that although in ordinary circumstances the Chief Magistrate there in 1914 would have retired toward the end of that year, his Wor- ship still holds the reins of office, and is to be re-elected for a further twelve- months. An Awkward Situation.</p>
        <p>Years ago I lived in a town the exceedingly' wealthy Mayor of which evidently enjoyed the position and work very much. He had been at the head of affairs for several years, and other aspirants for succession thereto, while they could not openly rebel, were known to chafe under the embarrassing state of things. At this period each year, out of courtesy and pur- suant to local custom, the Council formally in- vited his Worship to serve as Mayor for yet another year, and the distinguished one, flat-' tered by the insincere speeches made i]r• his praise time after time, 1/4graciously consented— to the grOwing despair and disgust of prospec- tive candidates for the coveted seat of honour.</p>
        <p>Not of Local Application. Let me hasten to guard against misappre- hension here—merely as a precaution, that is. The case just referred to is not for a moment comparable with that of Warrington or of the other place in question, nor is the implied criticism in any sense applicable to these. That, of course, 99 per cent. of my readers will quite understand, but it shopld be clear to all. It will be equally plain to everyone that in normal times it is an excellent plan to ensure the regular circulation of these municipal honours. It is pleasant to think that there is a practically exhaustless store of patriotic energy in our citizenship; but, of course, if men of education and ability are to be expected to bear their part freely and disinterestedly in the great field of public service, we must always be pre- pared to recognise the claim of any eligible aspirant for the highest honour the community has at disposal—nay, I would rather urge that we must ever continue to be so prepared, for our representatives have hitherto been so guided and I at least perceive no signs ot deterioration in that respect. The Mayor-elect may be confidently assured of the cordial and sincere congratulations and the grateful thanks of the burgesses. Gratitude, you know, has been wittily defined as "a lively sense of favours to come," and in this particular in- stance the prevailing sentiment in relation to Alderman 'Peacock may certainly be so inter- preted.</p>
        <p>• Arresting Headlines. " Vigilant " has a' happy facility in coining these; as noted in last week's " Gossip," his "Might of the Mite" has carried his fame to the Metropolis. Alone he did it. I happen to have done likewise into the teens of years ago, though I was too slow—or may I say too ex- cessively modest (a-hem !)—to take the small prize to which my achrevement entitled me. I had been appointed to a little town where it was customary to abstain from street lighting three days before and three days after the date markecl, on the almanac as "full moon." But Phceba often failed to keep her \ tryst; then darkness covered the face of that towri and gross ,darkness the people. At length sundry and divers bold and enterprising spirits on the Town Council determined, in a reckless, bang- went-saxpence sort of-way, that the public lamps of that theretofore benighted borough should be nightly lit continuously throughout the winter months Now to my point: one of the head- lines of my report of the memorable proceedings ran, "Dissolving partnership With th 6 Moon," and a smart inhabitant of those) parts lost no time in sending the example to a periodical offering prizes for such things, and was awarded a guinea fpr it. "Alone hel did it"; yes, in- deedy, as Sam Slick was wont to express it.</p>
        <p>Hard Lines, But That gallant member of the " Examiner " staff, Mr. Frank Kay, who has go long served with the Colours without holiday, has his own characteristic serio-comic way of commenting upon the situation. As he well knows,, there are many to share his grievance, and at the same time join in thundering an emphatic denial that they are in the least downhearted. One of my three soldier-nephews enlisted in the R.F.A. immediately on th 4 outbreak of war, and a very few months' later was actively engaged on the Western Front. At last he is fain to confess that he would like a week's leave or so, but—doubtless for good and sufficient reasons—he has been unable trrobtain it. An old young colleague of mine has covered 20,000 miles as a dispatch rider in Northern France, and, needless to state, has had various adven- tures and narrow escapes. No wonder he, too, begins to feel a little homesick, but leave is in his case also out of the question. Aeronautics.</p>
        <p>A friend of mine Anvites us to imagine Mat Prior—minor poet as well as politician and diplomatist, of the age of Queen Anne (since deceased, according to eminent authorities' repeated assurance) and of the "wee, wee German lairdie " of the Scots Jacobite ballad (George I.)--,imagine Mat Prior„ in the "silver age" of English literature, sitting over a pipe and a glass of home-brewed ale with Pope and Swift, Addison and Steele. One of the company declares that he has been the subject of a supernatural communication. In a vision it ba,,• been revealed to him that, in less- than 200 years ahead, men will fly like birds through the air; that the flight of birds, then only in a figurative sense to be imitated by man, would be actually emulated in the air by men and women. Mat Prior and the wits around him then would have heaped scorn _and derision upon such a dreamer. But the dream has come true. The conquest of the air is not only well begun, but it is much more 'than half done. The Farman Biplane.</p>
        <p>These reflections were: induced as on Satur- day afternoon I gazed skyward and watched the graceful flight of a Farman biplane. I am At the end of my tether, and may not dwell on the matter, but perhaps I may just be allowed to repeat one of the favowite stories of Mr. Orville Wright, the fanious flying man. It is a story about a man who called at a biplane selling agency and said: "Mt older brother bought a biplane here last month. You told him if any- thing broke you'd supply the missing parts." "Yes. Well?" said the manager. "Well," said the caller, "my brother wants you to supply him right away with six new ribs, a left leg, half a dozen assorted finger, and a medium-sized Roman hose."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</head>
        <p>THE LATCHFORD CADET CORPS.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending October 6th, 1916:— Parades.—Tuesday Parades are suspended until further notice. Friday: Fall in at 7.30 prompt, Company Drill, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Orderlies of the week.—Corporal E. Lea and Lance-Corporal Ashton.</p>
        <p>J. B. STARKEY, Commanding Officer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>RIFLEMAN W. H. HOWARD.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN W. H. HOWARD.</p>
        <p>ounded Again Mille Mending A Captured German Trench.</p>
        <p>Rifleman William H. Howard, son of Mr. and Mrs. A. Howard, 36, Orford-avenue, has been wounded for the second time. Writing frpm a French hospital, he says: "I have been wounded again, but not quite so seriously as before. I stopped a piece of shrapnel last Friday, the 15th, in the big push,' when our battalion went over the top. I managed to get to the place which we had to take. We were trying to get the trench a little bit stronger, when I got wounded. It hit my chin and then my throat, and it was a good job it did catch my chin first as it stopped the force of it. I was attended by a doctor as soon as I was hit, and if he hadn't been there I would have been 'landowner' by now. As soon as the Germans quietened down with the shelling I got out of the way as soon as possible. The Germans are a lot of cowards. They fight as long as you like until we get, on top of them, and then they throw their rifles down and cry for mercy. After any of the German wounded are left be-</p>
        <p>RIFLEMAN W. H. HOWARD. Wounded. ~</p>
        <p>Wakefield, South Lancashire Regiment, has been wounded. On Wednesday his parents went. to see him in hospital. His pal, Private Harold Smart, who resided next door, was also wounded. Private Wakefield, who is the elder son, was gassed in June of last year: He is 22 years —of age, and was formerly. employed by Messrs. A. Waring and Co., Ltd., tanrers, Winwiek-road.</p>
        <p>worry about, only I haVe lost my voice. I was digging with my company wh,ni a" shell burst behind me. I remembered nothing further until I arrived in hospital."</p>
        <p>Private Appleton, who is nineteen years of age, and was educated. at Beamont School, was formerly employed in the hairdressing estab- lishment of Mr. Freeman, Friars' Green. He enlisted almost eleven mOnths ago, and had been at the Front ten weeks. He worshipped at the Wycliffe Congregational Church, and played cricket with the Wycliffe team. His father, Mr. John Appleton, is in Scotland with the National Reserve.</p>
