        <p>PRIVATE J. APPLETON. Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>MISSING.</head>
        <p>MISSING.</p>
        <p>RIFLEMAN R. UNSWORTH.</p>
        <p>War Office Say " Missing" ;</p>
        <p>Comrades " He Is Dead."</p>
        <p>Official news has reached Mrs. Unsworth, 55, Hardy-street, that her husband, Rifleman Robert Unsworth, of the K.R.R., has been miss- ing since August 24th. In addition to the official news, a letter has reachell Warrington from a local man 'famed Hindes: It appears that the latter and Rifleman' Unsworth made an agreement that if anything happened to either of them the other should write home and con- vey the news. This Hindes has done; and in a letter to his wife he says he inquired of three men belonging to the same regiment as Rifle-</p>
        <p>son, Private Joseph Jones,,of the K. 0.5.8., has been posted as missing after ay_ engagement on September 3rd. Official news to this effect has been received.</p>
        <p>Private Jones, who is twenty-seven years of age, enlisted about twelve months ago, and had been at the Front ten months. He was edu- cated at Holy Trinity School, and was formerly employed by /Messrs. John Melbourne, Ltd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>ASSAULTS ON POLICEMAN.</p>
        <p>Magistrates Disagree As To Punishment Deserved.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor , (in the chair), Aldermen Bolton, Smethurst and James Evans, and Messrs. S. Wright, W. Furness, G. Pilling and T. Locker, A man of the labouring class named John Bartley, no fixed abode, pleaded- guilty when charged with being drunk and disorderly in Barbauld-street on Saturday night, and with assaulting Special Inspector Mason. Special Inspector Mason stated that at 9.35 on Saturday evening he was in Barbauld-street visiting patrols. Prksonet, who was in a drunken condition, was shouting after two women. When he- came towards witness he acted in an 'indecent' manner and witness arrested him. Defendant then raised his right hand, clenched, and struck witness on the jaw. Witness grappled with defendant and they both went down to the ground. Having struggled for some time he managed to blow his whistle for assistance, which did not come immediately. As his right arm was pot yet sound, prisoner got away and ran towards StanleyLstreet. Wit- ness overtook him and closed with him. Another struggle ensued and prisoner succeeded in entering a lodging-house. Detectives Ander-</p>
        <p>ton and Turner Came to his assistanct. The officers brought him out of the lodging-house and ,Special Sergeant Brad- burn and witness brought him to the Police. Station. In Museum-street prisoner again be- came violent, and it was found necessary to put on the handcuffs. Prisoner said he was sorry for what he had done. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ The magistrates, the Mayor announced, had failed to agree upon. a decision, and the case would be re-tried on the 'following day.</p>
        <p>Conduct Not To Be Tolerated.</p>
        <p>At Tuesday's Court, when prisoner was again in the dock, the magistrates were : Councillor Henry Roberts and Messrs. John Rymer Young, Thomas Locker and C. Jackson</p>
        <p>Holmes, The Chief Constable (Mr. Nicholls) said the charge had been heard the 'previous day, but</p>
        <p>the Bench could not agree to what sentence should be pronounced, and they adjourned the case;</p>
        <p>- Special Inspector Mason having repeated his story, The Chief Constable said that defendant had no settled place of abode. He was a labourer, and did not belong to Warrington. On behalf of the regular police he desired to point out very strongly this assault on an officer. This man struck a Special Inspector in the face, and unless they could guarantee the support of</p>
        <p>the Bench to support these men it was not right to those who were coming forward to ren- _ _ _</p>
        <p>der services to the town of which it was very badly in need at the present time. If it was one of his own constables he wQuld not mind so much, but these men, who were giving their services free and so much of their time, should be protected. The Chairman said the Beneh were deter-</p>
        <p>- mined to support the action a their Special Constables quite as strongly as they would sup- port the action of the ordinary constables. This kind of conduct would not be tolerated. Defendant was 'fined 305., or, in default, a month's imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>TOO-SPEEDY AMBULANCES</head>
        <p>TOO-SPEEDY AMBULANCES</p>
        <p>A Fine And A Warning To Hospital Motor Drivers.</p>
        <p>It was alleged at the Borough Police Court on Thursday that the motor ambulances in connection with the Lord Derby Military Hospital, Winwick, are driven at too great a speed 'generally, and a warning was given &amp;tir- ing a ease in which Private Thomas Livesley was charged with driving at an excessive speed and to the danger of the public. Special Inspector Richardson said that on September 12th one of his horses and carts was crossing Marson-street into Tanner's-lane, when Livesley came flying by with a motor ambulance, crashing into the horse and cart and knocking off part of the shafting. Livesley did not stop. Livesley said he was not going more than four miles an hour; he was only on second gear. The Special: He was going at from twelve to fifteen miles an hour.</p>
        <p>A witness went even so far as to fix the speed as high as seventeen miles per hour. A Lieutenant from the hospital said that Livesley was driving some dangerous lunatics 'and dared not stop; ,moreover, he did not know he had totiched the horse and cart. The Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls): It might have been one of the lunatics who was driving. (Laughter.) The Lieutenant put Lance-Corporal Franklin, who is in charge of the motor ambulance garage, into the witness box, and that witness said that it was impossible , for a driver who was impossible for a driver who was on second gear to go seventeen miles an hour with that motor.</p>
        <p>Chief Constable Surprised.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable remarked that ,he was surprised to find that the authorities of the Lord Derby War hospital supported their drivers tearing about in the way they did. It was 'a 'positive disgrace that those vehicles should proceed at so high a speed. Anybody who was about Warrington when the convoys were coming in would agree. After they had emptied they proceeded at a great rate. Only the other afternoon he saw one of them turn round Bridge 'Foot at twenty-five miles hour.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. A. Jolley) said that ou the, evidenCe, apart from general considera- tions they considered the prosecution had proved their case. As it was the first case that had been brought before • the Coue- it would be dealt With leniently, but if any other cacei were brought the. fine. would ,he much more heavy. Fine: 205., the licence to be end-r..sel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WORTHLESS CIGARETTES.</head>
        <p>WORTHLESS CIGARETTES.</p>
        <p>Londoner Defrauds Two Local Tradespeople.</p>
        <p>Already serving a sentence for a similar offence, James McKay, of 3, Ballance-road, Homerton, London, was brought from Derby Prison and Charged at the Warrington Borough Police Court on Thursday with obtaining £2 is. 6d. and £4 Is. by false pretences from two tradespeople in different parts of the town.' Through Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, he pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable 'said that the prisoner in Septembpr of last year went to one of the prosecutors; who kept,an off-licence, and asked him to give a good display of some herbal cigarettes. Various inducements were offered, including one that his place would be made the depot for the district. He was tcld that it was usual to pay for the first elivery and if the) were unsold or he was not satisfied with them the money would he returned in a month. The cigarettes were also to be excvely adver- tit,ed in the town and district. In view of that this prosecutor gave the prisoner £2 fs. 6d. It was afterwards found that the cigarettes were worthless, and there was no, call for them. The other prosecutOr was told a similar story and paid £4 is. Until to-day the parties had not met the prisoner again. It was a most despicable fraud. Setitence: Three months' impriSonment, to run. from Thursday.</p>
        <p>"ALL RIGHT IN WARRINGTON," BUT NOT IN RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on Monday, James Isherwood, taxi-driver, Bedford-street, Stockton Heath, was fined 10s. for not having obscured the lights of a car which he was driving at Sutton Weaver on the night of the 14th September. Sergeant McKenna said that the defendant, upon being asked why he did not obscure the lights, replied: "They're all right in War- rington."</p>
        <p>Lots to Talk About. Madge: "Did you have anything to talk about at the club meeting F" • MarjOrie: "Lots! On account of the storm there were only three of us present."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>TREACLE ON THE RUN.</head>
        <p>TREACLE ON THE RUN.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Firm's Against Railway CompanY. Last week, in • the County Court, before' his Honor Judge Scott Fox, a case was heard in which Bowman's (Warrington) Limited,- were plaintiffs, and the ,London and North-. Western Railway. Company 'defendants. The plaintiffs' claim was-"for £22 7s. dainegf.6' for loss of treacle, caused by the alleged IWO- gence of the defendant company. . The defence was that the casks in which the: treacle was conveyed were not suitable. for the purpose. Mr. ,Louis Kelly, barrister (instructed 14 - Mess`rs. Robert Davies and Co., Warringtt‘ appeared for the plaintiffs, and Mr. M. C. Tel ' appeared for the company., Mr. Kelly said that, the claim arose out of the transit of certain' goods in June, 1915, tween Garston and Warrington. These g°o"4.'" were damaged to such an extent in transit the' a large portion was lost. Bowman's, Limited, ' had ordered 48 casks of treacle from Crosfiell Oil Cake Company, Liverpool, at the rate? 4s. 71-d. per cwt. That purchase was made April, 1915. On the sth JUne twenty es-skst,, were delivered to the -defendants' vanmen Garston by the British Molasses Company, ac:- ing as agents for Crosfield'S Oil Cake Com Pall." They arrived at Warrington upon the 1°13,4 June and the casks were In such a. battere`ci condition, some- with the staves broken in ter others with the ends broken, that, as a mat Lei; of fact, the treacle was streaming very freell off the loiries, much to the delight of the 501,8,..s boys who congregated when they heard of t"1:9 pleasant news. (Laughter.) Any of the cesp. which lent themselves to the operation werde salved, with the result that eleven were salv;e„; and the claim was in respect of the remain nine: Between- April and June the price c'cL molasses had gone tip to 6s. 74-d. per cwt., _lan, on that basis the claim of £22'7s. was'nl,r-; Mr. Kelly then read a letter from the Rail-I0 Company, -in which the statement was nia that the casks were unsuitable for that class of traffic. . His Hon Our : Have they brought a coullte,r; claim fop the damage done to their lorry 1" your treacle? (Laughter.) Mr. Kelly: No, your Honour. Evidence was given on behalf of the to the effect that the casks were fully.co*Pei.he and tenter-hooked when handed over to '- railway company's representative. yas9 For the defendant company, John Tli!1,- Gaskell, checker in their employment at "ail rington, described the casks as seconl-111e ones. They were leaking badly through staves and the hoops were falling off. - Wm. Warburton, chief delivery clerk at War; rington, said the last witness drew his atteuti°; to the condition of the casks, all of which leaking. The hoops had slackened and ends of the casks had warped. Plaintiffs fused, to take the treacle and it was brougu'r, back. He called in the services of a o°, PL/1 and, having procured six fresh casks, 'eLe-- basks of treacle were salved. aid Richard Singleton, chief traffic inspector, 6 A he saw the twenty casks in the waigVn there was no evidence of Careless h.andling bad shunting. Thomas Albinson, of the firm of JoseP Adbinson, coopers and cask makers„ -Warring. ton, said he was called in by the railway' cA9nl- - to examine the casks in question. rchreZ were in a shocking condition and very old. 'did not consider that they were proper cil's to carry treacle. By Mr. Kelly: It would not improve barre.ls to keep them for four days in hot weather 111 an open ,truck. ot His Honour said that the casks turned 0 t not to be adequate to carry the treacle W1t11,°16,14 leaking, and he did not think it was Pru'rof that the railway company were guilty of negligence in dealing with these barrels. therefore gave judgment for the defendan'" with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>IT'S A MANLY ACT.</head>
        <p>IT'S A MANLY ACT.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Man Speaks Out" A plain straightforward statement. A grateful acknowledgment of help. Telling a personal experience. Making it everywhere known. It's a manly act. vi/IX It's manly because it helps others by real° _ _</p>
        <p>every doubt. 0115 No better help could be wished thall assurance' from Runcorn.</p>
        <p>On March 10th, 1915, Mr. J. Clucas, of 3, C'l;', 1 wright-street, Halton-road; Runcorn, said A have been a martyr in my time to sciatica' to ' was once so bad with it that I was unall:r do any work for eleven month. Even ai;io,s did get about again I was continuallY ba.tho bad bouts. The pains used to run dowe, sciatica nerve from my right hip to ray a-</p>
        <p>and I was properly crippled with it. "I tried embrocationsond all kinds of 0 meat, but I can truly say that nothing d_no'S any lasting good. until I started taking 1,01 Backache Vldney Pills. I was very bad I started with these pills, but they soon ga'aio relief, and as I persevered with them the t of gradually Nft me. Whenever I get a toile .05; the old trouble now I take a few of these Eelf: for they always give me immediate re'" (Signed) "J. Clucas." On February 23rd, 1916—nearly twelve ili6":00 later—Mr. Clucas said:—" I still have the I;eerl high opinion of Doan's Pills. They have -.60 worth pounds to me, and I always recogh" • them." stel' Of all dealers, or 2/9 a box, from feet, McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford London, W. Don't ask for backache or ifictaael pills,—ask distinctly for Doan's Backache -Pi</p>
        <p>Pillls, the same as Mr. Clucas had.</p>
        <p>DISAPPOINTED AVITH AAVAIIPI</p>
        <p>Sheet-Metal Workers Demand A</p>
        <p>Rise.— claf Having claimed 6s. per week for their thE' workers and 15 per cent. for piece woriccr';dist sheet metal- trade employes met in the S°c..- Hall, Bridge Foot, on ues T d •0. and ay:eveniut,, f pressed dissatisfaction with the award eri r. o„0- all-round advance of 3s. per week by .thnrge mittee of Production, over which Sir ‘-'e- Askwith' presides. They passed a resolution doll:611a° oa advance of 10s. per week for day Ivol.k6r'; 20 per cent. for piece workers</p>
