      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <p>tztters toot Oita</p>
        <p>CONCERTS FOR THE WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l fully agree with " Amateur's " sug- gestion that some lady or gentleman should be asked 'to undertake the responsibility of organising good concerts for the wounded Tommies " during the coming winter. This Is the only opportunity some will get to do some- thing for the lads, who are doing so much for us, and I feel aure if anyone will move in the ]natter, there will be a good response from the artistes of Warrington. I have twice had the pleasure of entertaining wounded " Tommies " ' at the Infirmary, and I may safely say that I have never had a more appreciative audience. "Come again" they said. I can assure you they will feel well repaid after the season.</p>
        <p>ENTERTAINER</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>SAT ON A FIRE.</head>
        <p>SAT ON A FIRE.</p>
        <p>Terrible Scene In A Rylands</p>
        <p>Street Shop.</p>
        <p>BOY'S AWFUL DISCOVERY.</p>
        <p>Telling her young nephew that he could go out for a walk for a couple of hours on Sunday evening, and that if anything happened to her he was to inform her uncle, Mrs. Edith rieesom, a draper, of 11, Rylands-street, was found on his return to be seated on the kitchen fire, her head up the chimney, her feet dangling from the grate, and lier clothes in a biaze. The boy ran fcr a police- man, upon whose arrival she was dead, por- tions of her body being scorched away and her face charred and covered with soot. It was a terrible sight. _ _</p>
        <p>, The inquest was held on Wednesday after- noon, and the proceedings were of a remarkable character.</p>
        <p>THE HUSBAND'S EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>The husband, Charles Heesom, a general labourer, residing at the Girls' High School, nalifax, said he had not been living with hit Wife for twenty-one months, and had not seen her during that time. He had, however, corre- sponded with her and occasionally sent her small sums of money. ' He heard from her as recently as a fortnight ago, and she seemed to be all right. She had never complained of being in financial difficulties, although she said she suffered from asthma and pains in the head.' The Coroner (Mr. S. Brighouse) : Why did yo., Part ?-0n account of business. The war caused it to go down and I left her to look after it 'while I went and worked elsewhere. I was compelled to work, and did not want to do so in Warrington. " There were no domestic difficulties?-oh, no. Have you any family?—No.</p>
        <p>So your wife stayed here to carry on the business while you went to Halifax to work?— Yes.</p>
        <p>Was this a mutual arrangemer. between you and your wife?-oh, yes. ' She agreed that I should go on account of the business. Have you any idea why she might have taken her life ?—No.</p>
        <p>You have not gOt the last letter she wrote you? —No. - -</p>
        <p>_ When you left her did this little boy, a nephew—Wilfred Lyndon Heesom—go and stay With her ?—Yes. He came after I went away. Did anyone live in the house except these rwo?—A lady and gentleman lodged there at One time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>BOY'S HORRI BLE DISCOVERY.</head>
        <p>BOY'S HORRI BLE DISCOVERY.</p>
        <p>The boy referred to was then called. Nearly 14 years of age, he gave his evidence fluently and lucidly. He said he had lived with his aunt for three or four months and had acted as her errand boy. She had complained to him of severe pains in the head and had also suffered very much from asthma. When the pains were very bad she would go upstairs and sometimes Bay, "If anything happens tell Uncle Frank," Meaning Mr. Frank Turner, ex-superintendent of the Warrington Fire Brigade. On Sunday night she looked very pale. At seven o'clock witness asked her if he could go out for a walk.</p>
        <p>‘` She replied that I could go for two hours," Went on the lad, "and if on my return I found anything the matter with her I was to run for Uncle Frank. I returned about a quarter to nine o'clock and on entering the shop smelt something burning. On going into the kitchen I found auntie „sitting on the top of the fire With her head up the chimney. I could just see her face. Her clothes were all on fire atm I tried to get her away, but could not. I then ran and told the policeman at Market Gate."' • P.C. Harrison said the woman was absolutely dead when he got to the scene. In a cupboard he found a note, which she had written, as follows, "Dear Wilfred,—Give -Uncle Frank the money." Mr. Turner was sitting in the well ..of the Court and Mr. T. S. Steel was present as his solicitor. The Coroner asked whether Mr. Turner could, say anything about the matter, and he replied that he could not, only to say that the lad took him the note, and on going there the police, were already on the scene. The Coroner: What' does the note mean—that she owed 'Mr. Turner some money? Mr. Steel: Yes.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: What was the trouble between her and her husband ? Is this a true story I have heard? Mr. Steel consulted Mr. Turner and. replied, 'She never said anything about it to Mr. Turner."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>CONSTABLE'S THEORY.</head>
        <p>THE CONSTABLE'S THEORY.</p>
        <p>'turning to the Constable, the Cor'oner asked whether Mrs. Heesom could have got into the Position accidentally? "No," he rejoined. " She could not She must have deliberately sat on the fire. In my opinion she must have got .hold of the mantelpiece, edged herself up the chimney and lowered herself on to the fire."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>SOMETHING JURIES NEVER 'LEARN.</head>
        <p>SOMETHING JURIES NEVER 'LEARN.</p>
        <p>Addressing the jury, the Coroner remarked that in this kind of case there was always a great deal which they never learned. " You get to know it some time after the verdict has been s given," he continued. "Of course, there is something at the back of this. No woman Would put herself on the fire unless there was ilorne reason. We all want to live as long as We can; not one of us wants to terminate his or her life without cause." So saying, the Coroner left the mystery wfh the jury and they returned an open verdict.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place on Thursday after- -neon at the Warrington Cemetery. There .Lvere present, among inany others, Mr. Charles esom: (husband), Mr. Turner (father), Mrs. nton and Mrs. Jepson (sisters-in-law), Mrs., waitson (niece) Miss G. Hinton, Mr. Frank 'Turner (uncle), Mr. and Mrs.' Frank Turner', -Mr. Allen, Mr. Reyes, Mr. and Mrs. Carmen, Mrs. Potter, Mr. and Mrs. Cook. Wreaths and floral 'tributes were inscribed: le loving memory from her husband," With deepest sympathy, from Mrs. Hinton," ats. Jepson. Mrs. Watson. The Rev. J. G. H. Hollingworth read the service.</p>
        <p>The funeral arrangements were carried out Daessrs. MounAeld anct.SQ4.s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>YOUNG GENERATION.</head>
        <p>THE YOUNG GENERATION.</p>
        <p>Councillor R. Henshall On</p>
        <p>Spiritual Wellbeing.</p>
        <p>• Special meetings in ,onnection with the War- rington Sunday School Union were held in the Wycliffe Memorial Hall on Saturday afternoon and evening.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. Melville Harris, secretary or Young People's Department of the Congrega- tional Union, delivered two very helaul addresses.</p>
        <p>Tea was served in the Hall, and was followed by a tea-table conference. Mr. Arthur Moun- field presided, and spoke on "The religious life of the scholar, its awakening, development and direction." The Revs. W. M. Harris and P. meson, Miss Vanda Taylor, Mr. A. Hoyle, Mr. J. Griffiths, and others also spoke. - Value of Religious Training.</p>
        <p>Councillor R. Henshall presided at the seven o'clock meeting, which was well attended, and said there was never a period in their history when they needed to emphasise so much as no 4 the spiritual aspect of the education of the child. They were spending well over £l,OOO a week in Warrington, wisely or foolishly, oi. "training the young idea how to shoot," and so far as he was able to judge, as a member of the Education Committee, the general defini- tion of education merely meant the sharpen- ing of the intellectual qualities. The moral qualities were nearly always left out. A privilege and a solemn obligation • were laid upon every Sunday school teacher to see that a spirit of reverence—he did not mean ,merely reverence in the Conventional sense—and lovu and affection pure and undefiled, grew up in their scholars. After the war was over they, might probably spend their money less foolishly on the intellectual education of the child, but surely, there was abundant evidence that they had whollk missed the mark if they merely trained the mind and left the heart untoucheu. The only way permanently to infliAence the scholar was by the example of the teacher. The Rev. W. M. Harris took as the subject of his address, "The call of the kingdom." He urged upon Sunday school teachers the import- ance of being thorough and earnest in their work. God's call was for skilled workmen, not merely casual labourers. \Teachers must he living teachers, and they muAt ever be learners. They must themselves remain scholars right up to .the end of the chapter, And if they did they would know how to handle the young and beautiful life placed in their care in such a way that they would never brush off the bloom. They must seize the opportunity when it pre- sented itself, and if they did they would indeed become workmen "who need not be ashamed." There was no office on earth more important than that of the teacher. .(Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>CHILDREN'S SERVICE.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S SERVICE.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon a delightful united scholars' service was held in Bold-street Wes- leyan Church, this handsome building being tastefully decorated with harvest trophies, it is 'a pity that the older people did not attend in larger numbers. As it was, however, the fine gathering of children overflowed from the galleries into the body of the church. Rev. Percy Meson was the chairman, and a portion of SO Mark's Gospel was read by the Rev. C. P. Pitt. There was a special hymn sheet, and Miss Minnie Whitmore contributed a solo most effectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Better,. Sweeter, Purer Day,</head>
        <p>Better,. Sweeter, Purer Day,</p>
        <p>The speaker was Alderman Townend, of Middleton, who has an extraordinary gift in speaking to child audiences and, indeed, to grown-ups, too. Each child he saw there, said Alderman Townend, reminded him of a notice he once saw on a vacant mill—" Power—to let.'• What were those boys and girls going to do: what power had they to- let? What was the life they were going to live? They should re- member that they had come from God and that they had to go back to God and, keeping that/ fact in view, they should make up their minds and hearts to go out into the world to do some- thing for God. Out of all this stress and tur- moil of the present time he hoped to see a better, sweeter and purer time for the young generation he saw before him. He wanted each of them to go out into the world as a soldier of Jesus Christ and gave them as their motto, "Do it to-day." He told them a story of Ruskin, that great writer whose books, when they grew up, they would revel in reading. He had a white piece of marble on the desk lb his study, and on this piece of marble he had painted the one .word, "To-day."</p>
        <p>Juvenile collectors took up the offering. The members of the Boys' Brigade attended with their band.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>SWIMMING GALA.</head>
        <p>SWIMMING GALA.</p>
        <p>Secondary School Students'</p>
        <p>Prowess Rewarded.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon the annual swim- ming gala for the students at the Secondary School took place at the Baths. The students were present in good numbers to cheer their favourites on to victory. The competitors themselves were in splendid condition, and some good swimming and diving were witnessed. The life-saving competition was excellently performed. Perhaps the item that created the most amusement was the hunting of the whistle, in which the hunters all blino- foldpd, chased by sound the elusive person with the whistle.</p>
        <p>After the programme had been gone through, the winners were rewarded for their skill in this particular direction by the presentation ~f certificates by Mrs. Cann, who received a bouquet from Miss Lucy Lygo, the girls' cham- pion. At the close the Principal, Mr. J. S. Broome, thanked Mrs. Cann for her kindness in coming and for the way in which she had distributed the certificates. The vote was cordially passed by the students, and Mrs. Cann suitably replied.</p>
        <p>RESU LTS. The list of winners and the respective items they won follows : Boys.</p>
        <p>Bfginners.-1, Woodward; 2, Glenton; 3, E Wooldridge.</p>
        <p>Two Lengths.-1, Critchley; ,J. Darbyshire; 3, E. Birtles.</p>
        <p>Four Lengths.-1, Critehley; 2, H. Lee; 3, A Jones. Neat Dive.-1, J. Dargshire; 2, H. Healey. Floating (one length).—J. Darbyshire. Squadron Race.—J. Darbyshire's Team. Hunting the Whistle.—A. Hale.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Girls.</head>
        <p>Girls.</p>
        <p>Beginners.-1, L. Plinston; 2, M. Knight; 3, J. Dunlop.</p>
        <p>One length.-1, B. Grounds; 2, L. Lygo; 3, O. Balthford.</p>
        <p>Two Lengths.-1, L. Lygo; 2, 0. Balmford; 3, M. Critchley. Neat Dive.-1, M. Crithley; 2, D. Broome.' Life-saving.-0. Balmford and M. Souther- den. Squadron Race.—B. Lee's Team. Hunting the Whistle.—L. Lygo. ' Miss Lucy Lygo was the girls' champion, and Quirks Critchley the boys' champion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Another Question.</head>
        <p>Another Question.</p>
        <p>He was earnestly holding forth to a village audience: "I want land reform; I want housing reform; I want the farmers reformed." "You want chloroform badly," said a man in the crowfl</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 30, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 30, 1916.</p>
        <p>• ••• •• •• •• • •:.• ••••• • • •••:".• ••• • ••• •• • • • • • • • • ..:••••••• • t) •:•• ••• 4••• •• • • ••• • • •• • • ••••• ••• • • ••• •••• • • • • •• • • • •• • • •• • • • • •• • • • , .:. .:. . .:.</p>
        <p>and be indispensable for wearing over the one piece frock which is to reign supreme. The cost of one of these coats in one or other of the many imitations of short-haired pelts, is not an extravagance, be- cause afternoon frocks and all descriptions of thin dresses may be worn under them. The new coats and capes have such delightfully warm linings that one is led to infer that manu- facturers and mantle makers are anticipating a very severe winter. All the new models are very full, and the collars extravagantly high, if not cumbersome, but this will doubtless tone down as the winter comes on. Capes in fur, velvet, fur cloths and plush, will make a bid for favour also, they have been fashioned on the lines of the garment worn by the French peasant, are round in shape, very full, and have slits for the arms, and, of course, high fur collars.</p>
        <p>High Heels and Backache. Writing on the subject of backache in the September "Good Health," Dr. A. B. Olsen gives the following warning against high heels: "High-heeled boots are sufficient to cause an unnatural position and thus bring On backache. Nature intended us to walk on the iteels.as well as the balls of the feet and raising them more than an inch above the floor .means inviting trouble. There can be no doubt but that heels are an invention of man and that natural foot- wear would_ not be provided with heels. The sandals which were the first shoes of primitive man- did not raise the heels above the rest of the foot, and this is undoubtedly the natural position in 'standing and walking. But a small departure from this rule is not more 'than an inch at the. very outside, can: be adjusted by the ligaments and. muscles of the back without causing pain: Raising the heals still higher from the ground is almost certain to cause strain and bring suffering." " Eat Less Meat."</p>
        <p>We aro still urged by the Government to eat less meat and if people only realised the bene- fit in health, strength and vitality that woulu ensue by cutting down animal flesh to the mini- mum there would be no danger of a meat famine. There is no need of taking meat of any kind for •breakfast,for a small helping once a day provides an ample supply of the repair and building nourishment material which the chief ingredient of lean meat. When one uses fat in the form of butter or margarine little if any other fat is necessary. A breakfast eon- sisting of brown or standard bread and butter, oatmeal porridge and milk, stewed fruit such as prunes or currants or a few dates with or with- out an apple or a banana makes a wholesonw, nourishing, and well-balanced meal on which to start the work of the day. A few pine kernels or other nuts may be added if desired. Pine kernels make an excellent substitute for ham or bacon and are far superior in food value and wholesomeness.</p>
        <p>Rice and Herb Soup. Well wash loz. of whole unpolished rice, and put it into a pint of boiling and salted water; boil for thirty to forty minutes, and tfien add half a pint of skimmed 'milk, a little salt, some parsley chopped fine, and a saltspoonful each of powdered thyme and marjoram. Boil again for five minutes, and if thicker than desired ad.l more milk or water befOre serving. Preventives of Moths.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>"CARRY ON."</head>
        <p>"CARRY ON."</p>
        <p>Watchword Of The Musical Society. •</p>
        <p>A SEASON'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>"Carry on" is the watchword of the War- rington Musical Society, which held its annual meeting in the Cairo-street Schoolroom on Tues- day evening, following a practice by the chorus and orchestra.</p>
        <p>That it has justified its existence during the war may be judged from the facts that while it has fostered and maintained the art of music in .the borough, last year it was the means of</p>
        <p>handing over t 34 13s. Bd. to the Prisoners of War Fund. In all it has contributed nearly £lOO to war funds.</p>
        <p>Of cOurse, the Society has its difficulties— those of an adverse balance of £l6 13s. 9d., and a scarcity of male voices, but it is hoped that these will be improved. Mr. W. E. At-kin, a vice-president and past secretary of the Society, presided over the meet- ing. _</p>
        <p>'Submitting his report, the Hon. Secretary, Mr. Lionel Fairclough, referred with great regret to the death of Mh,Patrick Walsh, who had acted as librarian for several years. He went on to say that in spite of any drawbacks they could congratulate themselves not only on having kept the Society going, but on having given last season two 'concerts and a perform- ance of "The Messiah,", and handed a cheque to the Mayor for £34 13s. Bd. in aid '9f the Prisoners of War Fund.,,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Basses in the Army.</head>
        <p>Basses in the Army.</p>
        <p>" Naturally, we have lost a considerable pro- portion of our male members, especially amongst the basses, and this section of the chorus, for the first time I can remember, was numerically weakv than the tenors," he added. "The season previous we had 27 basses, last season 12 only. This entails very hard work on the remainder, so much so that it was felt that unless we could get some more assistance it would be impossible to carry on. Judging by the new faces we have seen to-night, I am happy to say that I believe this difficulty has been overcome. We worked last season on very similar lines to the season before, with the f,x- ception that, by the courtesy of the Mayor, we were able to give two concerts in the Parr Hall, as against one the season previous. The Rectpr again placed the Parish Church at our disposal. We gave a performance of "The Messiah," at which a collection realised £l4 10s. 11d., which I am sorry to say was considerably short of the collection at "The Elijah" per- formance-229 10s. 7d. However, as I mentioned before, we were able to hand over the sum of £34 13s. Bd. to the Mayor, being profits on the two concerts, and the collection in the Parish Church, in aid of the Local War Prisoners' Fund. The, strength of the chorus was as fol- lows :—Sopranos 42, previous season 53, per- centage of attendances 66.66; altos 48, ,previous season 56, percentage of attendances 68.75; tenors 17,4 previous season 23, percentage of at- tendances 58.23; basses 12, previous season 27, percentage of attendances 66.66. The strength of the orchestra was 32 against 35 the season before. By the courtesy Of Mr. Cahill, the bandmaster of the South Lancashire Regiment, we ,were assisted at practices and concerts _I3Y a double bass, a horn, a trombone and two cornets from his band, so that with their assistance Our bill for professional help at the concerts was very small. I understand they would not be available this season, as the band had been transferred. We owe them our best thanks for the help the gave, and there is no doubt that their instruments and assistance at the practices made them considerably more interesting." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>PRESENT AND THE FUTURE.</head>
        <p>THE PRESENT AND THE FUTURE.</p>
        <p>In submitting his statement, the Hon. Treasurer, Mr. J. P. Reynolds, said that on the whole the season had not been an unsatisfactory one, although the amount handed over to the Prisoners of War Fund was rather small and a little disappointing. It might have been better. During the two war seasons the Society had handed over just shot of £lOO to the war funds, which was not a bad record. On the two seasons there was a total adverse balance of £l6 13s. 9d., £9 of which was carried forward from last season. Really that deficit should have been deducted from the profits of the con- certs, but the committee felt that, as the amount which would then be available for the Prisoners of War Fund would be so small, it was not desirable to deduct anythinr ior expenses. That, however, was not a policy that could be continued indefinitely. Members' subscriptions —234 12s. 6d.—showed a falling off, due in great measure to the loss of male members, many of whom had gone to serve their country. The concert subscriptions (X3O 35.) were not unsatiS- factory in the circumstances, but they would have been better if members had shown a 'little more enthusiasm. There was a number of members who did splendidly, but.others did little or nothing in the sale of tickets. In addition, many of their subscribers kindly gave additional subscriptions to enable the Society to defray the working expenses, and thus avoid drawing on the profits of the concerts. The sum raised in this way was £29 4s. With regard to the future that was rather a difficult 'question, and one which the committee would have to con- sider very carefully. His own feeling was that they could do nothing but "carry on" in the same way as last season. It seemed absolutely hopeless to attempt to go back to their ordinary routine or try to build up their subscription list, but they could go on and do as last season. One concert had already been arranged foi November, but that was all that had been taken in hand up to the present. A suggestion which he regarded as a very good one was that they should give The Redemption" in the Parish Church during Lent. He had mentioned the proposal to Canon Willis, who was very favourable to it. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>NOT A BUSINESS CONCERN.</head>
        <p>NOT A BUSINESS CONCERN.</p>
        <p>Also urging the Society to "carry on," the Chairman said that: after all, what was a loss of £1.6 when they compared it with the amount actually handed over to a most deserving fund? Of course, they had to bear in mind the present high cost of living and the fact that there were so many appeals to the public. Even if the loss on the coming season's work happened to be larger still, he did not think that, in the special circumstances, it would be any reason for discouragement. The Society had such a long continuity that he 'believed it would ,be a fatal thing to wind it up, even for one season. He implored them to keep it.going. Of course, they could not fight against the impossible, and if the war lasted longer than was anticipatei, it might become necessary to suspend operations. At the present time, mowever, he saw no reason to contemplate such a course. They were not out to make money as a business concern, but even if they were it would be had policy to give up. it would be better to face the chance of a few trying years and be in a position to take advantage of the good times which would follow the declaration of peace. The Society would win through if they would only not get dis- pirited. The report and balance .lieet having been adopted, Mr. W. H. Payton proposed an altera- tion of the rule relating to the formation of the committee, which will now be ,composed of eleven members, including three ladies. The inclusion of ladies in the committee would be an innovation, he said, but, in view of the tre- mendous amount of work which the ladies had clone in war time, he thought it was abundantly justified. Furthermore, he believed their advice would be of great assistance to the com- mittee. (Hear, hear.) Mr. G. H. Smith seconded, and remarked that even in normal times the ladies constituted the bulk of the membership of the Society, but hitherto, although they had worked as actively, they had had no part in . the managenfent: Their claims to representation were intensified at the present time, when alms* the whole of the vocal membership consisted of ladies. The nuniber was limited to thr4 ladies so that the men could protect themselves and avoid their conferences being turned into sewing meetings. (Laughter.) The following male members were then elected: Messrs. J. Sephton, 4. W., Bibby, A. Fieldhouse, J. E. Hannan. -C4'. H. 'Smith, W. Hipkiss, J. B. Poole and J. Newall. Tie nominations of the ladies were postponed, the idea being that they should take time to con- sider the names. Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart.. was re-elected president, Mr. Lionel Fairclough hon. secretary, Mr. J. P. Reynolds hon. treasurer, Mr. J. Scot- son hon. registrar, Mr. "W Brown hon. auditor, and Mr. Herbert Geary was elected librarian.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Garrett-Anderson's Pioneer MAicz.,l Work.</p>
        <p>It is hoped that the health of- Mrs. Garrett- Anderson will permit of her being present when the Queen opens the London School of Medicine for Women early in October. just fifty years ago this autumn, aTter overcoming what seemed insuperable difficiilties, in winning her medical degree, Mrs. Anderson set up a modest dispensary fw the treatment of diseases of members of her own sex. The event was scarcely noted by the Press or public at the time, but this modest beginning developed in course of time into the New Hospital f3r Women. In 1889 the present tine building in Euston-road, London, was completed and opened by King Edward, then Prince of Wales. This hospital has sent forth such students as Dr. Selina Fox, the present governor 'of the Female Convict Prison at Aylesbury, and other lady doctors who are rendering valuable service to the nation as inspectors of reformatory and in- dustrial schools, among whem may be named Dr. H.t E. Harrison and Miss Ada Whitlock, M.B. The magnificent building now being raised in India's new capital. Delhi, as a Women's Medical Collegr, is modelled on the plan of the London School. There are at pre- sent about eleven hundred women on the medi- cal register.</p>
        <p>Forestry for Womon. Forestry offers a profitable and interesting career to women. Hitherto this has been re- garded as men's work, but as nearly all the male students have been- taken for the Army, there is a lack of trained experts. Continental countries have for a long time employed women\ as workers in nurseries for trees, but the idea is a new one in Britain. University College,' North Wales, has an experimental farm for seed beds and nurseries, and this is under the superintendence of a lady, the first to gain the B.Sc. in forestry. The whole course is now thrown open to women, and it is hoped that a greater number will avail 'themselves this winter of the opportunity to qualify as graduates in this department of agriculture. The women employed on the University College farm live in a hostel and 'share the honsekeep- ing. The working hours are from seven in the morning until five, with an hour and a half for meals. The work requires a cerfain amount of physical strength, but it is healthy and very fascinating. Simple Names for High-born Babies. ...</p>
        <p>There are fashions in feminine names, as in dresses, and every generation Or two definite changes set in, so that it orten possible to tell from her name, in which decade of the 19th century a woman was born. Bible names were very popular in the early Victorian days, but these gave way to the more sentimental Evan- geline, Marion, Agnes, &amp;c. and the names chosen for the daughters of Queen Victoria— nearly all of which were foreign. Tennyson's liroines were very popular for a long time, and the Crimean War was responsible for a great number of Almas, and other Russian names, some rather barbaric in sound. Jewel names had a vogue nok So very long ago, favourites being Pearl, Ruby and Beryl. The Diamond Jubilee provided a name for many girls born in the year of its' celebration, and then change being , a necessity, flower names came in and Violet-3 Roses, Lilies, Pansies and Mays bloomed. Rose- mary, which in French is so musical, has recently returned to favour, with Ruth, Joan, Peggy, Betty and Anne, in fact any simple old- fashioned name is chosen just now by parents moving in the highest social circles for their baby daughters. Warmly lined Coats and Capes.</p>
        <p>The coat and,skirt costume found a formid- able rival in tlfe'coat-frock of last spring, this winter it will have two others to dispute its sway, in the overcoat and cape. The long coat in fur, or in blanket cloth, has been worn for some years as wraps, but this autumn it will be seen in a variety of form's, and in all lengths,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>4. .:. .:. +4.4.4.4.4. 4. 4. .:. 4. • .:. 4. .:..:. .:. 4. .:. 4, ..... * • . A</head>
        <p>4. .:. .:. +4.4.4.4.4. 4. 4. .:. 4. • .:. 4. .:..:. .:. 4. .:. 4, ..... * • . A</p>
        <p>•1: Chlidred Corner • •</p>
        <p>0 Conducted by UNCLE JACK. 0</p>
        <p>+44 4:4.4) 4.4. t•</p>
        <p>COMFORTS FOR SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>Mr DEAR. CHILDREN,</p>
        <p>The soldiers would be very well looked after if you had your way. Doubtless you will help your parents to supply as many comforts as 'is possible. This, I think, will be the fast winter for them in the trenches,i and we must all do the best we can to make it the most successful from our point of view. As you know, the better the men are looked after, the better they will be able to fight our battles. So let us keep on sewing 4nd knitting while we keep the home fires burning! The prize this week goes to 524 Niece Eva M. Morraii,</p>
        <p>47, Price-street, who joined the Corner a long</p>
        <p>_ while ago, when it was only half as large as it is now. This is the third time she has won a prize, so that she will be a' strong candidate for promotion when Uncle Ned returns from the wars and begins his scheme for awarding honours to his successful nephews and nieces. This week we will have for competition an essay entitled My Opinion of the Victory of the Somme. I know all the facts: I want to know what you think about them.</p>
        <p>Your loving - UNtLE JACK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>WINNING ESSAY.</head>
        <p>THE WINNING ESSAY.</p>
        <p>Niece .Eva M. Morrell, 47, Price-street.— "Although we hold the upper hand now on all fronts and the final victory is fast approaching, there is every likelihood of a severe winter campaign. For this we must be prepared. Our valiant soldiers must be supplied with warm clothing and other comforts. Mittens, scarves, woollen helmets and warm socks are being knitted by the girls and women who, knowing they cannot fight, do their duty in this way. I am glad to say I' am doing my share. Then the soldiers need those luxuries, yet neces- sities, such as cigarettes, Oxo and those( ventions of the war—if I may call them so— compressed tea, coffee, cocoa, milk tablets, also patent pocket stoves. Our armies are fighting for us; let us do something for them."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</head>
        <p>HIGHLY COMMENDED.</p>
        <p>N i ec.e Doris Whittaker, 62, Baxter-street (Evelyn-street School).—" I am glad to say that all the soldiers and sailors are getting comforts by children knitting socks and het-, mets. It is coming\winter and the soldiers will want strong boots and socks because of tramp- ing in the snow and walking in .the trenches when it has been raining hard. If I had my way I would send all the soldiers a pair of socks. Many comforts are needed by those who are in hospital, sick or wounded." . Niece Emily Marshall, 183, Winwick-road (Silver-street School).—"We are now in the midst of a great war and We will have to fight our way through it. Soldiers are fighting for us, and now as the winter comes on we ought to give a bit of money or buy wool and knit for them. When we send them things wp are paid back; it is a good job gor us that we were in England instead of Belgiu`zn. They fight and give their lives for us, so we should pay, them back in other ways. Some comforts which they enjoy are cigarettes, tobacco, mittens, choco- late, scarves and gloves. We should all knit or send them parcels." Nephew Frank Edwards, 8, Monks-street (Heathside School), says that a small spirit lamp easily taken to pieces so as to be carried in a</p>
        <p>There are many of these, and thq following have proved a success in our home and are inexpensive. Newspapers, tar. paper, and kero- sene are good. If these are properly used, no trace of the evil works of the moths will b 3 found. Boxes are best to store the things away in. Spray the corners and bottom with kero- sene, then fill over the bottom and sides with plenty of old newspapers- the garments and other articles. Cover with a thick layer of news- papers, tucking down the corners and' side. Spray the cover of the box with kerosene. Moths will not lay eggs in a protected place like this. In the fall, if the materials are hung out in the open-air and sunshine, all odour will evaporate. I know of a friend who •hangs suits, coats, furs, &amp;c., in the open attic in bags, with just a small piece of cotton odourised with kerosene in •one of the pockets, and she says nothing is ever touched by moths.—" Indiana Farmer."</p>
        <p>soldier's pocket is a great 'boon to men at the Front.</p>
        <p>Nephew George Emery, 69, Lythgoes-lane.— " Winter is drawing near, and every possible effort is needed to provide comforts for our Tommies at the Front. The chief •of these comforts is the sheep's fur which is made into a kind of jacket; the next are the mits and socks which many people will be busy making during the winter. Another is the woollen helmets, which help to keep the frost from their faces. Many igarmers will be very busy about this time of the year selling the furs to the Army." Niece Amy Campbell, 56, Lancaster-street (Evelyn-street School).—" Winter, as you say, is approaching, and the comforts for our soldiers at the Front must be our first thought. What I consider to, be most essential are good warm shirts and socks. Then come mufflers, mittens, and helmets. After these come the neat little packages of Oxo cubes, tea and sugar tablets, tinned milk, with the various other commodities with which 'Tommy' is able to make himself a good hot drink with the addition of a little boiling water. Last, but not least, come the ever-welcome soap, cigarettes and matches."</p>
        <p>Niece Jane Gough, 5, Phillip-street.—" Now that the cold weather has come our soldiers and sailors need comforts, ,and some need comforting. One way of comforting lonely soldiers that have no friends to write to them is to write kind, friendly letters to them. We can find lonely soldiers by writing to our own friends, asking them to send up the name and address of any soldier whom they know gets no letters. Another way is to knit socks and mittens and to send parcels to them, and make them warm and happy. While the war is on we must be more self-denying and do without the luxuries which we had before the war. They must be given to the soldiers and sailors who are making such sacrifices for us and our country."</p>
        <p>Niece Lily Healey, 85, Hoyle-street.--" I think that helnbets, socks, scarves and mitts are most useful to our soldiers while they are at the Front; especially mitts, which are much more useful to them than gloves, because they need not take them off when firing. It is very hard to say which they need most as it is so very cold and wet out in France that they need all the comforts they can get to keep them warm."</p>
        <p>Niece Elsie Johnson, 323, Lovely-lane (Viry- cliffe).—" Why, the greatest comfort for our soldiers would be for them to win the war! Other comforts, which differ much from these, are those made at home by their loved ones and by different societies at home and abroad. Cuffs and woollen helmets are worn by our soldiers in winter to protect them from being frost-bitten."</p>
        <p>Niece Maud Alice Lunny, 108 a, Bridge-street .Arpley-street School).—" Soldiers at the Front need socks very much to keep their feet in good condition for marching; also shirts, mit- tens and warm things for the winter. Now the weather is coming colder, warm clothes are mostly needed. Soldiers in the trenches need more comfortable things because they are ex- posed to all kinds of weather, having to stay there nearly all 06 time. The ladies are well thanked for their presents, and many a nice letter from the soldiers has been sent."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>ACKNOWLEDGMENT.</head>
        <p>ACKNOWLEDGMENT.</p>
        <p>Niece Emily Marshall, 183, Winwick-road, last week's prize winner, writes:— " Dear Uncle Jack,—l thank you very much for the book which I received at noon on Satur.. day. It will be very interesting for me as I 'read it through. I hope to win again another •time.—Your loving niece, EMILY MARSHALL."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>NEW MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>1009- Annie Lennon, 81, Dallam-lane; age 11; St. Alhan's School.</p>
        <p>1010 James Darbyshire, 35, Tilley-street; age 12; Hamilton-street School. 1011. Doris Pucill, 19, Albert-street. 1012 Jane Gough, 5, Phillip-street; age 12. 1013 Sarah Bate, 7, Bowes-strect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN..</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN..</p>
        <p>COUNTY FARMERS' HELP FOR ALLIED FARMERS.</p>
        <p>Lancashire and Cheshire farmers, who have raised over £13,000 for the Agricultural Relief of Allies Fund, have brought the fund to ,its first £lOO,OOO as the result of the gift sale at Chester opened by Katharine Duchess of West- minster. The £3,000 which was realised by the sale lifts the fund to £100,674, but the com- mittee aim. at raising much more in order to make an adequate response to the cry for help which will be made from the agricultural dis- tricts of Serbia, Belgium and Poland when the enemy is driven back. Of the amount so far raised over £60,000 has been secured by means of farmers' gift sales, and four such sales alone held in Cheshire—at Chester, Knutsford, Altrincham,‘ and Macclesfield—have produced £9,422 for this fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>CABBAGES FOR SPRING.</head>
        <p>CABBAGES FOR SPRING.</p>
        <p>Now is the time to plant out the main lot of cabbages for spring use. The young, plants from seed sown in July will now be in fine condition for setting out, more especially after the copious rains we have had. A few rows of the strongest plants should be set out on a warm, sheltered border to come in in March. For the main crop to come in in May there is no need to dig the ground. That from which the onion crop has been cleared answers well for the spring cabbages. Such ground having been well manured and deeply dug for the onions all that is necessary is to hoe it over and clear off the weeds. The firmness of the onion bed will check gross growth, which, should the winter be severe, is sure to suffer. If it is thought that the cabbages will benefit by feeding them in spring, then some manure placed between the lines will do good. If rotten manure, as is likely to be the case in these days of motors, is unobtainable, then recourse must be had to some artificial manure, such as sulphate of ammonia or nitrate of soda. The newer intro- ductions in cabbages, such as Harbinger, April, and Flower of Spring, are so dwarf and compact that they may be planted very closely, the plants as the hearts become fit for use being pulled up bodily, thus making room for those that are left to increase in size.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>MILK AS IT CAME FROM THE COW.</head>
        <p>MILK AS IT CAME FROM THE COW.</p>
        <p>The country papers frequently contain re- ports of Milk prosecutions which fail owing to the defendant pleading the High Court ruling in Hunt v. Richardson. A majority of judges held that milk, in order to be genuine milk, need not be up to a standard if it could be proved to have been sold just as it came from the now. Attempts have been made, and will be made, to override, or to evade, the ruling in Hunt v. Richardson. In a recent case the prosecution contended that "the supply to such an institution as a hospital involved a higher obligation than the mere sale to a per- son in the street." The defendant, a reputable farmer, had been supplying milk to a muni- cipal hospital for a number of years on a written contract that the milk should contain not less than 3.5 per cent. of fat, that being 1.5 per cent. above the Board of Agriculture I standard. After' the expiration of the last written contract he was allowed to continue to supply milk to the hospital. That,, it was con tended by the prosecution, assumed that the future supply would be in the terms of the written. contract.- No proof was offered that the milk had been tampered with in any way, or that it was otherwise than as it came from the cow. The suggestion that the defendant was bound by the terms of a contract that had in fact ceased to exist, does not appear to have been taken seriously, and in the result the case was dismissed. Presumably, though the reports do not say so explicitly, the ruling in Hunt v. Richardson prevailed with the Bench.—" Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>RAMBLER ROSES.</p>
        <p>In most gardens nowadays the rambler roses find a place, and in order that plenty of flowers may be forthcoming next year attention must now be given. to the thinning-out of the old and exhausted growths. As is well known, the rambler roses flower best on the., ripened woon made the previous year, so in order to admit light and air to ripen up the shoots, all the rods that have bloomed should be removed as soon. as the flowering is over. In the case of plants that are only two or three years old some of the old canes may be left if not too exhausted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>CODLIN MOTH.</head>
        <p>THE CODLIN MOTH.</p>
        <p>Now is the time to take steps to ,cheek the inroads of the Codlin moth. Put strips of brown paper round the stems of the trees at about one foot from the ground, and smea.</p>
      </div>
