      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 30, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>" MAJESTY " OF THE LAW-AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE " MAJESTY " OF THE LAW-AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>We are supposed to be living in Spartan- like days, in which the mamby-pamby cus- toms of a soft-hearted, and perhaps softer- headed, time have, for a season, been dis- carded. Men are, giving their lives for their country as light-heartedly as they formerly ran the milder risks Of the field of sport; women are suffering in silence for the loss of that which can never be replaced. We are getting. "harder," approach more nearly the actualities of life, and are banishing a flabby sentimentalism that had outlived its possi- bilities for good. We should expect to see our magistrates prepared to fall into line and be prepared, in the circumstances, to mete out justice under the guidance of reason rather than under an impulse .to lean to the side of "mercy " where mercy is entirely mis- placed. We were always under the impres- sion that mercy and kindly consideration were misplaced, and even misnamed, when they were invoked to save the skin of a ruffian who has assaulted and perhaps injured for life a policeman whose only offence has been that he has been doing his duty by the State by endeavouring to see that its laws were not broken. Mercy to such miscreants means the withdrawal of mercy from the representatives of law and order. When the innocent suffer and the wrongdoer is treated leniently mercy is a misfit, and becomes neither him that gii,e3 nor him that takts. The offender should be prepared to take his punishment and our magistrates should be prepared to administer it. If this is so in normal times the argument applies with manifold strength now. The Warrington Police Force has been reduced, in round figures, from 90 members to 45, the balance being away fighting in the cause of national safety and international right. To make up the deficiency about 150 volunteers have come forward to help to police the town. It is-essential that this remnant of the regular force and their voluntary helpers should have ithe power of the State behind them, wholly and unequivocally, or a most undesirable state of affairs at once prevails. Fortunately the town is, on the whole, peaceably inclined; it is, in fact, behaving its'elf most creditably. But there are some few, in some cases strangers, who are indisposed to comply with the rules that go to make up respectable and law-abiding citizenship. One of these excep- tions came to the surface on Saturday night. A half-drunken man was detected by Special Inspector MASON in Barbauld-street shouting to women and behaving in an indecent man- ner. The police have special instructions to cleanse the streets of this villainous Class of ' offender, and Inspector MASON 'proceeded to arrest his man. He received a right-hander from the shoulder' on the side of the jaw and a terrific up-and-down struggle ensued, in which another and similar blow was delivered.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 30, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 30, 1916.</p>
        <p>With the assistance of three or four other constables the prisoner was conveyed to the Police Station. One would have thought that here was a case in which an example could have been made; but on Monday what appears to have been an unseemly wrangle took place among the eight magistrates who heard the case. Presumably some wanted to commit to gaol, others to inflict a small fine. They were evenly divided, and the MAYOR, very wisely refusing to let u ruffian escape, ad- journed the case so that it might be heard by a fresh Bench on Tuesday. The prisoner, on being re-charged on Tuesday, was fined 30s. or a month's hard labour.</p>
        <p>As the prisoner was a poor man he went to gaol, which, in the result, was satisfactory. But we hold that these assaults upon the police should, unless there are strong reasons to the contrary, be followed by gaol without the option .of a fine. Had the prisoner in this case been a little better off, he would, without feeling the loss, have 'paid the fine; therefore, be has gone to gaol not becau-se he seriously maltreated a public official; but I.)te_.ause be was Door, which is absurd. We hope , the magistrates will, in future, see the wisdom, to mention the dignity, of pro- viding that those who are maintaining the law are duly respected when • they bring cases before them. • Otherwise, magistrates will merely be parties to bringing the law into contempt. Tears for prisoners are all very well, hut practical sympathy for those who are endeavouring to prevent and to punish wrong- doing is much better.</p>
        <p>A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>N12.31-IT3OM MEN'S STARTLING</head>
        <p>N12.31-IT3OM MEN'S STARTLING</p>
        <p>ULTIMATUM.</p>
        <p>The town has been threatened this week-end with a strike that would have endangered the public health and rendered us liable to the worst effects of accumulated refuse. The Sanitary Works Committee that met on Tues- day were confronted by a " request " from the ash men and nightsoil men and drivers for an increase of no less than 10s. a week in their wages. It was intimated that the men had' given notice last Saturday to leave their jobs if the request were not complied with by to-day. So that unless the committee were prepared to capitulate we were to have a strike, and the filth of the borough was to be left in the thousands of unsavoury receptacles that have been crowded in all our back premises. The system is wrong and must be put right; but that is another, question. What we are con- cerned about is that any body of working men should propose to injure a community by making bad worse if they .cannot have their exorbitant demands conceded without ques- tion or delay. We are glad to say that wiser counsels have prevailed, and that the notices have been withdrawn, the men, having agreed to the committee's suggestion that the " dis- pute" should be submitted to the arbitration of the Board of Trade. It may be pointed out that at present the drivers receive a wage of 31s. a week and a bonus of 25., and the car- riers a wage of 28s. and 3s; bonus, and the General Purposes Committee have just de- cided to give an additional war bonus of 2s. to all married male employes, which will take over £3,000 a year to pay, or an additional 3d. in the £ on the rates. Thus it will be seen that the Corporation has acted not un- generously by its employes. We do not think that the men are to be blamed for try- ing to do the best they can, for themselves in these days of expensive living. It is the peremptory method and reckless manner in which they have proceeded to which we object and which We feel sure the whole town will resent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>" Maggie " is still unresponsive.</p>
        <p>Captain Whittle being a prisoner of war at Offizier Kreigsgefangenenlager, Gutersloh, the Town Clerk has permanently solved intricacies of the address and the laboriousness of writing it by having envelopes printed !- I have specially entrusted the setting of it to a Welsh Linotype operator.</p>
        <p>Since Thursday most of the local works have been closed and the workmen have been at rest. I, too, am having a "breather." No smoke is coming from chimney steaks.</p>
        <p>The holiday is generally confined' to the works' employes, the staffs labouring on. :-But was it not the holiday spirit which induced two of the lady typists at Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., to change their minds on Thursday as they were on their way to a harvest festival and go to see " Moths " at the Royal Court Theatre? One of them was observed sitting with a. Prayer Book in her hand. Her companion held the programme.</p>
        <p>Gentlemen having always had the monopoly of the management of the Musical Society, three, ladies in a committee of eleven, members are now to be admitted to serve and, no doubt, they will be extremely useful.</p>
        <p>But will they be shy? At the annual meeting on Tuesday evening, a seconder to a proposition was required. The Chairman looked round appealingly, and one of the ladies modestly gave her acquiescence with a nod and a smile, although the occasion merited a speech. Per- haps she could have sung it better.</p>
        <p>It appears that some of the lady members have failed to settle up for concert tickets rt.- ceived last year, and one or tivo, even for the year before. Sheer ,forgetfulness, no doubt. Also the artistic temperament!</p>
        <p>It took two sittings of the magistrates this week to come to a decision to punish, in the mildest possible way, a prisoner* who had molested two women, acted indecently, and savagely assaulted a special police inspector.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable made a " special " appeal. With a stinging kind of irony which the second Bench did not apparently notice, h.) asked for the protection of a body of men who had come forward to make good the wastages of war hi the police force. "If it had been one of my own regular force," he said, "I would not have minded so much."</p>
        <p>Of course, he did not mean that. He meant that past experience of the attitude of some magistrates had taught him to know that it is little use asking the Bench for their protection. So he, as a matter of rhetorical strategy, threw his "own men if over and bent his energies upon appealing for those who are giving their services to the town. _.</p>
        <p>The appeal was but partially successful. To steal " property " of any kind is the nearest way to gaol known to the law. A policeman's jaw does not counts or counts for little.</p>
        <p>I see that only ; some of the gas lamps are to have painted 'white bands. So it will be your own fault if you select the posts without bands to run against.</p>
        <p>Special Constables. wearing badge and armlet have been given the freedom'of the cars—when on duty.</p>
        <p>"There was an attendance of 10,000 on the Warrington ground, where Runcorn were badly beaten," says the "Daily News."</p>
        <p>I am a firm believer in the veracity of the newspaper in question, but on the present occasion it would appear that Runcorn were not beaten half so badly by Warrington as was Truth by the "Daily News." The " gate " at Wilderspool was 2,000, which, .for war time, was remarkably good.</p>
        <p>VIGILANT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>PROMOTIONS AND DISTINCTIONS.</head>
        <p>PROMOTIONS AND DISTINCTIONS.</p>
        <p>-WARRINGTON TEACHER</p>
        <p>Promoted To Commissioned Rank For Services In The Field.</p>
        <p>News has been received in Warrington that Sub-Conductor David Morgan James, Army Ordnance Corps, has been promoted to a Second- Lieutenancy in that branch of' the Army for services in the field. Lieutenant James, who was formerly an assistant teacher at the Bea- mont Council School, is the second of the regu- lar staff to gain a commission in the field. A Welshman by birth, and unmarried, he is about thirty years of'age, and enlisted at the outbreak of the war. He has been in Egyrk for the past seventeen months, and has gone through every grade from Private to Sub-Conductor, this latter rank being equivalent to that of Regi- mental-Sergeant-Major. His further advance- ment to commissioned rank will be heard of with much lEatisfaction, testifying, as it does, to his many excellent qualities. ' As already stated, Lieutenant James is a native of the Principality, his father being an extensive farmer iti South Wales. He was trained at Bangor Normal College, and was one</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>.1 D.L.U.'s PROMOTION.</head>
        <p>.1 D.L.U.'s PROMOTION.</p>
        <p>I'Vell-known Teacher Becomes Second- Lieutenant.</p>
        <p>The many friends of Staff-Sergeant-Major J. Whitehouse, D.C.M., a well-known local teacher, will be pleased to hear that he has been granted a Second-Lieutenancy in the Second-Lieutenant Whitehouse, whose home address is 27, Fairfield-road, is the son of Mr. and Mrs. Edward Whitehouse, is married, and has one child.</p>
        <p>He has been mentioned twice in dispatches, and his •name appeared ,amongst the list of awards on the King's birthday in June, and he was awarded_ the D.C.M. "for continuous devo- tion to duty throughout. He has on many occasions carried on the heavy work of the administrative branch office single-handed, with conspicuous ability." Enlisting in the A.S.C. on January 24th, 1915, he was with the first landing at Gallipoli, where he Served throughout. the campaign. After re- cuperating for a time at Suez, his division w sent to ,France.</p>
        <p>Staii-§c,.rgeant-Major 'Whitehouse, wh(.. before</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>•LIECTENANT D. M. JAMES</head>
        <p>•LIECTENANT D. M. JAMES</p>
        <p>of the original staff appointed on the opening of thb Beamont Council School some ten years ago. While teaching at Beamont Mr. James was a great favourite with the boys, and when his promotion in the Army was announced in the school on Monday by the head master, Mr. W. Mason, there was a spontaneous outburst of cheering. He is a man of many gifts, being a cultured musician—bass vocalist—and a mem- ber of musical societies in Warrington. Tie is also a fine specimen of physival manhood, being an expert boxer and a rattling footballer and /cricketer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Kit AND BATTLE PROMOTION.</head>
        <p>Kit AND BATTLE PROMOTION.</p>
        <p>AValton Officer's Recotinition For Good Services.</p>
        <p>In the long list of heroes singled out for dis- tinction in connection with the great Jutland Naval Battle the name of Arthur Lee Picton occupies, a prominent position. When lli</p>
        <p>enlisting was engag6d as a teacher at the Bolton Council School, Latchford, was educated at Arpley-street School, and thi3 Boteler Gram- mar School. He began his teaching career at Arpley-street School, and afterwards proceeded to Borough-road College, London. lie has beeti teaching at the Bolton Council School for about five years.</p>
        <p>CORPORAL B. SHAM'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>CAPTAIN ARTHUR LEE PICTON.</head>
        <p>CAPTAIN ARTHUR LEE PICTON.</p>
        <p>Majesty's Destroyer Broke went into action on the occasion of that glorious victory over the German Fleet, this gallant officer was saluted as Engineer Lieutenant Commander Picton When :H.M.S. Broke—the last destroyer lA, reach port after the battle—reached the appointed haven three days after the conflict terribly k&amp;cked about and having a sad list of casualties to report—his name had been noted for special mention. When the expected Admiralty announcement was published it was found that, recommended for good service in action, Engineer Lieutenant Commander Picton had been promoted Engineer Commander, with the title of Captain. The promotion was exceed- ingly popular and well earned, and we under- stand that it was one of two cases in which officers of identical rank received the same recognition. • Referring to the vessel, the Admiralty report stated: "H.M.S. Broke was severely damaged and casualties were very great, but the morale appears to have been unshaken and the ship was successfully steamed back to port." Engineer Commander Picton is 38 years of age, and the second son of Mr. and Mrs. J. Picton, " Westleigh," Lower Walton. He was born in Warrington and was educated in the Grammar School. Choosing naval engineering as his future profession, he entered Keyham -College, Devonport, at the age of fourteen, as an engineer student. When war broke out he was at Wei-hai-wei, on the China station, and was called to more active and dangerous waters ! near home, being appointed to H.M.S. Broke. ' He was granted three weeks' leave of absence "‘after the Jutland Battle, and is now at sea again ! with the fleet. Commander Picton 's , elder brother is Major, J. T. Picton, A.S.C., who is in France at the present time. The Commander I married Miss Grime, of Helsby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>RUSSIAN DECORATION FOR, SOUTH LANCASHIRE SERGT.-MAJOR.</head>
        <p>RUSSIAN DECORATION FOR, SOUTH LANCASHIRE SERGT.-MAJOR.</p>
        <p>Already the holder of the Military Cross, Regimental-Sergeant-Major Talbot ' V. W. Roberts, of the South Lancs. Regiment, has been awarded the Russian decoration, the Medal if St. George (Ist Class). His brother, Mr. Aubrey Roberts; resides at 10, Amelia-street. Sergt,-Major Roberts recently arrived in War- rington for the purpose of spending a short leave with his relatives, but his nealth having broken, he is now a patient in the Lord Derby War Hospital. Sergeant-Major Roberts, who is ,a son of the late Quartermaster-Sergeant J. W. Roberts, of the South Lancs. Regiment, who was for some years stationed at Orford Barracks, has been at the Front almost from the beginning. He was in the retreat from Mons, and for fine work during these trying times he was mentioned in Viscount French's first dispatch. He was awarded the Military Cross, and received it at the hands of the King at Buckingham Palace in May. When quite young, Sergeant-Major Roberts joined the South Lancs. Regiment as a drummer, after having served as a Sergeant- Major in the St. Elphin's Company •9f the Church Lads' Brigade:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>.ii‘lardtd Alililar,s Medal For Distinguished Service.</head>
        <p>.ii‘lardtd Alililar,s Medal For Distin- guished Service.</p>
        <p>Corporal Bert Shaw, of the R.A.M.C., son of Mr. and Mrs. T. Shaw, " Sunnyside," Chester New-road, has been warded the Military Medal for services rendered at the Front</p>
        <p>Corporal Shaw, who, as a boy, attended the People's College and Secondary Schools, was formerly employed in the office at the White- cross Works. He joined' the R.A.M.C. in August, 1914, and has been in France fifteen</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>LIEUTENANT QUICK.</head>
        <p>LIEUTENANT QUICK.</p>
        <p>entire absence of wastefulness having made the depot notable in military circles. Lieutenant Quick has had an interesting Army career. After serving six years ill the Cheshire Regiment he joined the Salford Police ,Fbrce. lle stayed with the police only twelve months, and then rejoined his regiment as Sergeant. Six and a half years later he was promoted Regimental-Sergeant-Major, •first- class warrant officer. Later he became Garrison Sergeant-Major at Malta, and retired in 1900</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>RECORD MAYORALTY.</head>
        <p>RECORD MAYORALTY.</p>
        <p>Aid. Peter Peacock Honoured</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Four Times Running.</head>
        <p>Four Times Running.</p>
        <p>A POPULAR CHOICE.</p>
        <p>The unanimity of the General Purposes Com- mittee, which consists of the whole of the mem- bers of the Town Council, in inviting Alderman Peter Peacock to become Mayor for a fourth year, is a happy reflection of Public feeling, and we have great pleasure in recording the fact and extending to him our congratulations. By his acceptance a record has been achieved, for, although it is possibre to recollect gentle- men having been the chief magistrate of the borough for four years, Alderman Peacock is the first one to hold it successively.</p>
        <p>When the General Purposes Committee met on Mond/ty afternoon, the invitation was soon re- solved upon. Alderman Bennett presided in the unavoidable absence of - the Deputy-Mayor (Councillor Joseph). The proceedings were private, but, like Lobbyists and the Govern- ment, we have been able to gather some inter- esting facts. The proposition that Alderman Peacock should again be invited to fill the- Mayoral chair was proposed by Cauncillor Broadhurst and seconded by Alderman Tinnion—two gentle- men of: different political persuasions—and little was needed to be said in order that the motion should receive unaninpus support. This having been signified, the chairman, proposer and seconder waited upon Alderman Peacock, and asked him whether he would. fall in with their wishes. He agreed, and then entered the Coun- cil Chamber and expressed his thanks for the confidence they had shown in him. In again accepting the position he said he recognised the great honour which the Council had done him in selecting him for four years in succession. He did not under-estimate the heavy responsi- bilities' of the office, especially in such trying days as those through which we Were now pass- ing, but he could assure them that it would be his endeavour, as in the past, to serve all inter- ests and parties impartially; and to devote as much time as possible to the duties. Alderman Peacock was- present at a sale, of work at Brick-street Mission on Thursday, and, in reply to congratulations, made some inter- esting observations recorded in our report of the sale.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>HARMONY IN THE</head>
        <p>HARMONY IN THE</p>
        <p>CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Free Churches' Goodwill</p>
        <p>Towards The Anglican Mission.</p>
        <p>The Rural Deanery of Winwick are taking; their share in the National _Mission of Repent- ance and Hope next week, and as a token (if goodwill the Free Church Council have addressed the following letter to the Rural Dean (the Rev. Canon Willis) : • Bewsey Manse, Warrington, 27th September, 1916.</p>
        <p>Dear Canon Willis, As your Mission of Repentance and Hope begins next week, we should like to assure you and your colleagues and fellow Churchmen in Warrington of the hearty sympathy of the Free Churches. There is • one Lord and one Spirit and one God and Father of all, and we feel that, at this time especially, we should embhasise the great truths that are common to us and think less of the fewer and smaller things in which we differ. We agree with you that everything in our political life and our social and industrial order must be brought into harmony with the mind of Christ, and that moral reformation must spring from spiritual revival.</p>
        <p>We are requesting that prayer be offered in all our churches on Sunday next for the suc- cess of your effort, and that the grace of the Holy Spirit may be richly poured out upon you all, and through you upon our beloved land.</p>
        <p>Conscious that no Church liveth to itself and confident that your intercession and work will not be in vain. We beg to remain, Yours faithfully. W. T. DERRY. President, Free Church Council, HERBERT OGDEN, Secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Canon Willis Reciprocates.</head>
        <p>Canon Willis Reciprocates.</p>
        <p>To this Canon Willis has replied :--" Dear Mr. Derry and Mr. Ogden,—Please accept and con- vey to your council my very best thanks for your most kind and sympathetic letter. lam sure it. will give great pleasure to our clergy and workers, to whom I will read , it at our next meeting. I cannot tell you how gladly I personally value and appreciate the friendly recognition I receive from all your ministers, and I am sure that all the clergy and laity heartily thank God for your sympathy and prayers.</p>
        <p>"With kindest regards, "I am, . "Very truly yours, " FREDERIC W. WILLIS."</p>
        <p>In, conversation with our representative on Thursday evening, Canon Willis frankly ex- pressed his pleasure at having received the letter from the Free Church Council, and agreed that it was a new departure significant of much good. "We are on very cordial relations here in Warrington," he added. "Some time. ago I wrote to the Nonconformist ministers and the Roman Catholic clergy to say that we in- tended taking part in the National Mission dur- ing the last week in. October, and I asked 'them if they could see their way to simultaneous action.. I did not ask them to co-operate, be- cause I felt that there were difficulties in the way. I got some very nice replies, but the Roman Catholic clergy mentioned—that they were unable to fall in with the suggestion be- cause they always held their missions in Lent, and the Nonconformist minister./ replied that, at any rate, we should have their sympathy and prayers. That, I can assure, is a great deal, and we esteem their action highly."</p>
        <p>Thanks are now being given for the harvest; thanks also are given for the seed of good- will which has just been sown.</p>
        <p>"Great Confessions of Faith."</p>
        <p>On Sunday mornings during October the Rev. Irvine Lister will deliver, at Cairo-street Chapel, a series of addresses on "Great Confes- sions of Faith." To-morrow's subject will be "The Confessions of St. Augustine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Corporal-Chairman.</head>
        <p>A Corporal-Chairman.</p>
        <p>There was a good meeting on Sunday at the WYcliffe P.S.A., presided over by Corporal W. Carter, who was over on furlough. He gave the meeting a short history of his experiences "Somewhere in France." He has been in many ,:ngagements. He is conscious cif the great in- terest the P.S.A. always takes in its soldier- members. No one, he said, could say When the war would be over, but certainly the Germans were in the grip of the Allies. Rev. T. W. Bond, of St. Helens, gave the nddress, and followed up the remarks of the Chairman by saying it was the duty of those at home to do their bit by making any sacri- fice: they must work well, live well, and save well, and so help the country and our brave defenders to bring the war to an end. The Secretary (Mr. B. 'Farrar), welcomed the Chair- man, Sergeant A. Spencer,- who had been wounded and Was on furlough, and Private Buckley, on leave. A hearty greeting was given to Mr. Robertson, of the Soho Hill Men's Brotherhood, who was present from Birming- ham. Mr. Yearsley And Mr. Healey also took part in the meeting. The Secretary said he hoped to have the prize books ready next Sun- day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>PROMOTIONS &amp; DISTINCT! ONS.—Continued</head>
        <p>PROMOTIONS &amp; DISTINCT! ONS.—Continued</p>
        <p>with that rank. He has been a first-class war- rant officer for 24 years. At the outbreak of war Lieutenant Quick was emploked as inspector and outdoor manager by Messrs. George Shaw and Co., Ltd., Leigh Brewery, but immediately offered for active service, and was appointed to the depot of the South Lancashire Regiment, Warrington, with the rank of Regimental-Sergeant-Major. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Qiiiek, who is a brother of Mr. Thomas Quick, the noted race course official and inspector to the Jockey Club, is 54 years of age, and is probably the senior warrant officer in the British Army now serving. His nephew, Rifleman Alfred Quick, Rill) Brigade, won the D.C.M. at the Battle of Loos, but was after- wards,/unfortunately, killed. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>foratYnittligturt</head>
        <p>foratYnittligturt</p>
        <p>Lights for Coming Week. Light up Obscure Vehicles. Lights. Saturday (to-day) End of Summer Time. Sunday, Oct. 1 ... 6.23 7.23 . Monday Tuesday ,Wednesday Thursday Friday' Saturday A.R.C. Time Table.</p>
        <p>The Warrington "A B C "-fay Guige for October will be on sale on Monday. Many trains belonging to the summer service have been stopped, so. that the corrections this month /are of importance to the travelling public.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>First-Aid Classes.</head>
        <p>First-Aid Classes.</p>
        <p>First-aid classes connected with the St. John Ambulance Nursing Division will commence on Wednesday '?vening at the Whitecross Club Room, Green-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Needlework Guild Gift.</head>
        <p>Needlework Guild Gift.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. R. Laws, police court mission-er, wishes to acknowledge the receipt of a parcel received Per Mrs. Lyon, of Appleton Hall, from the We Cheshire Needlework Guild.</p>
        <p>Padgate Wesleyan Church AnniversarY The Padgate Wesleyan Chureh anniversarY services will be held to-morrow. The preacher morning and evening will be the Rev. W. F. Flemington, Liverpool. On Tuesday next the , Rev. 'F. Luke Wiseman, ex-president of the Con:</p>
        <p>ference will preach 'in the afternoon and lecture in the evening. ,</p>
        <p>Music in the Park.</p>
        <p>The League of the Cross Band will play tho following programme to-morrow (Sunday) Hymn, selected; march, "Legion of Honour,' Hall; selection, "Recollections of Carl Rosa,' Round; fantasia, "Country Life," F. Le Due; selection, "English Songs," Seddon; selection,/ "La Traviata," Verdi; "God Save .the King. - Conductor, Mr. S. Hynes. •</p>
        <p>British Workers' National League. A great patriotic meeting under the auspices of the Warrington Branch of the British Workers' National League will be held in the Royal Court Theatre to-morrow evening. The speakers will be Mr. Joseph Terrett, Londor.i, and Mr. J. A. Seddon, while the chairman I! Mr. James Sexton, J.P., of Liverpool. A special invitation is extended to soldiers.</p>
        <p>Boy's Leg Fractured. When starting on his rounds the other day iP a bread cart owned by Messrs. Carter, Bridge street, by whom he was employed, a thirteen- year-old youth named Harry Higham, residing in Roome-street, was thrown, from the cart through it skidding in the tramlines. A wheel of the van passed over his leg, fracturing it. He was removed to the Infirmary, where be is progressing as well as can be expected. A Harvest Festival and a Pastor's</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Anniversary.</head>
        <p>Anniversary.</p>
        <p>Harvest festival services aire- being held to- morrow (Sunday) at Golborne-street Baptist Church, where there is the additional interest of the Pastor's fifth anniversary. The Rev. TT. • Ogden has worthily Occupied the ministeris? office and progress has been made during his ministry. To-morrow he will preach both morning and evening, the soloist being Mr. A• W. Pearce. In the afternoon a sacred cantata, entitled "A Song of Thanksgiving," will be rendered by the Legh-street Primitive Metho- dist Choir. The following Thursday evening 3 fruit banquet and congratulatory meeting f°l. the Pastor will be held.</p>
        <p>N.S.P.C.C. and Child-Neglect. e The National Society for the Prevention °L Cruelty to Children investigated 3,617 COD plaints of neglect and cruelty in England, Wales and Ireland during the month of August. Of the 3,531 completed cases, 3,401 were found true, affecting the welfare of 10,377 children and involving 4,186 offenders. Warnings were issued in 3,076 cases; 145 were prosecuted (re" suiting in 144 convictions); and 180 were dealt with by transfer or in other ways. From its foundation, in IsB4, the society has dealt with 924,030 complaints, involving 2,591,442 children. In the Warrington Branch during the same month 14 cases were dealt with, affecting 36 children.</p>
        <p>Emergency Training for Adults at I'Vpod's College. Splendid results have been achieved at the combined day and evening courses which have been in operation since January at this popular business-training centre. The course is of f" weeks' 'duration, and the fee is three guineas. Quite a large number of ladies have been sae' cessful in acquiring .a sensible and practical working knowledge of routine clerical duties, which has enabled them to accept well-remuner- ated positions as general or departmental clerks in many of the largest offices in the district. -A fair general education is an essential for pros" pective members, the training is comparativell easy of acquirement, and really good prospect! are open to ladies between 18 and 25 years 01 age. For further information address: The Secretary, Wood's College, 15, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Congregational Anniversary.</head>
        <p>Congregational Anniversary.</p>
        <p>Successful anniversary services in connection with the Elmwood-avenue Congregations' Church were held on Sunday. At the morning service there was a fair number of adults Pr,' sent, and a good attendance of children. Tne Rev. C. P. Pitt conducted the service.</p>
        <p>spoke to the children on unselfishness, and plained how that many Churches were moll; ments of this noblest of virtues. He pointea out that a church anniversary was not only 3 day of celebration, but also a day of re-0" mination. The foundations of a church were not primarily in the security of its buildin.g' but in the spirituality and passion for service found amongst those who composed•the Churc,ll,; The evening service was conducted by Rev. T. W. Bond, of St. Helens. The church accommodation was greatly taxed by a larg,e congregation. Special music was sung and CO lections were taken on behalf of Church</p>
        <p>British Industries Federation. The Federation of British Industries te ti;;; formation of which we referred several wet- .‘ ago, has issued a circular setting forth its P 7,1 gramme. The Federation, it is stated, v(i' represent producers and manufacturers in nalleilf the smile way as the General Federation ° Trade Unions represents • organised labour' ‘, Among the immediate objects of the Federatie° will be reconstruction after the war, devel°P; ment of sources of supply of raw material, sra consideration of questions arising out of tiir transition from war to peace such as demo sation, the future of temporary war worker 2: measures to mitigate unemployment during 1.`..1,,.; • transition period, and economic problems 1 general. The Federation will seek its "in co-operation with Labour and in conjune- tion .with the Government and Government dri. partmehts." On the Executive Council are sla J. S. Ham mood Banner, M.P. (the Pearson Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd.) and Mr. Peter Rylands (Rylands Bros., Ltd.). Pearson and Knowles. A general meeting, formal in eharacter—Pelle ing financial adjustments referred to in '- directors' annual report—was held on Tuesdele Sir Harmood Banner, M.P., in the chair. report was adopted on the motion of the Chi"' men, seconded by Mr. John J. Bleckly. On the motion of the Chairman, seconded bYt Mr. W. Peter Rylands, it was resolved: —"Th6t. the usual dividend at the rate of six per cole per annum on the first and second preferen shares, and a dividend on the ordinary sharesth% six shillings and sixpence per share for to- half-year to 30th June last—all less tax--re, gether with a bonus of five shillings per shill free of tax, on the ordinary shares, be paid ° the 29th instant." the Mr. Rylands was re-elected a director on motion of Mr. H. S. Bleckly, seconded by gala' H. Harmood Banner. nual The auditors were reappointed at an en 7 remuneration of £3OO. ueet It was decided to consider the balance fied at an adjourned meeting on a date to be and Sir Harmood Banner was thanked for3:llr. conduct in the chair, 45' 'llo6°ll of John J. Bleckly, second, Barrett.</p>
        <p>JAI led by Sir Wm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 30, 1916. PRIVATE DUKES.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 30, 1916. PRIVATE DUKES.</p>
        <p>Trade Unions To Fill His Place On Pensions Committee.</p>
        <p>STILL ANOTHER MEETING.</p>
        <p>The nominations of Private Charles Dukes (at the time he being a civilian) and Mr. A. E. Seabury as Labour representatives on the War Pensions Committee not having been accepted by the Town Council owing to their anti-war policy, the matter was referred to the Statutory Committee in London, and, as the outcome a it, a meeting of trade unions representatives is to be held at the Town, Hall on the 9th October.</p>
        <p>The letter which the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) has sent to the various unions reads as follows: "The Statutory Committee have up- held the objection of the Corporation to the nomination of Mr. Charles Dukes as a member of the local committee. With- regard to Mr. Seabury, the objection is,. disallowed; conse- quently there is now a vacancy, and I am re- quested by the Statutory Committee to com- municate the decision to- you. It will itow be necessary to meet for the selection of- another representative in place of Mr. Dukes,. '4nd I shall be glad if two representatives of your union will attend a meeting to be held at the Town Hall on the 9th of October at 8 p.m., in order to make the nomination, and I shall be pleased to receive the names of the delegates beforehand."</p>
        <p>A letter whieh somewhat ampli4.es the intro- ductory paragraph of that issued by the Mayor is now appended. It is written by the secre- tary of the Fabian Research' Department and addiessed to the secretary. of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council, Mr. A. E. Seabury holding, that position during, the absence of Private Dukes. .</p>
        <p>"Mrs. Sidney Webb, who is one of the repre- sentatives of Labour on the Statutory Com- mittee administering the Naval' and Military Pensions Act, has asked me to write to you on the question of the objection brought against Mr. Dukes •and-Mr. Seabury as 'representatives of the Trades Council on the Warrington local committee. It was reported that Mr. Dukes is at present under the military Service Act and thereby unable to serve on the committee, and to this objection Mrs. Webb and Mr. Gosling could bring-no opposition. In the case of your other representative, they refused'to•allow that •any valid objection had been brought; and the Statutory Committee has accordinglY refiised to sustain the objection of the Warrington Town Council to' your other- representative. Mrs. Webb and Mr. Gosling-ask me to' say that they hope the Trades Council 'will find this satigfae- tory ; they also suggest that it would be well to make no further agitation on the question of Mr. Dukes' position."•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>VOLUNTEER ORDERS..</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER ORDERS..</p>
        <p>Warrington .Company. Headquarters, Drill Hall. ORDERS- FOR WEEK, ENDING OCT. 7th,</p>
        <p>By CAPTAIN, W. PETER RYLANDS, Officer Commanding.</p>
        <p>Officer on duty during week.----Second-Lieut. C. IL Honit..</p>
        <p>Orderlies.—Corporal Cleeton an Lance- Corporal Wright, jun.</p>
        <p>Parades.</p>
        <p>To-morrow (Sunday), October lst.-2.30 p.m., Bank Park; short Route March; disperse 5 p.m. Monday, Tuesday and Thursday.-8 p.m., HeadquartOrs, Fire Drill.—Thursday, 6.45 Fire Brigade Station.</p>
        <p>Musketry.—Tuesday, 6.30; Wednesday, 7.30 p.m.; Legh-street Range.</p>
        <p>Memorandum.</p>
        <p>It is hoped next week to complete four Platoons, and all men who have not enrolled are requested to do so at once.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>VOLUNTEERS AND HOME DEFENCE.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEERS AND HOME DEFENCE.</p>
        <p>Lord French's Forecast.</p>
        <p>Lord French ingpected Volunteer battalions at Liverpool on Wednesday, a detachment of the Warrington Company of the ,-7th Lancashire Battalion being present. - - _</p>
        <p>Addressing the commandants and officers, he said the turniaut reflected great credit on every- body. The men appeared to have the makings of a very efficient body. He wanted the com- mandants and officers to impress on the men that we were living in very serious times, when the services of every man were required, and he hoped they would do all they could to dispel the ridiculous idea that had got abroad that the Volunteers were not wanted, and that the authorities did not pay much importance to them. That was absolutely false and erroneous, and he could assure them that their services were appreciated to the utmost by the War Office.</p>
        <p>He was sure they would do their utmost to make the Volunteers, what they promised to be, a really efficient and fighting body of men. If the war went on the time might come when they would look to the Volunteers for the defence of this country. That was the test for which they must prepare themselves. The Warrington Company (under the com- mand of Lieut. 3¢athias) was strongly repre- sented. The men joined their battalion at head- quarters (Toxteth Hall), and, headed by their fine bugle b§tnd, marched to the Pieqead, where they took up their position for the inspection. The smart serge uniforms of the Warringtonians were in striking contrast to the khaki and sombre " green-grey " drill of the Liverpools, and were favourably commented upon. The detachment was afterwards entertained to tea at headquarters, and the Commanding Officer (Major Fraser) expressed his satisfaction at the appearance and general bearing of the men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>SUMMER TIME.</head>
        <p>SUMMER TIME.</p>
        <p>A Successful Innovation Which Ends To-day.</p>
        <p>The Press Bureau issues the following At 3 a.m. (summer time) in the morning of Sunday, Ist October, Greenwich time will be restored by putting back the clock to 2 a.m. The hour 2-3 a.m. summer time will thus be followed by the hour 2-3 a.m. Greenwich time. All railway clocks and clocks in post offices and Government establishments will be put back one hour, and the Government requests the public to put back the time of all clocks and watches by one hour during the night of Saturday-Sunday, 30th September-lst October. Employers arc particularly recommended to warn all their workers in advance of the change of time. From Ist October onwards Greenwich time will be used for all purposes. By Order of the Home Secretary.</p>
        <p>Trains and the Time Table.</p>
        <p>The Railway Executive Committee give notice that trains starting on Saturday night, September 30th, or early on Sunday morning, October Ist, which are en route at 3 a.m., when the clocks are put back one hour, will after that time be running one hour earlier than the time shown in the official guides. In addition to those, certain other trains will start and run one hour before the time shown in the official time-tables. Particulars of the trains affected will be an- nounced by the companies concerned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>SHOP HOURS AND THE LIGHTING</head>
        <p>SHOP HOURS AND THE LIGHTING</p>
        <p>ORDER. To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln View of the corning Order for the closing of all shops a 7 p.m., would it be pos- sible for our authorities to make an Order that this bo the time for the lowering or extinction of lights in shop and business premises? If such an arrangeusent could be4nade the advan- tages to all are obvious. In the fiTst place it would do away with the vexatious search for the 'correct " Zepp." time, which differs nightly, and sometimes leads to a sharp argument with the police on duty. Further, the shopkeeper would be able to do his business in comfort until the hour of closing, when window lights would be extinguished and there would be. no question as to whether they were sufficiently subdued.—Yours, &amp;e., J. H. HOUGHTON. 28, Mersey-street, Warrington, September 20th, 1916.</p>
        <p>..,,,1 ! ''t 1 ~. , , .tw ;i::-</p>
        <p>- _ _ _ •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>DIED FOR T EIR eOUNTRY.</head>
        <p>DIED FOR T EIR eOUNTRY.</p>
        <p>Additional Local Casualties in the Various War Areas.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE E. PYE.</p>
        <p>"A Good Chap " Who Hoped For "Better Days To Come."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Heesom, 95, Birchall-street, has received official news of the death in action of her gallant brother, Private Ernest Pye, South Lancashire Regiment, on the 29th August. A comrade of the late Private's, writing to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>KILLED.</head>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>SERGEANT A. HELSBY.</p>
        <p>Going To Help Wounded Corn-</p>
        <p>rade When Fatally Hit.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Hewitt, 4, ,Wellington-street, have been notified that their son, Sergeant Arthur Helsby, a member of the Canadian Ex- peditionary Force, has been killed in action. A comrade writing to Mr. and Mrs. Hewitt said he was killed while running to help a man who was wounded; and he died as he had lived, a soldier and a man. _ _</p>
        <p>Sergeant Helsby, who emigrated to Canada seven'years ago, enlisted in Tune, 1915, and had been at the Front for thirteen months J ~,He was 29 years of age, and worked .as a moulder for the Pear Son and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., before going to Canada. He was educated At the National School, Church-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>RIFLEMAN T.- RYAN.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN T.- RYAN.</p>
        <p>Eighteen-Year-Old Youth Killed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>In Action.</head>
        <p>In Action.</p>
        <p>Only eighteen years of age, Rifleman Thos. Ryan, of the Rifle Brigade, son of Mr. and Mrs. N. Ryan, 40, Lythgoes-lane, has been killed in action. The news first reached Warrington through the medium Of a letter which a com- rade, J. Foster, wrote to his wife at 72, Dalton Bank, saying that Ryan had' "gone under." Mrs. Ryan immediately ;wrote to the War</p>
        <p>Killed.</p>
        <p>Office, and received a reply to the effect that her son was killed in action on the 3rd September. Rifleman Ryan wrote his last letter home on August 15th, and said: "I think the good old times are ended, but let us hope for the best. Things are looking up a bit out her." He sent a field card to his mother on September 3rd—the very day he was killed— saying he was quite well. Rifleman Ryan; who was formerly employed by Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd. joined the Army on the ,2nd October, 1914, and had been at the Front twelve months. Ile was educated at St. Benedict's R.C. School, and has two uncles serving at the Front.</p>
