      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>PRIVATE 'HERBERT BRAZENDALE.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE 'HERBERT BRAZENDALE.</p>
        <p>Killed.</p>
        <p>under the same date :—'</p>
        <p>" It is with 'a sad heart</p>
        <p>, that I write. No doubt by the time you get this letter you will have heard that your son was killed in action yesterday, the 15th, along With other members of the battalion. We were a little 'way from the front line at the time, and the Germans were shelling rather heavily. One shell burst only a yard or two away and killed your son. I know it will be a great blow to you to lose such a dear lad. I can assure You he was loved by all of us here, and will be greatly missed. .. . It may be some con- .solation to you to know that your son died While doing his duty for his country, and the home he loved dearly. I pray God will be with YOU in the sad loss of one, who along with Others, has been called upon to make the great sacrifice. Please accept the deepest sympathy of all the men, of his idatoon, for we all feel WO have lost a true and dear friend.- Private Brazendale, who, as a boy, attended the Christ Church SChool, Latchford, would lave been twenty-two years of age on the 13th "of next month. He belonged to the South Lancs. Regiment prior to the outbreak of the War, and has been in the Army altogether about three years. He went to the' Front twelve taonths last February. He was formerly enlployed as a moulder by Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co., Ltd., and attended Christ Church, Latchford.</p>
      </div>
