      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>PRIVATE P. L. ROWE.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE P. L. ROWE.</p>
        <p>Conflicting News As To His Fate Received By His Mother.</p>
        <p>Private Paul L. Rowe, Grenadier Guards, and son of the late Mr. P. J. and Mrs. Rowe, is reported by two persOns to have been killed, and by two more to be wounded. The letters which we reproduce below have been received by his mother, :who resides with her son-in-law, Mr. B. B. Ashbrook, at 129, Lovely-lane. , The first letter, dated September 24th, from a comrade, ran, "Just a few lines concerning your son Paul. We have been chums since we joined the Army, and we agreed to write to each other's parents if anything happened to either of us. I am sorry to say that I now find it my very painful duty to write and: let you know that Paul was killed in the trenches on September 14th. He put his head too high over the trench and 'a sniper shot him through the head He was very well liked by all his platoon. Only a few weeks ago the Platoon Sergeant asked him to take promotion, but he refused." .</p>
        <p>The same post brought a letter from the Rev. J. Leo Prescott, which ran, "Your son Paul has asked me to write to you to tell you of his wound. I am afraid it is serious. . . . Don't be surprised if worse news follows. I have given him the last sacrament. We have many bad cases, hence my short rioter" The third letter was received on Wednesday from the Rev. P. Casey, who said, "Your son Paul asks me to let you,know he is wounded and gone to hospital—not seriously I hope. He will soon get all right. He sends his kindest regards." •</p>
        <p>A fourth letter vats also received from a clearing station, dated September 24th, "I very</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. STEWART. Killed. -</p>
        <p>of Scotland, but' had resided in Warrington for 18 years.</p>
        <p>FURTHER NEWS OF CAPTAIN 0. LYON WHITTLE.</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) has received a telegram from the American Express Co. at Rotterdam stating: "Captain Whittle quite well; prisoner at Offizirr Kreigsgefangen- enlager, Giitersloh." . The Flying Corps Aid Committee have in- formed Mr. Whittle that the camp at Giitersloh is one of the best. The prisoners are in a large building (originally a lunatic asylum) and. have .a tennis court and are allowed to walk outside the camp three times a week. They are able to buy a good many things, but the rations provided are not at all good, and it is very necessary to send them regular parcels.</p>
        <p>Tennis Club and Wounded Soldiers. Last Saturday was the third occasion on which the 'Stockton Heath Tennis Club entertained wounded soldiers from the local hospitals. Tea was provided, and the members and their guests passed a pleasant evening at tennis and other games.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
