        <p>PIIIVATE JAMES DENTON. Died of Wounds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <p>of the South Lancs. Regiment, died of wound's on September 17th. • About a week ago Mrs. Denton received two letters saying her son was wounded. The first, from Second-Lieutenant Turner, ran as follows: "I regret that Private Denton has been wounded, but it is not a serious wound, so there Is no occasion to worry on your part. For my part I shall be sorry to lose him from my platoon, and shall be pleased to see him back again:"</p>
        <p>A comrade, Private B. Lynsky, wrote: "Just a few lines to let you know that James has been wounded, but not seriously, so you don't aeed to worry, as I hope he will be in England shortly. He was very cheerful when we left him at the dressing station." Private Denton, w,ho was twenty-three years •)f age, had been in the Army almost two years, and at the Front fifteen months. A native of Stockton Heath, whqe his family had resided until recently, he attended St. Thomas! School. Leaving 'there, he went to work for Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co. Ltd., where he was , employed as wmoulder until joining the Army. Another brother, Corporal J Denton, is serving J lin the Royal Engineers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>To Four Men Who Assisted</head>
        <p>To Four Men Who Assisted</p>
        <p>Captain Collingwood.</p>
        <p>Four brave men who rendered assistance on the Bth August to the late Captain Coiling- wood, of Newton, just before he died, have been presented with silver matchboxes from his widow, the mementoes being engraved with the date mentioned. Two of the four soldiers are Lance-Corimiml Ashworth, son of Mr. and Mrs. John Ashworth, of 15, Wakefield-street, and Private Charles Cranston Taylor, son of Mr. and Mrs. W. 'Taylor, of 1, Kendrick-street. ,With the iiresents came a letter to each, a typical one being: " Dear . The, enclosed is a small remembrance for you for being one a the four men who carried my husband. Captain Collingwood, when he was so badly wounded, to the dressing station on August Bth. Thank you very much. With best wishes for your safe return. Yours sincerely (signed) M. C. C. Collingwood." It may be added that when the lads left the Captain at the dressing station he remarked to them, "God bless you!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>PRIVATE W. BROADHURST.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE W. BROADHURST.</p>
        <p>Former Crosfield Employe Dies</p>
        <p>Of Wounds.</p>
        <p>Private William Broadhurst, of the South Lancashire Regiment, son of Mrs. Broadhurst, a widow, 5, Atherton-street, has died of wounds in a French hospital. In 'response to a tele- gram received on Tuesday morning saying he was lying dangerously ill, Mrs. Broadhurst cr6ssed to France to see him. Later, a letter reached Warrington from the assistant matron at the hospital stating that Private Broadhurst was seriously wounded in many plaees,, but was improving slightly. Yesterday morning, how- ever, the sad news, was received from the War Office that Private Broadhurst passed away on September 27th. A message of sympathy from the King and Queen was also received.</p>
        <p>Private Broadhurst, who waa twenty-seven years of age, was formerly employed in the box department at Messrs. Jos. Crosfield and Sons' works. He was called up in his group on the 14th February of this year, and had been at</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <p>• ~.._,... ..„....„....:. miii•:4.i.::::,..:,z,,,,,,,:,,,,,,,,,,,,A,,,,.,,,,,,,,,ii:mai4.,::::,,g,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,.A,,:04,,,::::0,i,,,, itt fmorgoisv...AlN' lAttglinititaM.!%.....g ...*5410:44, ';... ~Wgzet-0::n...,::-....:::::::,-,,.:;,:..=:4t1..ilt* ..,..5.04,.. , t4:-.wigge.,,,,.:043g,..-:, pwa. 4.:Amift.c,,:::::ooo, . 15.v,,,54--.6gßaigusl:4l:k42. ' ,-,-,,,,,vfx*....:,,,:min•-uw,o. • ,ft ;--, •••:::,..::;:.,-..:..:4::§i,:iif.,0,,,....:•:ig,›--.... &lt;'::;10.0::::&gt;•':".410:084WW.o.i,,,...- ,:&gt;-;,-,w .&lt; :' ...- s .;::altdOklikilat?.....iigx4;:il.:,4 *- : . 0.-Z,•,:-X:::•• ...4i.ilT-fri.tftt:.!.:•':::.s i:::. .m...,1,4Wrf-;.-..-::.,mtgiroiF..,:''',... *- . ::-;:-,:-..,•it‘,;::4.1.ii,i:A.: ~,,,,i,..i:i-,::.*K:i§§-,....:•:,...,miz,:rw,„-:,.---.-..,..--,s.i ... .. :...... .R.emaii.t.-:•::3:::::..D--.:,:•..t.:.,..4gii:,-..:: ...--.:4, ...... ,••,.'14..,...- • i'..4.:*:::.-40.:1-?,..:.V.`&lt;ziefi:.zt::.:,•,,,i.- 1,..w.,,,,-,..: $ .r-,,. . ... ,&lt;;.: • ....„.• .-"',.'..;:.'..,:,.0. ; , •:;.:-2''.:•',::!1,.,':'..-41''',.‘,.;:av-,:.*:::4, • ..•::::,;“,,5,',..,V.::'::44,1m...- - ci.,:.:,,;,.4::g::::::::Qtr.. s-.*...,,..,A.•::.' ::,:1:::fg•.A"......t.411"::ii3g.P.:&amp;-::..?.!::::::.:7:';11..40•Zigt:'.. A.Z.* - e:..;;Te.,g,:7.;..,,,' ... L.f.,,,...Aa#0,:.•::.,:-'g 1 4 ~m,..i.;, :...'•••:,..w,-,04,,::..K.A.,....-,:.;.:,-5::,:.:,,,:i.,:,......,A,:,,..im•:::\.. ~ :11i1R . .gw,ko...Y. ..z10,.1,:,.:: 1i5,.....:41 4 .21.W.,.....,.,:::.!:::EnAigi.,4,:::,t'.:,......; .....4 ...\::'...,,,:5.;,. ;-,..:5.-•;" ' :::0.00k.,-:.cw...,:.•;;,,:•.im00,m:.4.;:iiii...-.:.:3,..,,i..z., k,.,..ef.,:::,,,,,::•,:,,,:.,:.,,,,,,,,,,K,:wf.,;:,::,:::x 240-4etiolvailki.eNtrw. 3,ANA -,:w,.........;0):*:4,,.4,1fittt ;ktbil, A‘ • .:,,, .• •,:-••• :•,,,•• •:?,,i.,,,,,..0-.,:A„....,•,,,,,,,. ~... ,5.,..:„..„,.:..,...,..4. ~,..„..,...„,..::,,,„„•:::,.;,:.:„:., : ::,,,.: "...\ft;;,.:-..*443:w..•-i..,:',.:•e•:.,-,z, ,mws:7.;v:ig,', ....i:.,i,....; -., -,••,..1.,4...-- 4..--.)::_,,--•;-:. , :::-., s-.......;,,,,,N.-§....v.5.,,,,,v:.:,,,, ' .:,:.J.-.:!.::':;•N:•-,:,-.'.'..°Pl 1.4..•-::...;: •'-.;N:%'%nis:N::.7: ii.:.•''.••:•-• iiblite4.... ..,:it:A.v.4-• :::, '..- ,;%,3')... zi:.:-::'..... ..,•:•,,,,,,....,,w,-,.,. ~::... : :,,,,,--.i„:-.v.:-.N-i $4 't • ::.,,,:;.-...::::-:.., - .:, •, ::::.•:,,i.;::,,.. -.:•,.:,,,:::::::•,..,.,.:,.:,...........,...-.•:,4v,:•:.:Re..:v0:,,,0.., ~. ,-. . '.. . . -•:- ~..„-.•:,,:..,,,,,....•:::. •,,,...,a,m,::::!:::::,...,,,,Ki-::?;:.i,;.,,,,, .. . . .............. ... . ..</p>
        <p>PRIVATE WM. BROADHLTRST Died of Wounds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>LIEUTENANT S. J. PRICE.</head>
        <p>LIEUTENANT S. J. PRICE.</p>
        <p>1915. Before joining the Army he was an assist- ant master at St. Mary's R.C. School, Butter- market-street, and took great interest in the schoolboys' football competitions: He was also actively connected with the Catholic Boys' Bri- gade and the Catholic Young Men's Society. Four of his brothers are serving the Colours, an,l one of them, Battery-Sergeant-Major W. J. Price, of the Royal Field Artillery, won the D.C.M. in January.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>MEMORIAL SERVICE TO THE LATE CORPORAL J. BRIMELOn.</head>
        <p>MEMORIAL SERVICE TO THE LATE CORPORAL J. BRIMELOn.</p>
        <p>On Sunday night, in the Working Men's Mission, Bank Quay, Coun. G. Dale, in the course of a memorial service to the late Corporal J. Brimelow, said their young ,brother, whose loss they, mourned,' had finished his course at the age of 31, just when life seemed full of promise and hopeful- ' ness. Under conditions that were by no means ideal he had tried to do his duty as a Christian. He lived not for selfish ends, but to a very • large extent to help and 'bless others: He was associated with the Sunday school,, and intihe Christian Endeavour Society the members had looked forward hopefully to the end of ,the war with the expectation of having brother "Joe," with his cheery', smiling face, once more in their midst. 'But away out yonder on the battlefields of France the angels beckoned him and he followed. , And in connection with the League of Help, the lantern lectures and the Order of 'Rechabites he was inspired by the same spirit of self-abnegation. He had fought the good iight and had kept the faith, and in these trying: days of doubt and scepticism they were glad to know that he had kept the faith. They would think of their heroic brother in the coming days as one among the' noble sons of Britain who had fought the good fight and ha'l kept the faith and who, in aoing this. had "Scught to aspire to the highest ideals that the soul of man could know. To the berea4ed parents and each member of the family so sadly bereaved, also to the one who had their brother lived, would have been the partner. in life of his joys and sorrows, they would say, "Sorrow not as those without hope," but took forward to the time of re-union, and above all remember the words of our Lord: "Let not your heart be troubled; ye believe in God, be- lieve also in Me."</p>
        <p>Poor Work and Poor Pay. Poor work for poor pay is just what keeps thousands of workers where they are.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>AGE OF mum</head>
        <p>AGE OF mum</p>
        <p>Bishop's Striking Harvest Ser- mon' At Parish Church.</p>
        <p>BEER OR BREAD?</p>
        <p>"I believe that the ages that are coming will look upon our time as the age of waste," re- marked the Lord Bishop of Bu'rnley in a power- ful sermon relivered at a harvest festival ser- vice in the Warrington Pariek Church oni Thursday evening.</p>
        <p>That was perhaps because this country had been given an abundance of the gotta things of be added, and we under-valued. and wasted tliem.. It was _notorious that compared with other lands we wasted our coal instead of using it wisely, and it might be that higher prices would lead some to show more thrift in that</p>
        <p>ireetion that they had been willing to eserciBe</p>
        <p>in gratitude tcl the Almighty Giver. And then there was food. Those who had travelled in foreign lands knew well how through carelea%3ness and often through ignor- ance we wasted and made little of the food— meat, bread, and other articles—which ,we had enjoyed' in such an abundance here, turning often the most wholesome forms of food into something unwholesome and casting aside mueh that was good. How often had lone seen it not only in the homes Of the rich, but the poor. It was as though we had taken the food gifts of God4nd- thrown them back in His face.</p>
        <p>Our Lord filled the people with five barley. loaves and two fishes. In the East of England at present there were scores. of miles of barley; and how would it be used? How had we used it generally in times past? A little of it—that which we counted a lower quality—had been used as food for cattle, but the best of it and the most of it had been turned into strong drink. They could, therefore, surely say that the greater part of that gift from God had been useless, as it had been devoted for the ruin of the health•orthe body and of moral life. Were they doing right, especially in these days of comparative scarcity of food, in allowing barley to be used for strong drink, which they could very well do without, and which hAd proved' a snare and a curse to many, including wives of poor soldiers who did not titink they were fighting for homes spoiled by drink? They should learn how to be thrifty with Gbd's gift:s. The Bishop also spoke of the wastage due to infantile mortality and had a word of stricture for pro;sperous, self-centred people, who never did 'anything worth while, and when they went to the grave there was no obvious- vacancy to be filled.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving service, which in- cluded.the singing of the "Hallelujah Chorus,'' was beautifully and impresisvely rendered. Harvest thanksgiving services will be COIT.</p>
        <p>;Hine&amp; to,moProw in the Parish Church. The</p>
        <p>Rev. C. C. Eleum, Liverpool, is to preach iirthe morning, and the Rev. and Hon:- C. F. Cross, vicar of Stretton, in the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>CAI RO-STREET.</head>
        <p>CAI RO-STREET.</p>
        <p>Harvest festival thanksgiving services were</p>
        <p>Leld at the Cairo-street Chapel on Sunday; the</p>
        <p>preacher being the Rev. D. J. Evans, of Chester. An anthem, "0 Lord, how manifold are Thy works" (Barnby), was excellently sung by the choir, Mr. James Muir being at the organ. The vicinity of the pulpit had been nicely decorated by Miss Monks and the Misses Pemberton. Afterwards the flowers and fruit were dis- tributed among the local hospitals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>ST. JAMES'.</head>
        <p>ST. JAMES'.</p>
        <p>Harvest thanksgiving services in connection with St. James' Church were -commenced' on Thursday evening, the preacher being the Rev. F. S. Thatford, vicar of St. Paul's, Maccles- field. There was a large congregation present and the choir rendered special music, Mr. E. Davies being at the organ. The services will be continued to-morrow (Sunday), when the preachers will be the Rev. G. C. Briggs (vicar of Christ Church, Latch- ford) in the morning, %said the Vicar (the Rev. H. B. Firth) in the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>ST. ANN'S.</head>
        <p>ST. ANN'S.</p>
        <p>At St. Ann's Parish Church on Sunday har- vest festival services were held. The Vicar (the Rev. James Davies) preached in the morning, and the Rev. Horace Gray (headmaster of the Grammar School) delivered a sermon in the evening. Special music was rendered by the choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>BOLD-STREET WESLEYANS.</head>
        <p>BOLD-STREET WESLEYANS.</p>
        <p>The IBold-street -Wesleyan Church harvest thanksgiving services, held on Sunday, were largely attended, and the collections taken vn behalf of the Society Funds were considerably in advance of those of last year'. The preacher at the morning service was the Rev. F. Hudson, who recently began work in the Warrington Circuit. The choir gave, a beautiful rendering of Smart's " Te Deum." The Rev. P. Meson, who has just completed twelve months of his ministry in charge of the Bold-street., Church, was the preacher at the evening service. An anthem, " ,Praise the Lord, 0 Jerusalem," was sung by the choir, Miss Emily Taylor taking the solo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ST. PETER'S.</head>
        <p>ST. PETER'S.</p>
        <p>Harvest thanksgiving services were continued at St. Peter's Church on Sunday, in the morn- ing, afternoon and evening. The preacher morning and evening was the Rev. H. S. Bolton, vicar of Ravenhead, and in the after- noon the Rev. T. M. Armour, curate of St. ElPhin's. Special music was rendered by the choir, Mr. E. A. Padmore being at the organ. At the morning service the anthem, "While the earth remaineth," was rendered, and in</p>
        <p>the evening Simper's " Magnificat " and " Nunc Dimittis," 'and an anthem, "Ye shall dwell in.. the land," were sung with taste and expression. Solos in the anthem were taken by Miss Mabel Percival and Mr. 11. Sudlow. There wete good congregations at each service, and collections were taken on behalf of special church expenses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL INDEPENDENT METOODISTS.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL INDEPENDENT METOODISTS.</p>
        <p>At the Grappenhall independent Methodist Church on Sunday the harvest festival was cele- brated. Mr. Mort, of Lymm, was ,the preacher both morning and evening, and the choir ren- dered various anthems. In the afternoon a cantata was given by the Friars' Green Methodist Choir. The day was a great success in every way, the fine weather adding a finish to the whole. The proceeds amounted to the sum of £6 14s. 10d., being an increase of £2 on the preceding year. On Monday evening a tea was given, after which followed a short concert, there being both songs and recitations, and finally the fruit, vegetables and flowers, &amp;c., which added to the beauty of the church by their decoration on the previous day, were sold by Councillor R. Henshall. The proceeds, combined with the Sabbath's takings, made a total of .210 3s.</p>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival at the above church was held on Sunday. The preacher.in the morning ' was Mr. Lloyd Jones, and in the evening the Rev. F. Hudson, the newly dppointed minister. At the eveninc, service an anthem was well rendered by the choir, under the leadership of Mr. Pennington, and a quaxtet was taken by Miss Bromley, Miss Gough) and Messrs. H. Butler and J. Webb. The organist was Mr. F. Webb. The church had been beautifully decorated with fruit and flowers by lady mem- bers of the congregation. On Monday evening a fruit banquet was held in the schoolroom, at which there was a large attendance. The Rev. I F. Hudson presided, and he gave an interesting laccount of the manner in which a fruit banquet 'was held in the Transvaal, from which colony the reverend gentleman has just returned. The following programme was given :---Duet, Miss Gough and Miss Morris, "Goat bells "; solo, Mr. 11. Kay, "Asleep in the deep "; solo, Miss Morris, "Children's home"; solo, Miss Gough, "Home land." The children, led by the Chairman, also sang the well-known hymn, "Gentle Jesus." At the close of the pro- gramme a hearty vote of thanks was passed to the Chairman, the artistes,. the decorators, and all who had assisted in the work in connection with the festival. Afterwards the fruit and vegetables were sold and the proceeds were forwarded to the funds of the Y.M.C.A. in their work on behalf of the Army.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>STAGE AND KINEMA,</head>
        <p>STAGE AND KINEMA,</p>
        <p>Royal Court Theatre. All tkose interested in the higher class of plays regard Mr. Raynor as a benefactor, for he is one' of the very few gentlemen touring high-class repertoire at the present time.</p>
        <p>He has had his own companies for eleven years, and if hp found it upfiiii• work ttlitte earlier years he has now Income eminently successful.</p>
        <p>Since starting as play producer he has staged! 75 plays—possibly as many, as onyone else id% the profession. This week at the Royal Court .four plays have been presented—'A Bunch of Violets," "The Dancing Girl," "Moths," and "Lady Huntworth's "Experiment," - "The Winch of Violets," to be given to-night, is the first play which Mr. Raynor ever prriduced. aril happy to intimate that another visit will be paid by this company to Warrington irt January next.</p>
        <p>The announcement of Thursday matinees, as well' as those on Mondays, has met with a chorus of approval. The theatre was full on Thurs- day, when " Moths " was produced.</p>
        <p>Next week a lively revue., will doubtless diair full Minsk to the Royal Court. This is one of the latest and most amusing farcical feasts of fun, and the title "&amp;u Revoir Paris,' iS enough to suggest an evening of undiluted' amusement. In six scenes, a number of unusual attractions are promised, and the merriment will be coutinuous. Forty first-class artistes will occupy the stage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>'alace and Hippodrome.</head>
        <p>'alace and Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>The performance at the Hippodrome during ;he week has been of such all-round excellence</p>
        <p>that it i•llard to single out any particular turn far specfal praise Allan MacDonald and Com- pany, from the Apollo Theatre, London, put on, a bright Scottish sketch, "The Elder of the Kirk." The part of the theatre-abhoring Church' deer was excellently filled by tho author, 3fr. Allan MacDonald.</p>
        <p>Harry Russell had it 'practically all his own way- withi Ilis really amusing turn—at least as much of his own way as his own conductor would permit His songs and cross-talk occasioned' great •merriment. Another Harry made a good hit—Harry Pearson—and there is an excellent reason for his so doing. He is a Lancasliii-e- comedian and a rare one at that.</p>
        <p>Next weelYs programme contains the name of that marv-elibus memory man, Datas, who promises an entirely new act, with all the incidents oft the present war up-to-date. Con- way and Lerand the merry monopedes, are a big attraction,. amtl a troupe of Japanese con- jurors will introduce their enchanted fountains</p>
        <p>and "Box of "Death." Gus Harris, Hebrew comedian, iS,-a great favourite in the halls, and the Carlton Trio of vocal and instrumental entertainers, and The Three Dancing Madcaps will supply further attractions.</p>
        <p>Owing to repeated requests the management have arranged for a season of Monday after- noon performances, the first of which will be held on Monday next. Variety in the afternoon is quite as enjoyable as in the evening, and thousands will' appreciate the innovation at the Hippodrome. !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Kinemas.</head>
        <p>The Kinemas.</p>
        <p>There is now showing at the Star Kinema House, Church-street, a, fine drama, entitled "Hazel Kirke," and Part I. of the great serial, "Peg o' the Ring." For the first half of next week we are to have Hazel Dawn in "My Lady Ineog.," and for the latter half of the week the "star" picture will be "Little Mary Sunshine."</p>
        <p>At the Grand Electric Theatre a fine film, entitled "Niobe "' now holds sway. For the first half of next week that fine picture "The Battle of. the Somme" will make its second appearance in Warrington. Special matinees will he held eac-h day at 1 and 3 o'clock and evening performances at 6.30 and 8.30. The eighteenth episode of "The Diamond from the Sky" will also be screened. The great circus serial, "Peg o" the Ring," will commence dur- ing the latter half of the week, 'and .that great picture, "Carmen."</p>
        <p>At the Pavilion, Sankey Green, there is now being screened' "'Four Feathers," a soul-stirring photo play. During ,the first half of next week "A Vagabond's Vengeance," a fine drama by Floience Britton, and the eighteenth episode of "The Diamond from the Sky" will be shown.. For the Tatter half "The Christian," adapted from the well-known novel by Hall Caine, will be the chief picture.</p>
        <p>FOYER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>LINDLEY-HOGG.</p>
        <p>A wedding of a quiet nature took place at St. Paul's Church on Thursday morning, the Rev. E. Downham officiating, between Mr: Frank Lindley., brother of Councillor Lindley, Bewsey-road, and Miss Eliza Hogg, who for- merly resided with Dr. Jago, of Latchford. The bride, who was given away by Dr. Jago, was neatly attired in a navy blue travelling cos- tume. Mr. Harry Sudlow acted as best man. Following .the eeremony the happy couple left for Blackpool. Numerous presents have been received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>WILLIAMS-ENGLAND.</head>
        <p>WILLIAMS-ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>The wedding was solemnised at the Padgate Wesleyan Church on Saturday morning last of Miss Mabel England, eldest daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Ebenezer England, of Walden, Fearnhead, and Private Alfred Edward Wil- liams, son of Mr. and Mrs. Williams, Earle- street, Earlestown, now serving in the R.A..M.C. at Aldershot. The bride, who was given away by her father, looked very charming in a cream costume, with hat to match, and the brides- maid, Miss Bessie England, sister of the bride, was attired in a blue costume and hat to match. Both carried bouquets of pink carna- tions. The duties of best man were ably car- ried out by Sergeant R. R. Rice, of the Train- ing Reserve Battalion, Whitchurch: A recep- tion was held at the home of the bride, after which the happy couple left for Wallasey,. where the honeymoon was spent, the bride- groom having been granted a few days' leave for the occasion, previous to embarkation. The officiating minister was the Rev. A. J. Morton. The wedding, owing to the war, was of a quiet nature and was by special licence.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Fewer Births and Deaths.</head>
        <p>Fewer Births and Deaths.</p>
        <p>- During last week 30 births took place in the borough and there were 16 deaths, as against 41 and 23 respectively for the corresponding week of last-year. •</p>
        <p>Warrington Market Prices. At Wednesday's Warrington Market the price of new wheat was Bs. 3d. per 701 b., and 4s. to 4s. 3d. per 451 b. was realised for new oats. Flour realised 475. 6d. to 525. Gd. per 2401 b., thirds 2s. 4d. to 2s. 7d. per 201 b., bran and other off als is. 7d. to is. 9d. per 201 b., wheat straw 70s. per ton.</p>
        <p>Legh Street Primitive Methodist ' Church. Special services will beheld to-morrow in this church. The Rev. T. A. Young, of Widnes, will preach. On Thursday the Rev. H. Davenport, of Oldham, will preach in the afternoon, and in the evening will deliver a lecture, his subject being "C. H. Spurgeon."</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Dunn, Stockton Heath. Yesterday the funeral of a most respected resident of Stockton Heath took place, when Mrs. Dunn, of 47, Cawdor-street, was interred in Hill Cliffe Graveyard. Mrs. Dunn was the widow of Mr. Dunn, who was formerly a. traveller for Messrs. E. Richardson and Son, of Bridge-street. Mrs. Dunn was the daughter of the late Mr. Howard, of Sankey Bridges, and sister of the late Rev. Joseph Howard. She be- longed to Bold-street Wesleyan Church, and leaves one son, Mr. Arthur Dunn. Her death was due to pneumonia.</p>
      </div>
