      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>PHOSPHATE GIVES WONDERFUL STRENGTH.</head>
        <p>PHOSPHATE GIVES WONDERFUL STRENGTH.</p>
        <p>Specialist Tells How To Double Strength And Endurance.</p>
        <p>A, great authority on the subject of health, strength, and endurance, in explaining the remarkable strength and endurance obtained from the use of the product known among chemists as bitro-phosphate, clainA it is entirely due to its wonderfnl nerce-building properties. He says, and _J must be admitted that his logic is unanswerable, that, inasmuch as the muscles simply transmit power derived from the nervous system, strong nerves and a big reserve of nervous energy are infinitely more .important than big muscles. Bitro-phosphate, not being a drug or a stimulant, but a food which supplies direct to the brain and nerves the required phosphoric elements, was first prescribed by physicians with astonishing suCC'eSS in the treatment of neurasth9nia, insomnia, nervous- ness, debility, and mental depression. It was soon noticed, however, that, in .addition to permanently relieving nervous disorders, it also ,marvellously increased strength and endurance. The amount usually prescribed is one 5-grain tablet with each meal, and, in view of its com- paratively inexpensiveness, those who wish to increase their strength and endurance, as well as those who suffer from nervous disorders, are strongly advised to give it an immediate trial. We understand that pure bitro-phosphate is -obtainable from H. Y. Ashton, 63, Sankey- street, as well as from other high-class chemists everywhere,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>SERGEANT DANDY.</head>
        <p>SERGEANT DANDY.</p>
        <p>Death Of Hero Who Was impers.onated. •</p>
        <p>IVIATR :MON iL ' MYSTERY RECALLED.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Dandy, a Lynnn man, who was impersonated by another soldier who professed to be the husband of Mrs. par:dy, must now be added to the list of dead heroes. Mrs. Watson, his sister, who lives at 173, Clowes-street, West Gorton, hts .received official information that he has been killed. He was reported missing at Gallipoli on June 4th, 1915, and nothing further could be heard of him, despite the most searching inquiries. The Sergeant's name came prominently be- fore the Public in connection with a remark- able story of mistaken identity which was re- lated in the Manchester• City Police Court and at the Manchester Assizes at the .end of last year. _ _</p>
        <p>- His wife, Mrs. Dandy, was the victim of a cruel case of impersonation on the part of</p>
