      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>SERGEANT DANDY.</head>
        <p>SERGEANT DANDY.</p>
        <p>another soldier, who was afterwards identified as George Parkin Hall, a labourer, who had enlisted in the Lancashire- Fusiliers, and who was sentenced to three years' penal servitude for the offence.</p>
        <p>It will be remembered that Mrs. Dandy and the relatives of the dead sergeant were de- ceived by Hall's remarkable likeness to the sergeant, but their suspicions were aroused later, and the man was arrested.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Dandy was the son of Mr. and Mrs. Frederick Dandy, of Newfields, Lymm, and was 39 years of age! He formerly worked for ,the late Mr. F. 0. Mostoia, fustian cutter. The trial of Hall last November created a great sensation. Mr. Justice Low, charging the Grand Jury, said the peculiarity of the case arose from the circumstance that the prisoner, as was usual in charges of that nature, did not use force to accomplish his purpose, but was alleged to have used artifice amounting to fraud. The woman's husband had gone away to the war, and it was said that the prisoner returned from the war and personated the husband. There were some re- markable features in the case, in that the hus- band had been absent a comparatively short time, something under a year, and yet his sister and other relatives seemed to have be- lieved that the prisoner was the husband. Be- yond question he lived and cohabited for some days and nights with the wife.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE MEETING.</p>
        <p>Prosecuting Counsel explained that the charge was made under the Criminal Law Amendment Act. A man who committed the offence named against a -woman by _impersonat- ing her husband was deemed guilty under the section, the consent of the woinan, being im- material. Mrs. Dandy was the wife of a ser- geant in the Bth Manchesters who went out to Egypt last autumn, and she kept a baker's shop in Clowes-street, West Gorton.. On the morning of July 16th a neighbour named Dutton brought the defendant into the shop. Mrs. Dandy asked who the man was, and Dutton said "Herbert." Mrs. Dandy said "Never." Explaining the defendant's appearance, counsel said that be- fore entering Mrs. Dandy's shop the defendant went into Dutton's shop and said, "How is Mr. Dutton jetting on?" Mr. Dutton in- quired, "Is it Herbert Dandy?" and the de- fendant answered "Yes." The def.endant ex- plained that he had ,got,an hour off from Whit- worth-street Hospital and asked Dutton to tell his wife and children, but after ,t,ome discus- sion he was induced to go himself, Dutton accompanying him. The wife was suspicious and said, "You have altered," and the de- fendant replied that he had lain for 72 hours without help on the battlefield, and that was sufficient to a,lter anyone. Mr,. Dandy began to press him about personal matters, and in answer to questions he said he had lest his memory. The defendant was undotibtedly like Mrs: Dandy's husband. She sent for her lit s- band's sister, and the sister took him to be Sergeant Dandy. The neighbours were simi- larly deceived. The defendant left at one. Next day she took him home, and he remained with her as her husband for some days. Then Mrs. Dandy became suspicious, and asked him why he was dressed as a private. He said he had broken out of Netley Hospital because they were going to put him in a padded room, and he took the first clothes handy. She asked him what had become of the abscess mark on his neck and tattoo marks. He answered that the first had been filled up and the second burned. out. On July 23rd she went to the hospital with him, and from something said by a soldier she occupied a separate room. Afterwards Mrs. Dandy sent for her brother in Yorkshire, and also for a sister, and both thot.tat the defend- ant was her husband. They took him with them to Yorkshire for a few days, and after his return on August 9th Mrs. Dandy would not allow him to stay in the house until he had proved that he was Sergeant Dandy. She went to the station to see him off, and there he was arrested by Detective-Inspector Thomas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>HALL'S WIFE'S DECLARATION.</head>
        <p>HALL'S WIFE'S DECLARATION.</p>
        <p>.1f</p>
