      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <p>V-4 --F. L 'I</p>
        <p>that she was married to the accused in Lincoln- shire, and there had been six children. Mr. Brocklehurst : In what year were you married?</p>
        <p>The witness coUld not remeniber, and caused much amusement by saying"As . him" (the prisoner) "he is there." It transpired that they were married in 1899. Her husband, she added, enlisted in the 19th Battalion Salford Fuailiers, and she had received 10s. a week as separation allowance. A remarkable story was told by Inspector Thomas, who stated that when he saw the prisoner at Exchange Station he asked him to explain his movements for a time before he saw Mrs. Dandy. Hall said: "My memory Is a blank during that period." The prisoner ap- pealed to Mrs. Dandy, saying, "You are not going to have me locked up?" She answered that she was not. Thereupon the officer said, "It is not now in your hands." The witness asked him certain questions about his marriage, all of which he answered. He said he was married to Mrs. Dandy at Latchford, and that a child was born and Christened there. The witness said he was satisfied, after trying to find an abscess mark on prisoner's neck, that Hall was not Dandy, and he said he would lock him up. To this the prisoner remarked, "It is a fine thing for a man who has bled and fought for his country to be locked up." Hall did not go into the witness box and, after the jury had found him guilty, the Judge, in passing sentence, said: "This is a most im- pudent crime."</p>
        <p>- Hall: I was led to it. The Judge: You by some means or other rye don't know ,</p>
        <p>Hall: It was in a public-house -</p>
        <p>The Judge: You got to know that you bore a strong resemblance to Sergeant Dandy, and, availing yourself of that information, you de- liberately perpetrated this crime. You must F., to penal servitude for three years.</p>
      </div>
