      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 30, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 30, 1916.</p>
        <p>..--„, _ A yik. _ -;aii., .. 4 4.. ~. , i,: .. 2 , j PAST - ICT - E ,k, 3 i•-• I . ... . . _._ ....„ ..._ .:___</p>
        <p>LAYMIVI</p>
        <p>LOCAL FACTS AND FANCIES.</p>
        <p>By CRITIC. Don't fGrget that to-night's the night, and put your clocks back one hour.</p>
        <p>Our timepieces have been continually " lying " just lately, but once again they will have the opportunity to tell the truth.</p>
        <p>An extra hour's sleep. What a blessing!</p>
        <p>For all of us, bar the poor " Specials " who happen to be on duty at that particular time.</p>
        <p>They will find themselves "let in" for an extra hour's vigil.</p>
        <p>iNever mind, ,they said they could do it. (Without sweating.)</p>
        <p>And if the Zepps come they will already be on duty.</p>
        <p>And won't knock their shins on the furni- ture getting out in the dark.,</p>
        <p>By the way, what do you think about the Zepp scare last Tuesday morning?</p>
        <p>The Volunteers and Specials were soon on the scene; in fact every man notified turned out.</p>
        <p>The Specials patrolled one half of the village and the Volunteers the other.</p>
        <p>This is the first bit of real work they have been called upon to perform, and it is gratify- ing to know that they performed it satisfac- torily. •</p>
        <p>For convenience, the Volunteers have changed their headquarters from the Drill- Hall to the Conservative Club.</p>
        <p>This only applies in file case of air raids Will putside members please note?</p>
        <p>The password down my way at the present is, "Put that light out there."</p>
        <p>Our workers, have just had a well-earned vacation. ,</p>
        <p>The principal, works in this district closed down on Thursday for the remainder of the week.</p>
        <p>Who was the person that wouldn't put hi 3 light out the other morning because he could not see to go to sleep?</p>
        <p>The' local Volunteers were last Wednesday in- spected by Lord French. the Field-Marshal Commanding the Home Forces.</p>
        <p>, This interesting event took place at the Town Hall, Chester.</p>
        <p>The platpon turned out in strength, for the number on parade was fifty-two all told.</p>
        <p>It is officially understood that the Rev. Tudor-Evans, the rector of Oughtrington, will in future act as the regimental chaplain.</p>
        <p>I hear that the Lymm platoon have now got a hew marching song. It goes something like this : • Field-Marshal French he came to me and took • me by the hand, - He said to me that the Lymm platoon was the smartest in the land. Tra-la-ra-la,</p>
        <p>• A final warning. Don't forget old Father Time this evening.</p>
        <p>Late Mr. A.' W. Callender, J.P.—At Monday's meeting of Lymm Urban Council, Mr. W. MuHard (clerk) read a letter from Mrs. Callen- der acknowledging, with many thanks, the Council's resolution of condolence so kindly passed at their last meeting. "Will you be SCr good," she wrote, "as to convey to the Council my heartfelt thanks for your very kind expres- sion of sympathy in our overwhelming sorrow? Will you please tell them how much touched we are by their kind thought for us in pur most sad bereavement, and how greatly we appreciate their kindness and sympathy?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>VOLUNTEERS AT CHESTER.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEERS AT CHESTER.</p>
        <p>Inspected. By The Field Marshal Commanding Home Forces.</p>
        <p>Stirring scenes were witnessed at Chester on Wednesday evening, when the members of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment were inspected by Lord French, the Field-Marshal Command- ing the Home Forces; The entire regiment were lined up in "battalions irt mess" along Northgate-street, facing the Town Hall. Soon after 6.30 the inspecting party arrived, and the regiment was called to attention, and the com- manding officers of each battalion stood at the salute while the regimental band played the regulation bars. The distinguished Field- Marshal then walked down the line and made a thorough inspection. Afterwards the entire regiment marched past in column of fours, the saluting base then being the Town Hall steps. Quite a large number of wounded, soldiers, Cadets, Scouts and veterans of the Cheshire Regiment were also inspected. The Lymm and Oughtrington platoons, which are attached to the Altrincham battalion, were amalgamated again under the command of Platoon Com- ander G. R. Williamson, with Platoon Sergeant S. D. Moston and Sergeants 'I: _I./eters lind N. Wood. The Rev. Tudor-Evans, rector of Oughtrington, who is a member of the Ougllt- rington platoon, Was in attendance as regi- mental chaplain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>LYMM PLATOON ORDERS.</head>
        <p>LYMM PLATOON ORDERS.</p>
        <p>Tuesday, October 3rd.— 7.30 p.m., Recruits' Drill and Musketry; 8 p.m., Platoon Drill. Thursday, October sth.—Patrol Drill in Uniform. Fall in Conservative Club, 8 p.m. prompt. .This being a special parade, will all members make an effort to attalnd?</p>
        <p>Orderlies for week.—Sergt. Wood and nivate Edwards.</p>
        <p>G. R. WILLIAMSON, Platoon Commander.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <p>SERGEANT DANDY.—Continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>" FIELD " DEMONSTRATION.</head>
        <p>THE " FIELD " DEMONSTRATION.</p>
        <p>At Monday's meeting of Lymm Urban Council,</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Councillor F. Lockyer); in re- ferring to the public demonstratibn in honour of Q.-M.-S. Field, D.C.M., said he wished to express his thanks to the , Council and officials and all those who took part in the procession, and also to those who so kindly acknowledged the invitation and who could not attend. He also proposed that the best thanks of the Council be conveyed .to Colonel McClure, Major Alexander, Captain Cartwright and Captain Gledhill for thfir kindness and the honour which they had paid to the Council on that occasion, which would be remembered in the days to come. (Hear, hear.) Their sincere thanks were also due to the bandmaster and the police for their kind assistance on that occasion. and he would als6. like to thank Mr._ Cowper . . _ _</p>
        <p>and Mr. Gavin Cooke for the interest they had taken in the proceedings. Councillor Cowper thanked the Chairman for the kind manner in which he mentioned his humble services. He was always pleased to do what he could for his King and country. Councillor Gotobed said they had a day that would be looked upon in the future as a red letter day. (Applause.) They did honour to a man who deserved honour. The Chairman was to be congratulated on the arrangements that had been made. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>EXEMPTED MEN.</head>
        <p>EXEMPTED MEN.</p>
        <p>The Danger Of Rushing To Munition</p>
        <p>Works.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the business of Lymm Urban Council on Monday (routine matters were disposed of), Councillor Cowper, as deputed to look after registration in the dis- trict, asked how it was that some men-in certi- fied trades who had been exempted by the Tribunal afterwards left their trades and went into munition works. In Manchester, when men were exempted, they .had to keep in the business they were in. •</p>
        <p>Mr. Mulla-rd, the clerk, to whom the question had been put, said it was one which he could not answer.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gotobed said they had appointed Mr. Cowper to this office, and he suggested that if he had good grounds for the statement—and he was sure he had—he should- embody them in a letter to the Chairman of the Tribunal.</p>
        <p>Councillor Cowper said he was much obliged to Councillor Gotobed for mentioning.that, and he would do so.'</p>
        <p>Councillor Holt said that the Tribunal grants a man conditional exemption or temporary exemption. He comes before them at the ex- piration of a few months, and• if he has shifted his occupation the circumstances are different, and he would be differently dealt with. The military authorities had got to deal with these men. They were going through these works combing out and seeing if a man was badged whether he was entitlecto it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</head>
        <p>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</p>
        <p>Chairman, T. J. Ridgway; Military Repre- sentatives, Messrs. A. de C."Parmiter and 11! E. Evans.</p>
        <p>Not Yet Released.</p>
        <p>The case referred to at the last,meeting, in which the man applied for had been called to the Colours, came up again for hearing. The applicant (a local farmer) stated that the man had not yet been released, but he had received a letter from the military authorities at Chester stating that if he would forward them particulars as to where his unit was stationed they would endeavour to get the man trans- ferred to the Army Reserve. Stating his case for exemption, he said that he had a farm of 50 acres, 17 of which were amble. He had three horses, one foal and 1.0 head of cattle. The man was teamsman, and could do any farm work. The man was over the other week- end and said that the military authorities at his end would not even listen to him. When did he attest?—December 10th. He was most anxious not to be a conscript. , The Military Representative: He was called up because he gave his addiv,3 as Thelwall, , Thelwall then being in the Frodsham recruit- ing area.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Yes, but I cannot understand them keeping him. The Military Representative: He will be put in Army Reserve. Applicant: He is entitled to be put in the same positioxi, he was in prior to going to' Chester.</p>
        <p>Adjourned until the 3rd November for man to appear.</p>
        <p>Painter For Son.</p>
        <p>A master, painter, plumber and decorator, who made a second appeal for his son, stated that he was his only workman. He had a lot of work on hand, and he himself could only do work that needed no climbing. Without the assistance of his son, whq was a married man with two children, he could not possibly carry on.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. L. Welford; solicitor, representing applicant: Has the son been before the Medical Board ?—No.</p>
        <p>Hai-e you ever tried to get labour?—Yes. I have never had a man applying for work this summer. I have offered 10th per hour, which I really could not afford to pay. I should have been out of pocket. Temporary exemption till October 20th, the Chairman remarking that before the applicant could lodge the next appeal he would have to get the consent of the Tribunal. Appeal Out Of Order.</p>
        <p>A master painter, plumber and decorator„ who appealed for a man, stated that ye was a salaried partner, and had managed the business for some considerble time.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. L. Welford, who represented_ appli- cant, in apologising to the Tribunal, stated that the member of the firm who intended to be present had been called away on important business, and was unable to attend. In the cir- cumstances he would ask the Tribunal to grant an adjournment. (This was the second appeal.) The Clerk pointed out that the present appeal form was signed by the firm, in the firm's namb, and the previous one was signed in the man's name. The present appeal was, therefore, out of order, and could not be consideied. The Chairman: The only way that we could put it right is to grant an adjournment and give him leave to re-sign it. The Tribunal granted an adjournment until October 6th, and gave leave to amend the appeal. " Coal-bagging Trade.</p>
        <p>A coal merchant who appealed for his son, stated that he bought the business for his sons five 'years ago. He had two sons, but one suffered ill-health, and recently had to leave the business owing to the work being too heavy. He had 150, customers to serve weekly, which represent an average of about! 15 tons weekly. The coal was taken from house to house in bags. The weight of each bag of coal was slightly over lcwt. Does your client work?—Yes. The doctor told him to do light work. He has twice burst blood vessels. The Military Representative: Have you any medical evidence to this effect?—No! He was, very reluctant about telling me. Why? There •is no disgrace in bursting blood vessels; -</p>
        <p>Adjourned "till October 6th for the man to go before Medical Board.</p>
        <p>Wounded Soldiers Entertain School Children. —The wounded soldiers from the Brookfield Hospital entertained a large number of school children in the Drill Hall last Saturday after- noon. They gave a repeat performance of the previous t Wednesday's splendid concert (slightly curtailed), which was thoroughly enjoyed. The children from the Lymm and Oughtrington Schools were specially invited, and the wounded soldiers from the 'Raddon Court Hospital also attended. Each child d ,pai a penny for admission, and by these means be- tweeri four and five pounds was added to the already magnificent sum. Great credit is due to Sergeant Corcoran (the organiser) and his merry men for their splendid effort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>DEATH OF FORMER TERRITORIAL.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF FORMER TERRITORIAL.</p>
        <p>Air. H. Rigby Succumbs To Injuries</p>
        <p>Sustained At Work.</p>
        <p>It is with the deepest regret that we announce the death of ex-Private Herbert Rigby, a well- known time-expired Territorial. He was fol- lowing his employment at the Partington Steel and Iron \Co., Irlam, last week-end, when a large portion of red hot slag fell on the top of ,him and a fellow workmate. His workmate suc- cumbed to the severe burns the same morning, and despite the best attention rendered by the officials of the Manchester Royal Infirmary, Mr. Rigby succumbed early on Thursday morning. The news will be received with sorrow by our lads "out there," for up to a short time ago he was one of their popular comrades. During the present crisis he has about two years' service to his credit, sixteen months of which have been spent in France. He was 24 years of age, single, and resided with his parents at Rush Green. He was a brave and noble lad, always willing to de a good turn, and exceedingly popular. His fellow victim was a married man with two children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>PRIVATE THOMAS MOSTON- ILL IN HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE THOMAS MOSTON- ILL IN HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Private Thomas Moston, of the Royal Scots, has been admitted to hospital suffering from a general breakdown. In a letter received by some relatives in Statham, it is stated that he had ,been admitted into hospital, but at the time of writing he was feeling a good deal better. Private Thomas Moston resides with parents at Rush Green. He enlisted in the Royal Scots in February, 1915, and was drafted out to Egypt in August of the same year. After serving some month's in this Eastern clime, he was sent out to France with anothei battalion, arriving there in May of this year. His battalion took part in the big advance, and at the time he became ill they were on the banks of the Somme. Prior to the war he was employed as a butcher at Northenden. He was educated at the .Lymm School and wor- shipped at the Congregational Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>GRAPPENTIALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENTIALL.</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPL. FAHMURST</p>
        <p>A Good Soldier Who Was IViarked For Promotion. Lance-Corporal Thomas Fairhurst, of the South Lancashire Regiment, son of Mr. and Mrs. Frederick Fairhurst, Knutsford-road, Grappenhall, was killed in action September 9th.</p>
        <p>Writing to Mr. Fairhurst, his superior offiecr says :</p>
        <p>"I want to send you a few lines on behalf</p>
