      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>LANCE-CORPORAL T. FAIRHURST. Killed.</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL T. FAIRHURST. Killed.</p>
        <p>of myself and the officers of B Company to ex- press our sorrow and our sincere sympathy with you over,the loss of your gallant son. He was a good soldier and N.C.0., and it was my in- tention on leaving this part to put him up for a second Stripe. He died doing his duty, and I think this thought will help to alleviate the bitter grief of his loss.—With deep sympathy, I remain, yours truly, E. 0. Crosfield." Lance-Corporal Fairhurst was 24 years of age, and was an old member of the South Lancs. Regiment, having been with them four years previous to the war. When war broke out he felt it his duty to join the Colours. After train- ing for a few months he finally crossed over to France on the 12th February, 1915: He had but one leave, which was last November. Always bright and of a cheerful disposition, he was be- loved of all who knew him. He was born in Grappenhall, and was educated under Mr. Evans at the Grappenhall School, and was also a member of the Grappenhall Independent Methodist Sunday School and Chapel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for NVeek ending Saturday, October 7th. Monday.—Musketry at Range, 7.30 p.m.; Range Officer, Section Corporal E. Pearce Tuesday.—Company and Recruit Drill. in at headquarters, 7.45 p.m. Friday.—Musketry at Range, 7.45 p.m.; Range Officer, Section Commander W. Trantom. Saturday.—Musketry at Daresbury. Fall in at headquarters, 2.45 p.m. Orderlies.—Section Commander W. Trantom, Private J. W. Lee.</p>
        <p>Fall</p>
        <p>F. A. FROST. O.C. Our report of the inspection at Chesar appears under Stockton Heath news.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>PAD GATE.</head>
        <p>PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild Social.—The opening social of the Fesley Guild for the winter session was held on Thursday evening, presided over by the Rev. A. J. Morton. In a ishort address to the members he mentioned that we were passing thrpugh the greatest epoch of any history that hag ever been made, and woe betide any Church who is not alive to its liabilities. We ought not to be careless and listless, but we want to keep the spiritual things of life fore- most, all pleasures to have second place. Songs were very ably sung by Miss Vera Jones and Mrs. Hazeldine, and recitations were given by Mrs. Nurse. Progressive games, musical chairs, &amp;c., were played. Refreshments were served during the interval. The accompanists were Mrs. Cottrell, Mrs. W. J. Bate, and Mr. Wads- worth.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Miss H. Bennett.—A social evening qrganised by the past and present scholars of the Senior Girls' Class Of the Pad- gate Wesleyan Sunday School was held on Saturday evening in the Schoolroom. As a mark of respect and appreciation of services rendered, a presentation was made during the evening to Miss H. Bennett, Bruche Heath, who has resigned her position as teacher of the class, having been connected with Sunday school work at Padgate as scholar and teacher practically all her life. The presentation took the form of a silver inkstand, pen and nickel reading lamp. Mrs. Charles Healy, one of the' old members of the class, who handed over the gift, spoke of the length and kind of service which Miss Bennett had rendered and ex- pressed regret that she had been compelled to relinquish her position and wished that the resignation had only been temporary. Miss Bennett very suitably replied, expressing her appreciation of the kindly thought which had led the members to -.make her such a useful present. The rest of, the evening's proceedings, which were of a social nature, were presided over by Mrs. Dakin. The soloists were Miss B. London, Miss N. Appleton, Mrs. W. J. Bate and Mrs. Dakin, and recitations were given by Miss E. Holt and Miss H. Thring. Games were also played during the. evening and refresh- ments were provided.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Parish Council.—Mr. W. Mclntyre, chairman, presided at Tuesday's meeting a the Stockton Heath Parish. Council. The customary pay- ments were authorised, and no matter of unusual interest appeared on the agenda.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Warrington And The Disposal Of The Village Refuse.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Pilling Presided at the meeting of Stockton Heath Parochill Committee on Tues- day. Mr. Hough (clerk) reported in connection with the disposal of night refuse that he had communicated with the Town Clerk of War- rington asking if the Corporation would take the six loads weekly as suggested. From an in- timation he got from the Deputy Town Clerk he. understood that the Corporation would try to do what was asked. The contractor had told him that up to the present they had been taking ten. Mr. 'Westbrook thought' this very satisfactory, and moved that the Corporation be_thanked for their consideration.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pearson seconded, and the Chairman re- marked that he had great pleasure in putting the resolution to the meeting.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hough was requested to convey the reso- lution to the Town Clerk.</p>
        <p>LORD FRENC:i'S PRAISE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>Stockton Heath And Grappen-Jiall Volunteers inspected.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath And Grappen- Jiall Volunteers inspected.</p>
        <p>„ The Stockton Heath and GrappenbAn De- tachments of the V.T.C, being the 4th Com- pany of the 6th Battalion (Knutsford) of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment, attended the inspection by Lord French at Chester on Wednesday afternoon. _</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath Detachment numbered about 80 and were under the command of Platoon Commander F. Warburton; while- the Grappenhali contingent, under Platoon Com- mander F. A. Frost, paraded about 20! strong. Leaving Warrington by the 4.22 p.m.. train, the company, on arrival- in Chester,. marched from the general station to the Town Hall amd formed up on the roadway in line With the other detachments of the regiment. Lord French arrived at 6.30; and, having inspected the regiment, the Company 'Commanders were called out and the Field Marshal addressed a few congratulatory remarks, on their smart ap- pearance,. speaking in almost the same terms. as he did at Liverpool. The regiment then marched past in companies in column- of route and the parade terminated. The day was fine and bracing and the men enjoyed themselves very much. - The local contingents returned to Warrington at 8.15.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing Ist Octotier : Monday.—Company and Recruit Drill.; also instruction in the "Care of Arms," Headquar- ters, 7.45.</p>
        <p>Wednesday.--Afeeting of N.C.O.'s,. Mulberry Tree, 8 o'clock.</p>
        <p>Thursday.—Recruit Drill, 7.454. Headquarters. Friday.—Class Firing for members who have not yet taken their targets, Drill Hall, 7 o'clock.</p>
        <p>FRANK WARBITRTON, Officer Commanding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. Geerge Davies.—On Tuesday another well-known member of the community passed away, after a very short illness, in the person of Mr. George Davies, Warrington-road. In February he attained his seventieth birth- day, but has not lived long enough to receive his national allowance. Originally a file cutter, he at various times followed several careers. He possessed the - long service medal of the old Volunteers, which he joined in Warrington very shortly after their inception. As an ardent member of that force he won badges and dis- tinctions, worthy mementoes of a worthy object. He was also a well-known bass singer, having sung in various churches in town before he removed to the Sankey and Penketh district, where he became a prominent member of the Sankey Church Choir, which position he held for nearly thirty years. The Musical Society also claimed him as a member. For the last twenty-five years he has been official lamp- lighter, walking fourteen miles each day, seven miles lighting and seven turning out.. A widow and four daughters are left to mourn his loss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>ROSES AND WAR..</head>
        <p>ROSES AND WAR..</p>
        <p>Raising Funds For The District Working Party.</p>
        <p>The Penketh Rose Society recently held a rose show for the purpose of raising money for the War Relief Fund. The attempt has succeeded wonderfully well, for 21 18s. has been handed over to the secretary of the Penketh and Sankey Working Party. The winners at the rose show were Mr. J. Ray; 2, Mr. J. Appleton; 3, Mr. J. Morris; 1, Mr. J. Davies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>Work The Pai-ty Is Doing.</head>
        <p>Work The Pai-ty Is Doing.</p>
        <p>The following is a copy of a letter from the Hon. Treasurer of the Working Party, sent to the employes of the Penketh Tanning C0.:.-" I have to-night received your note and cheque for £lO for the funds of the Penketh and Sankey Working Party, for which I enclose receipt. it is very generous of the employes to help us so much. As far as possible parcels are sent to every man that goes from the village. If any are missed out it is simply that their names are not given in. The women members of the working party have been asked to bring names and addresses to the Mission on Thursdays. We have now sent 196 parcels to men. In many cases more socks have been Aent to village men who require them. I enclose a balance sheet. Full particulars of accounts can be seen at the Mission on the Thursday we meet, also a list of the boys to whom parcels have been sent. If you would like the list I could send it by Mr. Woods; then if you found any boys had -been left out it ;could be rectified. We have also pent a self-propelling chair to Winwick. Of colirse, at present the hospitals get a great amount of ciur work. If anyone who visits WinwiCk cares to look on the back of the chairs they will find one with a brass tablet on, saying it is from the Penketh and Sankey Working Party. Each chair costs £4 Bs. pd., and that was collected in small amounts from the workers. Thanking you and the employes for their generous support of our work.—Yours truly, Harriet Gilbert, hon. treasurer."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>WORKHOUSE AS MILITARY HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>WORKHOUSE AS MILITARY HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Some Handsome Gifts.</p>
        <p>Seventy-two soldiers are expected to arrive bn Tuesday at the Runcorn Workhouse, part of which has been converted into a military hos- pital. Already several handsome gifts have been made to the hospital, but there is, of Course, room - for others. Lady Harmood Banner, of Aston Hall, has presented a Broadwood concert grand 'pianoforte, Lieutenant C. E. Linaker a threeqyarter size billiard table and accessories and gramophone records, and Colonel Talbot, of Aston Lodge, a gramophone. Mr. L. Ratcliffe; the officer in charge, ex- presses the hope that the public will see that the soldiers are plenWully supplied with to- bacco, cigarettes and pipes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>RAILWAY DISPUTE.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY DISPUTE.</p>
        <p>War Bonus Increased By Five Shillings.</p>
        <p>An agreement has been reached on the ques- tion of the railwaymen's demand ,for an in- crease of wages to meet the increased cost of living. The basis of the agreement is that the previous war bonus of ss. for employes of 18 and upwards is to be increased to 10s., ar d that an extra 2s. 6d. is given to those whose previous bonus was 2s. 6d.—employes under 18, with cer- tain exceptions.</p>
        <p>PICTURISED RO. ADAPTED FROM THE SENSAT BY ALE C</p>
        <p>Insets:sin this story are purely imaginary living</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XVLII.</p>
        <p>Waiting. in, the row-boat, Quabba heard Esther's scream, as she lumped from the yacht to avoid Blair Stanleigh. As she came to the surface the girl. grasped the rail of the boat, and Quabba drew her in and. headed the boat straight for the beach. On. deck Blair and Luke were fighting viciously,. It was a splendid, smashing scrap, and the sailors thoroughly enjoyed, the spectacle. Like maddened beasts the two men tore at each other, and the snowy deck of the yacht was stained with blood. Against the panelled mahogany of the cabin side the fighters crashed, smashing and splintering glass and woodwork_ And then, in sheer tearing rage, combining all their strength to dash each other to insensibility and death, they 'plunged from the smashed. cabin side at the centre to the stanchion rail. At the heavy impact of the two strong bodies:flung against it the rail bent outward and the stanchions snapped like pipe-stems, and the two. men locked in death grips-, went over the side and. into the water, kicking, thrusting, cursing. In the watbr, the more activetßlair loosened hold and dived under. Coming to the surface, he waited for the giPAY's head to appear; and when Luke rose to the surfate, bubbling and gasping for breath, Blair struck him a smashing blow between the eyes and swam to the companion-way. Half- stunned.. and half-strangled, Luke screamed in inarticulate insanity of rage and hate. Then through his dull mind came a realisation that Blair, as friend of the owner of the yacht and as "a gentleman," would have the aid and assistance of the sailors. Leaving the matter for another day to settle, ,for Luke was of a patine that loved to nurse and cherish his enmity,. the gipsy turned and swam strondly and doggedly to the shore.</p>
        <p>Blair,. panting, water: soaked, and dishevelled, leaned over the rope railing of the companion-way and looked after Luke, also with that respect one good fighting man has for another. "Hurt, sir?" a sailor asked. "Only a little scratched," said Blair, noting with some satisfaction that the cold seawater had checked the bleeding of his wounds.</p>
        <p>"The. poor fellow must have gone roaring mad," added Blair in explanation. "We had been lost in the woods for some days, and he must have had some whisky and gone clean crazy, for the first thing I 4 knew he sprang upon me, fighting like a mad dog!"</p>
        <p>It was this same explanation that Blair made to the sailing master when the latter returned in the launch with the sailors from their shore leave. For the two sailors haft in the forecastle had not seen or known of Esther's boarding the yacht in search of Arthur, or her wild leap from it when faced by Blair and Luke, nor had the sailing master known of her presence aboard the yacht. The sailing master had seen the row-boat with Quabba and Esther headed straight for the beach, but the row- boat was too far away for even keen sailor eyes to note that the girl was wet and weak. "I have heard of sailors getting homicidal d.t.'s," said the sailing master, "but I never knew a landsman to get 'em that quick." The two sailors who had witnessed the Titan combat grinned, for it had been a bout to delight the eye. But just then the sailing master gave a sudden cry. "Look at that fool! What is he doing? Trying to commit suicide?" and the master pointed to the ,beach, where the row-boat, with the tired, unskilled hands of Quabba on the oars, was negotiating the surf.</p>
        <p>"Shall we lower a boat?" asked one of the sailors. "There was a woman spilled from the boat with that awkward land lubber !" The sailing master shaded his eyes with his hand and said, "By George, the girl can swim all right She is even helping the land lubber. They're all right. See, the fellow with her is ask- ing someone to save the boat. There is quite a little bunch of people on the beach now." Not only were Esther and Quabba safe ashore, but Luke had gained a deserted part of the beach unobserved, shook the water from him, and strode off.</p>
        <p>Luke Lovell had no use for the town. Far away in the Sierras the Lady Veronica mines and other prosperous and busy diggings called him. By train and on foot he made his way, and by another day Santa Barbara was far behind him. But though he went for work, and to get away from his very hate of Blair Stanleigh, he had not deviated one iota from his set, sullen purpose. He had seen the paper that Blair Stanleigh had pur- sued and persecuted Esther to obtan. Then, too, Luke had beheld The Diamond from the Sky. Luke clenched his hand and looked drown as though he expected to see the great gem he had thrice grasped as his own, only to lose, still burning bale- fully in his bronzed and brawny grasp. But the Diamond was gone, and he clutched at the empty air and trudged on, resolved that the purpose of his life would be to have it. But he would have no more of Blair Stanleigh; that he was resolved. Away at the Lady Veronica Mine there was ex- citement. The driver of the trap had been suc- coured by ranchers. Brought back, he told the " boss " of the attack made upon them. The fore- man acted at once. The girl had come to the mines to find John Powell, and twice had been attacked. "The master must know of this," he said to his wife, and he used the long-distance telephone to get into communication with Arthur. oxe)neceneeeece</p>
        <p>)(tV.,)I</p>
        <p>ROY LIVECOMEIL ,ONAL "FLYING A- SERIAL FILM; J. BRAID.</p>
        <p>and have no connection with Dr relation to arti person.]</p>
        <p>He apoldgised for the expense and hoped he had done right.</p>
        <p>"Yes; you did right, McKenzie, to call me UP and tell me! I'll give you ten thousand dollars if the young lady is all right. Stop all work, arm all the men, and search everywhere till the girl ia found. Don't stop to ask any questions, and it the men are caught who molested her string them up or shoot them down—or both! I'm taking the fastest car and I'm making for the mines !" At his office Arthur dashed like a madman frora- the telephone, crying excitedly to his secretary he went that a most urgent matter called him to, the mines, and ran from the building to his wait- ing motor car.</p>
        <p>Scarcely had he left when a wire was received ab the office from Esther: "Came here to see you on urgent business. Wire instructions." The only thing the secretary could do was to wire that Mr. Powell had left for the mines seeking her. There was nothing now to be done, but return to, the mines, and Esther and Quabba too train to the nearest station and engaged horses for the remainder of the journed.</p>
        <p>It was pay-day at Mammoth, a noisy mining settlement. Mr. Huff was the proprietor of " Pete's Palace," the one amusement and 'public acooninlo- dation enterprise. It was a dance hall, a bar, and a restaurant combined. Money flowed like water at Mammoth, which was in the centre of the newly-opened mines in the mountains, where johu Powell and other magnates were putting in more money than perhaps they ever would get out. But .things were at a lull when Frank Durand, "King of Diamonds," arrived. He had received word from some underground source that several of the train robbers who had escaped after the hold-up in which The Diamond from the Sky was taken from a woman passenger—Vivian—consorted at " Pete's Palace" at Mammoth. A few signs in the under-world fraternal code and Pete and Durand were in conference. A few Words over- heard by a hulking zipsy fellow at the bar, and third person was party to the conversation. This person was Luke Lovell, who, retracing his stePs in search of • work, had encountered at Mammoth the wandering desert Indian. In him Luke had proof to substantiate to Durand his story of the diamond.</p>
        <p>"It's at the bottom of the sea," concluded Luke, gruffly. •</p>
        <p>Mammoth would seem to be the magnet that drew together many concerned in this strange story. From two worn horses alighted Esther aIICI' Quabba, tired and hungry. " Pete's Palace" No° getting crowded, but it was the only place in! miles that offered refreshment for man and beaa'.. Arthur, speeding through the wilderness in 1119- high-powered car, that went direct, leaving train systems far behind, had picked up a strange wan— derer in the wilds, an exhausted English lawYer' carrying a deer head by a strap-handle, and; ton tired to talk.</p>
        <p>" Pete's Palace" filled up, the dance was at its height. Arthur, who, as employer, had. furnisbed. most of the money that was being spent in "Pete-a Palace," arrived with the tired lawyer for refresh-. ments ere speeding on in his feverish search for Esther. He had not raised his eyes at the door when there was an outbreak at the counter that. attracted the attention of all. A man, who, was a' beast had grasped the shrinking Esther:, saYit'g : "Come and dance." As he clutched her the neck' of Esther's blouse was loosened, and The Diamond; from the Sky dangled blazing in the fierce light, of, the great lamps overhead. Durand saw it.;; Luke' Lovell, who had stepped aside at seeing Esther,. saw it, too.</p>
        <p>There was a wild uproar, and Pete, with 1119- ruffianly', help, sided with Durand in the bold clBln3. that the latter made for the diamond. It via! torn from the neck of Esther, and, in the grasp Peter Huff, some secret spring was touched; the back of the locket opened, and a creased PaPer fell to the floor.</p>
        <p>A woman known as "Kansas Kate" seized and read he paper: "My child, not a diamond but loving mother's prayer is the true 'charm against harm.'" Even a life such as the woman wee leading could not stamp out the desire to protect the innocent and motherless. Great tears coursed down her cheeks as, turning in fury upon Pete' she said, "This is a mother's prayer. The locket is that poor girl's." As though this were a signal at which all demoniac passions were to break loose, the dance hall viragos, in an outburst of emotion and frenzied anger, sprang upon the proprietor of the place end all his bullies, scratching and biting. For one brief moment Arthur and Esther gazed into each other's eye, and for the first and alas! for the last time fOr many bitter days, were clasped each other's arms. And then pandemonium reigneal Instinctively all took sides, the good elemellk against the bad, and even the better element ef the bad against the worst. Esther and Artlitir were torn apart; men and women fought like maniacs with hands and feet and teeth; eve"' article that could be lifted and smashed was throw or struck with. The lunch counter went over Nvi,th a crash, things toppled and fell, and then the surge of the crowd, as a wave of ,the combat drove it to one side, tore out the west wall of the rickety frame building, and down smashed 01° roof, crushing all, and beneath it was Arthur Stanleigh. shattered to insensibility. But where was The Diamond from the Sky? (To be continued.)</p>
