      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>FOOTBALL FIELD.</head>
        <p>THE FOOTBALL FIELD.</p>
        <p>•:. •:. .:. • .:. tt• 4,...,,,..:-:".:..:-:-.)-:=.).:••:•-:-:-:-:-:-:.,:.•:•.:-:.4.:.•:-:••:-:.-:-.:.-4:-.54...:44.:...:-:.•:-.:-:...).:-:- .4)</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>missing passes he was as much annoyed as the spectators; he looked awfully embarrassed and vexed when he spoiled a fine movement from the right by dropping a clean pass from Tranter. ,</p>
        <p>(Lancashire Section.)</p>
        <p>TWENTY POINTS 10 NIL.</p>
        <p>`^ The Linnets" Have Their Feathers Clipped At •Wilderspool. Warrington soundly drubbed Runcorn at Wilderspool ,by beating them by 20 points to nothing. This was how the score mounted pp: First Half: Tries. Goals. Jylley - Baker</p>
        <p>Harmer Baker Second Half:</p>
        <p>T ranter Jolley Douglas Tranter</p>
        <p>Dumbell 0 Dumbell ... 1 Matthews ... 0 Dumbell 0</p>
        <p>Quite an interesting and convincing list!</p>
        <p>• When the teams trickled on to the field the crowd of about 2,000 took the measure of °them, and Warrington's advantage was • apparent. Most et the Runcorn players were very youth- ful, ,like raw recruits, and the only old stal- warts among them were J. Jolley, Okell, 'Richa,rdsort and Burgess. There was a vast difference between the Runcorn team and that which was graced by Knoxy Houghton and Jimmy Jolley. On the other hand, Warring- ton were in the happy position of blending well and truly tried and young players, and the com- bination was effective. Knowing the, quality they were 'up against, they forced the game greatly in the first half, with the result that in the second half not a few of the visitors suffered from weariness of physique and, as the impos- sibility of their winning became more pro- nounced, weariness of spirit. The interval also was too long and, on a hot day, this always tends to produce lassitude. Xhey were handi- capped, moreover, by the leave-taking of Nickson, who, when tackling Jolley, sustained a bad cut on the left eye.</p>
        <p>'One was glad to mitice that the taking of goal-kicks was not confined to Jolley, for in seasons when we have had one wan repeatedly taking them ' and, When he has not been playing, there was no one else. who could act as a good substitute. The development of this branch ,pf the art generally is all to the good, and, judging 13 the shots. on Saturday, is much needed, for only one goal was scored, though, of course, most of the attempts were made from difficult angles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <p>The forwards were very good, and hung to- gether well. Matched against them, however, was not much weight, and they indulged in some rushes which reminded one of the days of the Shugars—Boardman--Thomas combina- tion. These onslaughts are worth cultivating. In this department there is no doubt that War- rington is well served.</p>
        <p>-- Coming to the half-backs, Douglas and Callon, though they played hard, were not quite up to Young and Nicholas in the match against Broughton, and one Was pleased to observe a smartening up of the game after the interval, when Young changed places with Douglas. ,Immediately the former took up position at half the game moved much more quickly. If Young has to "join up" it will be a serious loss to Warrington, because he is far and away the " classiest " man inothe team.</p>
        <p>• Among the three-quarters Baker was at his best, and it was rather a pity that the move- ment which started from the half-way line and culminated in his running round the post should have been negatived through an obstruction by one of the forwards and, though it was not appreciated by the majority of the crowd, one -• may say that it was a legitimate decision. His would have 'been a glorious first try but for that. It is as well that spectators should be reminded that there are laws of the game. Tranter was again a tower of strength to the team—his flutter with Salford has done him no' harm! A pretty swift and strong runner— Euston to Glasgow express style, tearing up the Permanent way—his chief asset is his brain. Re has a mind for football. -Re can take in a situation at a glance, and makes tracts for deal- ing with it while others are merely thinking •••-• over it. This showed up particularly on one occasion, when Runcorn threatened danger. } Four of the visitors were racing along and pass- ing excellently. Seeing them, Tranter set hin- self to break up the movement by getting in such a position that the man with the ball had either to part with it and lose it as well as his mates, or, if he stuck to it, remain within easy reach of Tranter. The ball wa loosed and Tranter scooped it up and danger was averted. One recollects that Tranter was introduced to ',Warrington -as a forward, but his initiative so distinguished him that the committee were soon persuaded to shift him to the' three-quarter line. In the forward line he was good stuff wasted, although he had always played in that position in the works' competition. A strong lad, he has a terrific and irresistible rush with him—a "Tank," as it were.</p>
        <p>It was Britton's first spell with Warrington, and, obviously, the lad was nervous and, to make matters worse for him, he did not receive much sympathy and encouragement from the spectators. It does not aid a young .player tf) hear shouts of, "Push him in the pack." It 9ight have been better for him had, he been flaying away .first, because, at home, the spec- tators watch every movement and are quick to detect error. But under the circumstances, Britton did well. A sure judgment of his ability is impossible from orie match, brit one thinks him worthy of an extended trial. At</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>INCIDENTS OF TRE MATCH,</head>
        <p>INCIDENTS OF TRE MATCH,</p>
        <p>Coming to the details of the game, Runcorn won the toss and set the home team to play against a strong wind and sun. Play became furious and on the centre line Harmer had to make his mark to stem a great rush by the whole of the Runcorn forwards. Warrington forged ahead against the wind, and frbm a scrummage Young got possession and raced through several men, but spoiled a good chance by a forward pass. Tranter getting the ball was within a few strides of passing the Runcorn line, but Jack Jolley brought his man down and saved grandly. Warrington still pressed and Matthews, Dumbell and Ward looked like scoring, but Flanagan spoilt their efforts on the last lap and touched down: Runcorn cot the ball away and were travelling nicely when an infringement of rules gave Warrington a free-kick at the half-way line and the game once more raged under the visitors' cross-bar. A good kick by Jolley relieved the pressure .and midfield play followed, both sides showing sterling form. NicksOn and Hailwood for Runcorn were very keen and took a deal of watch- ing to prevent a break through. Okell and Rich- ardson were next prominent for Runcorn and the Warrington line was for the first time in danger. The ball was kicked away, but Runcorn returned and kept Warrington on the defence..; Baker got the ball and was on the point of scoring, but Glegg obstructed and the chance was missed. Ben Jolley was called on to clear his lines, which he did • handsomely, and al scrummage took place in Runcorn territory, the whole of the forwards endeavouring to carry the ball over. A free-kick for off-side against the home lot brought relief, but F. Jolley, the Runcorn back, failing to gather the ball a minute later, Baker and Britton rushed for the line and looked like getting over safely, when Burgess rushed up and just got the ball into touch at the corner. The home Jolley tried to get through, but was grounded. Still the play hovered about the Runcorn goal. Jolley and then Tranter tried this forcing game, but Runcorn were not to be had and tackled so keenly that individual play did not succeed. Jolley then tried other tactics, making his mark near the centre line and finding touch well into the Runcorn half. Runcorn came again and Lightfoot nearly got through, Ben Jolley checking him in good time. A grand passing movement by the whole Warriugton quartet of threequarters was spoiled by Britton passing forward. Harmer scored the first try for Warrington during e melee on the Runcorn line, Jolley failing to add the goal. Just 'on half-time F. Jolley missed his kick, and Baker, taking prompt advantage, scored. Jolley again missed at goal. Half-time: Warrington, 2 tries (6 points); Run- corn, nil. Nickson, who had cut his face in collision with the home full-back, did not turn out again, and Runcorn, handicapped by his absence, the wind, and the sun, had but a poor prospect. Warring- ton were almost immediately pressing and Young made a bid for the line, but was brought down. In less than five minutes Ben Jolley took the ball past several opponents, passed to Tranter, and the latter had little difficulty in crossing the line, thus scoring try number three. Dumbell took the kick, but missed the goal. Ben Jolley tried to score once again and this time succeeded in such an easy position that Dumbell easily added the goal. After that Douglas and Tranter added unconverted tries in capital style. Result :—Warrington, 1 goal 6 tries (20 points); Runcorn, nil. The teams were :</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON: B. Jolley; Baker, Tranter, Britton, Young; Douglas, Callon; Harmer, Cunliffe, Glegg, Matthews, Dumbell, Ward. RUNCORN: F. Jolley; Walker, Flanagan, Light- foot, J. Jolley; Peacock, Okell; Richardson, Bur- gess, Spruce, Booth, Hailwood, Nickson. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
