      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>VISIT TO WIDNES TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>VISIT TO WIDNES TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Warrington play their second away match of ,the season at Widnes to-day, and there is every pros- pect of victory: The team, which will leave on the 2.10 train from Bank Quay Station, will be selected from: Jolley; Mills, Britton, Callon, Tranter and Baker; Nicholas, Young and Douglas; Harmer, Green, Cunliffe, Matthews, G-legg, Jones, Ward and Dumbell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>PAROCHIALS' EASY A\ IN.</p>
        <p>Beamont and Parochial met on the Rylands's ground on Saturday, before a fair number of spectators. Only about half-a-dozen of the selected Beamont players put in an appearance, and the team was outplayed almost from start to finish, the final score being 17 poinis to nil in the Parochials' favour. The Parbchial lads were stronger in every department and made the mist of their opportunities.</p>
        <p>MATCH FOR TO-DAY. Whitecross v. St. Thomas', at Wilderspool; kick- off, 3.30; referee, Mr. J. Flynn.</p>
        <p>LEAGUE MEETING. The League meeting will he held on Monday next, the 2nd Prox.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>IMMORTAL JACK FISH.</head>
        <p>THE IMMORTAL JACK FISH.</p>
        <p>" ' Wirepuller ' writes me," says Forward in the "Athletic News," "from a training camp, and asks me to assist him in providing a few facts for a discussion. The point at issue is the merits of John Fish and the late James Leytham. A North-Country soldier in the tent declared that the Wigan player was 'the finest footballer who ever Played 'for the Northern Union.' 'Wire- puller ' naturally put forth the claims of 'Jack ' Fish, of Warrington. "Without in any way favouring one side or the other, I might say that both were great players, each with a distinct but none the less effective style of his own. However, ' Wirepuller ' wants some Nets about Fish, so here they are. "The' Warrington man's best season was in 1900-1, when for his club he scored 167 points was, for his county 43. His best county score that season was three goals and five tries against Northumberland and Durham. Several times Fish scored five tries in one match for Warrington, and his club record was eight goals and five tries (31 points) for Warrington against Huddersfield in a Northern League match' on October 20th, 1906.</p>
        <p>_ I may tell my correspondent that, in addition to his football skill, Fish was a fine runner, and that in one season in track racily-, he took £57 in prizes. The N.C.A.A. Suspended him as an ama- teur because of his connection with Northern Union football."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <p>Another War Economy. Old Gentleman (to boy, with back on the ground and legs in the air): "Don't you think you might hurt yourself (King that?" Small, Boy (making frantic effort to stand on his head): " Yessir—but—mklyver—told me—to play at somefink—what don't wear my boots out."—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
