      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 30, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 30, 1916.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>(Lancashire Combination.)</p>
        <p>THE TOWN'S REVENGE.</p>
        <p>South Beaten By One Goal On o*n</p>
        <p>Ground.</p>
        <p>As the Town had visited the Dingle Park 'enclosure the previous Saturday, and remem- bering the exciting game then witnessed which resulted in a victory for South Liverpool by the only goal of the match, their meeting on Satur- day was looked forward to with great interest. There did not appear, however, any likelihood of the South meeting with a reverse, but the Town players were full of confidence, ankl won by two goals to one.</p>
        <p>South Liverpool fielded ,practically the same side as on the previous Saturday; but the Town made a few changes, Whitfield, Haywood and Stephens being included. Warrington lost the toss and started down hill with plenty of dash, and it was arly in evidence that they meant to make certain of victory, and the front line ' being well supported by the halves, set up a violent bombardment, and had it not been for the soundness of the home defence the Town would have been through more than once in the early stages of the game. Some pretty open play followed, both sets of forwards taking part, and it was clearly a "forwards' game." The 'defence of both sides seemed unbeatable, and for a time play was in midfield, until Hay- wood, bluffing his Opponents, raced along, pass- ing to Owen, who centred and Aspinnll, beat- ing man after Man, put in a splendid shot, which gave Moore absolutely no chance.</p>
        <p>This roused the spirit of South Liverpool, and they swept to the attack with determination, but Leah and Millward were tackling strongly, and Kite was as safe as ever. The half-backs, more than holding th4ir own, returned the ball into Liverpool's quarters. Several shots were tried, but without result. The Town front line was a constant source of danger to the home defence, A tpinall especially playing a great game. Behind, the halves were a determined lot, Whitfield playing a "brainy " game, Hay- wood brilliant, with Stephens making. an effective debut.</p>
        <p>About 'five minutes from the interval the Town were again rewarded for their strenuous efforts, Aspinall again working his way through and giving his side the lead by two goals at the interval.</p>
        <p>The first-period of the game had provided a vigorous tussle, yet full of good football, the sides were equally balanced, the Town making the best of their opportunities and turning round with confidence commenced the second half up-hill arid facing a strong sun. South Liverpool at once made great strides for Kite. Time and again they did all they could to get through, but the Town were determined and always kept a good grip of their opponents, Ditchfield, as usual, working like a Trojan and very excellently trying some of his first-timers, but without success.</p>
        <p>Owen was twice put out of action and received first-aid—the second time after a fine run and a shot which deserved success, but which struck the upright awl went out of play. Taylor was exceeaingly weak, and requires training. Illidge seems to have got rid of his selfish methods, and a marked improvement was noticeablc. He is deserving of further encouragement. About fifteen minutes from time, after a scramble in front of the Town goal, South Liverpool scored, ,the rest of the play was up and down, the South making, every endeavour to find an equaliser, but the end of a great and exciting game saw the Town the victors after 90 minutes of thoroughly interesting football.</p>
        <p>It is something to boast of to gain a victory at Dingle Park. South Liverpool, when wtn- ning the toss, invariably play up hill first halt, and when thus favoured have never been beaten. Their suporters are eager for another game to be played at Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>ECCLES BOROUGH AT HOOD LANE TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>ECCLES BOROUGH AT HOOD LANE TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>After an absence of two weeks at Liverpool, War- rington Town are to play on their own ground at Hood-lane to-day (Saturday), their opponents being Eccles Borough. A good game is anticipated. Last season perhaps the best match at Hood-lane' was that between the Town and Eccles, when we were the victctrs by three goals to nothing—a triumph minimised by a subsequent defeat. of one gal to nothing at Eccles. A few weeks 'ago, it will be recollected, Leigh, of Eccles, was a wing man with Siddall for Warrington against Tranmere Rovers. but his own team. Eccles, have claimed him, and to-day he will be among the opposition. Last season Siddall played for them, and it will be par- ticularly interesting to-day to watch his play. Warrington's team will be selected from: Kite; Leah, Millward ; Stephens. Whitfield, Ditchfield: Illidge, Aspinall, Kendrick, Siddall, Giblin and Owen.</p>
        <p>Heywood, who played for the Town last week, has gone to the Front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. F. A. PTS. Howley St. Elphin's Bewsey A.S. Penketh juniors Latchford Recs. Orford Juniors .1 Highfield Juniors Latchford Cadets</p>
        <p>HIGHFIELD JUNIORS v. BEWSEY A.S.</p>
        <p>On the around of the former club.</p>
        <p>Highfield : J. Thornhill; Worrall, Jepson; Wish- lade. Hankey, Barrow; Dunne, Moulsdale, Bell, Ford, Tomlinson. Bewsey A.S. : Perks; Atkinson, Main; Worrall, James, Dickinson; Hassall, Roylance, Payton, Bradburn, Schofield. Referee, Mr. P. Gosling.</p>
        <p>The visitors won the toss and played with a good breeze at their backs. Bewsey were seen to the best advantage, and after ten minutes' play they opened the score. The home forwards played more keenly, but were opposed by a stubborn defence and play was transferred to the home quarters; and after Thornhill and his backs had been (severely tested they again had to yield their fortress to the visitors. In spite of these reverses the home team play,' very pluckily and on several occasions were unfortunate with good shots that were a shade too wide or too high. The visitors scored again before the interval, when the score was :—Bewsey. 3 goals; Highfield, 0. The home team now having the wind in their favour were fully expected to decrease their arrears, and they at once set a fast pace and put the visitors on the defensive. There was no holding the home forwards and Bell scored after five minutes' play. Moulsdale scored a second goal, and five minutes later Ford made the scores equal. Now came the tug-of-war for the winning point, and both teams played strenuously. Each end was quickly visited and the backs were de- fending strongly under pressure, but the N; isitors I appeared to have the better staying poweri, and during an attack a few minutes before the dose of play the home backs blundered and let in thei visitors' forwards. They made the best use of the opening and gained' the winning goal. 'Result: Bewsey A.S., 4 goals; Highfield, 3 goals.</p>
        <p>PENKETH JUNIORS v. ORFORD JUNIORS. On the ground of the former. An early visit was paid to the visitors' defensive lines. The visitors retaliated, but found the , home defence too gopd. The game Continued to be 'evenly contested, and each end was visited 'without result, and at half- time no score had been registered. The second half opened very vigorously, with the home team more in evidence, and, owing to a mis- take by the' visitors' defenders, Pearson broke through and scored for the home team. The visitors replied strenuously,- but failed to break down the home defence. Moss and Thornasson displaying very good form. No further scoring took place, and the, home team gained a hard-earned victory by one goal to nil.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD CADETS V. LATCHFORD RECS.</p>
        <p>These keen rivals met in their first League en- gagement, in Victoria 'Park, before a very fair crowd.</p>
        <p>Cadets: Greenhalgh; Jbnes, Williams; Shaw, Massey, Ashton; Bradshaw, Smith, Gibson, Porter, C aawrigh ,</p>
        <p>_ Recs. : Bancroft; Brandwood, Hubberstey; Faii - brother, Dutton, Cooper; HeesOm. Green, Will- cock, Trainor, Jams. 6Referee, Mr. J. Wright. The Cadets Won the toss and set their opponents to face the wind and 'sun. In. spite of these dis- advantages the, Recs. made the first attack, and Greenhalgh and his backs had to exert themselves 'to clear their lines. , After a brief visit to the ReNs.' goal the Cadets were again on the defensive and Willcock opened the score after fifteen min- utes' 'play. The teams appeared to be Very evenly matched 'arid keen play was witnessed. After several futile efforts by the Cadets to get on gqual terms the Recs. again attacked strongly and Green</p>
        <p>In the Junior League the- games last week provided some keen encounters, and victories were equally divided between the home and visiting clubs,' and ii—good crop of goals wa,. secured, the winners claiming thirteen and the losers six. .</p>
        <p>The game between the Highfield and Bewsey teams; who were opposed to each other for the first time in a League engagement, was very keenly contested, the home team being very eager to gain their initial points, but at the interval the visitors had gained a substantial lead .of three goals, and it looked very gloomy for the home supporters; but after the chang,, of ends the home players cheered their droop- ing spirits by playing in the very best style, and after they had scored their first goal the Bewsey defence ~vaS found plenty of work to do, and eventually the scores were made equal. It was at this, pciint that both teams played with fresh vigour, and the visitors proved dui stronger lot. During a stern attack on. the home goal the backs blundered and the visiters quickly availed themselves of the opening and snatched the victory in the closing...,minutes of the game.</p>
        <p>The Latchford Cadets and the Latchford Rees were also in opposition for the first time in League struggles, and the Cadets, who had pre- viously met with defeat' on their own ground, were.out to gain their first League victory, but they had built their castles in the air and their hopes were again Shattered, defeat being their share: Four•.goals were scored ' against their solitary one, and they now occupy bottom position with eighteen goals against and only two goals for.</p>
        <p>It is evident the Cadets require some shoo t- i ng practice.</p>
        <p>Bradshaw, on his first appearance, played very well, and scored the Cadets only goal. Greenhalgh and Massey also did \well. The Recs. were best served by Brandwood, Green, Willcock and Heesom.</p>
        <p>The game on the Penketh Rec. between the Juniors and Orford proved a very keen and 1.,r- -teresting contest, and the result being oxe goal against nil in favour of the home team was very satisfactory to the supporters of both clubs, of whom there was a ,very good number present. Throughout the ga'mo the, defenet, proved equal to all demands made by the opposing forwards, with the exception of a mis- take by the Orford backs,, which allowed Pear- son to nip in and snatch the only goal for the home club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>increased their lead. At the interval the score —Recs., 2; Cadets, 0. _</head>
        <p>increased their lead. At the interval the score —Recs., 2; Cadets, 0. _</p>
        <p>was</p>
        <p>.„ The Cadets gave a much-improved display on resuming and set up a strong attack on the Recs.' •goal, where Branclwood, Hubberstey and Bancroft showed fine form in defence and cleared their lines successfully. This was followed by an attack on the Cadets' stronghold, which had to capitulate to a splendid effort by Heesom. After this reverse the Cadets tried more open methods by long, swinging passes, which caused the Recs.' defenders to waver, and Bradshaw scored the Cadets'• first goal. Following this the Recs had the best of the exchanges and Green increased their lead before the call of time, the result being :—Latchford Recs., 4 goals; Latchford Cadets, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>HOWLEY UNITED v. ST. ELPHIN'S</head>
        <p>HOWLEY UNITED v. ST. ELPHIN'S</p>
        <p>The 'meeting of the above strong rivals in the Victoria Park wa the means *pi attracting a large crowd. . _</p>
        <p>Howley United: Walters; Foster, Rowlinson; Hoarty, Miller,- Atherton; Humphries, Woodhouse, Davies. Shalieshaft, Boyle. '</p>
        <p>St. Blp—hin's; Dor'rian'; Furness, Tunstall; Buck- ley, Miller, Cooper; Chisholm, England, Jones, Watson, Holden. Referee, Mr. S. C. Essenhigh. .</p>
        <p>The United won the toss and Jones set the ball rolling, and by strong play the Saints' goal was quickly placed in danger. Good play by Tunstall and Furness relieved the situation, and even play followed. Good dribbling by, Woodhouse in mid- field enabled the home forwards to lead an attack on the Saints' goal, and Shakeshaft, receiving a good pass from Boyle, opened the score for the United. The Saints straightaway made a keen assault on the United goal, a corner resulting. This was well placed by Chisholm, and Walters had to concede another corner to save his goal from being cap- tured. This time Holden placed well in front of goal, and Walters fisting out, Miller (Saints) re- ceived and returned the leather quickly and made the scores equal, Walters making a splendid effort. to clear. The game was now contested in strenuous fashion, and many fine struggles took place between Miller (United) and Jones, the Saints' centre-for- ward. Gradually the United gained the upper hand, and came to close grips with the Saints' defence. During a scramble Davies gained the lead for the United. Following this midfield play was the order until the Saints were able to resume the offensive by the aid of a free kick. Holden, re- ceiving the ball when well placed, shot hard at goal. and from Walter's clearance Jones secured and again made the scores equal. A little later Wood- house made several futile efforts to regain the lead, but the interval arrived with the scores being 2 goals each. The second portion was resumed in ding-dong style, and keen tackling was frequent, several players receiving minor injuries., Walters was suc- cessful in dealing with a hot shot from Jones, and then play was transferred to the Saints' goal and a penalty kick was awarded to the United for a trip by Tunstall. Davies was entrusted with the kick and placed the ball safely between the uprights, Dorrian being well beaten. The Saints now tried hard to equalise again, but Miller, at centre-half for the United, was playing a strong game, and broke up many of the Saints' attacks. Play veered to the Saints' quarters and Shakeshaft centring well, Woodhouse placed the home team further ahead with a capital shot The Saints replied in spirited fashion, and Foster cleared well when hard pressed. Rowlinson had to retire for a while owing to an injury, and Woodhouse fell to the back position, and was prominent in defeating the visitors' fe;r- wards on many occasions. Chisholm scored for the Saints, but was ruled to be in an off-side position. The players now began to show signs of fatigue. The pace slowed down and Igo further goals were scored. Result: Howley rnited, 4 goals; St. Elphin's, 2 goals. For the Howley team, Walters in goal gave a good exhibition, and was ably assisted by. Foster and Rowlinson. Miller was' the best half-back on the field. Woodhouse was the outstanding forward on the day's play, arid was ably supported by Shakeshaft, Davies and Humphries. For the Saints, Tunstall and Furness kicked well when hard pressed, H. Miller being the best of a moderate middle line, and Jones, Holden and Eng- land were the pick of the forward line.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>MATCHES -FOR TO-DAY</head>
        <p>MATCHES -FOR TO-DAY</p>
        <p>Bewsey A.S. v. Orford Juniors; S. C. Essenhigh. Penketh Juniors v. Highfield Juniors; A. Holt.</p>
        <p>Latchford Recs. v. Rowley; Mr. Burgess.</p>
        <p>Latchford Cadets v. St. Elphin's; J. Tomlinson. The monthly meeting of the Junior League will be held on Monday night at the Peter-street Dining Rooms. There will be some interesting business brought up for approval, and a full attendance is expected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>JUNIOR LEAGUE NOTES.</head>
        <p>JUNIOR LEAGUE NOTES.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>SANKEY ATHLETIC v. EMMANUEL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>On the Atherton Quay ground, before a fair crowd.</p>
        <p>Sankey Athletic,: Gleave; Holden, Brinksman',. Gough, Shakeshaft, Worrall; Eccleston, E. Tay- lor; W. Taylor, Bedall, Kenyon. • Emmanuel Institute: Wright; Hallam, Hankin- son; Parr, Hardy, Ayres; Hughes, Knight, Ford, Stubbs, W., Eaton.. ..e. Referee, Mr. Rustidge. A fast pace was set by the visiting forwards, who rushed towards, the home goal. A long centre ,by Eaton placed the home goal in: danger, Gleave having to leav6 his rroal to kick away. A corner-kick to, the visitors proved ineffective and then end-to-end play followed. The hoine team improved and a 'good run- by Kenyon and Bedall resulted' in the former causing Wright to concede a 'corner-kick. This was well-placed, but the visi- tors', defenders were equal to the occasion and cleared. The home forwards returned to the attack and forced another cerner; this time Eccle- ston heading just wide, of .the upright. Play was now ()Way in the visitors', half and a fine .centre by Eccleston gave a fine opening 'to Bedall, which he failed to .accept. .The visitors warmed to their work, and by a spell of good combination between their forwards the home goal was placed in dan- ger. A strong shot by Hardy hit the cross-bar and from the rebound the home team gained relief. Some keen exchanges were witnessed and each end was visited in turn, both ,Gleave and Wright having some good shots to save: At length a: good centre by Eccleston was converted by E. Taylor scoring for the home team. The visitors played.strenuously to get on even terms, Ford and Knight being prominent with a good effort, which caused trouble to Holden and Brinks- man. The visitors still continued to be aggressive and- after good exchanges Stubbs equalised with a rasping shot. Half-time: One goal each. A muddle' in midfield enabled the visitors to make the first attack in the second half, and Holden and Cleave were lucky to clear. At the opposite end Wright saved smartly on two occa- sions, and Shakeshaft hit the cross-bar with a stinging shot. From the rebound Hankinson</p>
        <p>The results of the day's play were:— Second Round Winners.—J. Cooper's team, J Middleton's, R. Sunderland's, J. W. Plinston's, T Mannering's No. 1, H. Whitley's 43, Isaac's, and T Horrabin's.</p>
        <p>Third Round. H. Whitley's team v. J. Cooper's team. H. 'Waitley F. Casey J. Isherwood</p>
        <p>R. Sunderland's v. T. Mannering's No. 1. T. hayes R. Sunderland H. Taylor ...... . 6 W. Barnes</p>
        <p>T. Horrabin's V. J. W. Plinston's</p>
        <p>A. Ellison W. Thomason • l3 J. W. Plinston ...... 4 T. Horrabin</p>
        <p>J. Middleton's B. Isaac's, A. Glea,ve, jun. H. Cook' l3 T. Locker J. Middleton</p>
        <p>38 27 • Semi-Final. T. Mannering's No. 1 v. T. Horrabin's. A. Broady ' 13 A. Ellison D. Richardson W. Barnes</p>
        <p>H. Whitley's V. J. Middleton's</p>
        <p>H. Whitley</p>
        <p>13 A. Gleave, jun.</p>
        <p>F. Casey J. Isherwood</p>
        <p>Final. T. Mannering's No. .1 v. H. Whitley's. A. Broady L. 4,ichardson W. Barnes • 6 J. Isherwood</p>
        <p>Re-WY (5 up). T. Mannering,'s No. 1 v. EL Whitley's. A. Broady D. Richardson ...... 5 F. CaSey W. Barnes 15 8</p>
        <p>THE PRIZES PRESENTED.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of. the games 'Mrs. Frost pre- sented the prizes to the successful competitors.</p>
        <p>In asking her to do so, Mr. Locker thanked Mrs. Frost for attending, and said the games had been well fought, and he was sure the winners would treasure the prizes which they received at the hands of Mrs. Frost.</p>
        <p>Presenting the prizes, Mrs. Frost said she was proud to come there that afternoon. She had never been able to play bowls herself, but she thought she had now better begin to do so in her old age. (Laughter.) In congratulating the winning team and sympathising with the losers, sl3e said the games had been a pleasure to watch and she would like to give a prize herself next• year, if someone would only '" jog " her memory. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Mr. W. Murray proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Frost, who, he said, had so willingly come forward to perform that duty. She was a lady of energy and always did what sh‘ could for the general good. (Applause.) Mr. E. Wallington seconded, and Mrs. Frost suitably replied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <p>cleared and play, was transferred to the home end, where Gleave had to exert himself to save a dan- gerous shot. The respective goalkeepers had saved .several -rood shots and play was in midfield when, unfortunately, the ball was burst and the game had to be abandoned at a very interesting stage, with the score :—One n'oal each.</p>
        <p>MONKS, HALL &amp; CO. v. BRUNSWICK VILLA. Efforts are being made at the Monks, Hall &amp; Co.'s Works to form an Association team, and on Saturday they raised .a fairly good eleven and journeyed to Earlestown to play their first game, which was decided under very favourable condi- tions. For a while it was evenly contested, but he home team proved the better marksmen and *held a lead at the interval by four clear goals.</p>
        <p>The . second half was very similarly contested, the visitors being finally outclassed by seven clear •</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CENTRAL v. EEAMONT.</p>
        <p>The above clubs opened their fixtures with a friendly on Saturday, on the Steel-street ground. Both clubs were fairly represented. Beamont won the choice of ends and played with the sun and wind at their backs. Gradually the Central put Beamont on the defensive and after five min.utes' play Horrocks opened the score for them and a few minutes later the same playir added a second goal. A little later Horrocks outwitted the (lefence and completed the "hat trick." Beamont IIDW set up a strong attack and were awarded a penalty kick, from which Oxley scored, and Horrocks scored again for the Central immediately after. Hall-time: Central, 4; Beamont, 1. Aft:- changing ends the Central continued to have t.a came nearly all their own way arct was a continual attack on the Beamont goal. Further goals were scored by Glover (2), Emary, Hands, Horrocks and Davis, the Central being easy winners. Result :—Central, 10 goals; Bea- mont, 2 goals.</p>
        <p>CROSFIELD'S JUNIORS v. GRAPPENHALL JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>Played at Crosfield's Recreation Ground on Satur- day. Crosfield's won the toss, and Grappenhall set the ball rolling. From a mistake on the part of Hardy, the home full-back, the visitors scored. The home team then took the offensive, and from a fine centre by Bissell, Sudlow scored. Half-time arrived with the score even. The second half was well contested, and both teams failed to score again. Result: Crosfield's, 1 goal; Grappehhall, 1 goal.' For the home team Beswick, Mason and Bissell played a good game, while for the visitors Barton played excellently in goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
