      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>LADY JELLICOE.</head>
        <p>LADY JELLICOE.</p>
        <p>Father's Death Prevents Visit</p>
        <p>To Warrington.</p>
        <p>GENERAL REGRET AND SYMPATHY.</p>
        <p>Last week the town was thrilled by the an- nouncement that Lady Jellicoe was to visit Warrington yesterday in order to receive at the hands of the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) in the Parr Hall, the cheque for £2,160 raised at the Navy Day held in August for the benefit of Admiral jellicoe's fund. However, owing to the illness of her father, Sir Charles Caynr, Lady Jellicoe telegraphed on Tuesday cancel- ling the engagement, and her fears for his con- dition had melancholy confirmation on Thurs- day, when he passed away at his residence, Aberfoyle, Ayrshire. These announcements have caused widespread disappointment i regret and sympathy—dis- appointment at not being able to afford her ladyship hospitality, regret that her father should have been removed by death from a sphere of usefulness, and sympathy with her and the family in their bereavement. Expres- sive of these sentiments the Mayor (Alderiopan Peter Peacock), with his usual promptitude and consideration, hal. written Lady Jellicoe a letter on the town's behalf.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>Children See The Big Chcque.</head>
        <p>Children See The Big Chcque.</p>
        <p>News of her Ladyship's inability to be pre- sent was first received by telegram on Tuesday afternoon, and the Mayor immediately went t..) the Parr Hall .to acquaint the ,school children rehearsing for the demonstration arranged for last night. Their sorrow was generally mani- fest. Partly with the idea of mitigating it the Mayor showed them the big cheque which was to have been presented. It is drawn on the Warrington Branch of the Manchester and Liverpool District Banking Company, being made out by the Mayor to Admiral Sir John Jellicoe and "crossed." Its signatories are Harold Smith, president; Peter Peacock (Mayor), chairman and hon. treasurer; Nora Rylands, hon. secretary for the Warrington Navy Fund. The children having looked at it most interestingly-it was the biggest cheque they had ever seen—the Mayor placed it in hic-• wallet, and, returning to the Town Hall, put it safely away until further arrangements have been made for its presentation. When and how this will take place has not yet been decided, suggestions from Lady Jellicoe being awaited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>MILITARY TRIBUNALS.</head>
        <p>MILITARY TRIBUNALS.</p>
        <p>CLASS "W."</p>
        <p>Men Who Cannot Be "Placed"</p>
        <p>By Medical Board.</p>
        <p>Wednesday's Tribunal in the Town Hall was composed of Dr. Joseph (Deputy Mayor) in the chair, Alderman Tinnion (who subsequently presided), Mr. F. A. Frost and Mr. W. Brad- bury. Captain Hackett attended as military repre- gentative, Captain Mathams being at present on sick leave.</p>
        <p>The ingenuity of the Medical Board in classify- ing the fit and partially fit has apparently added a further class to their many differentia- tions.</p>
        <p>The Class is lettered "W," but what thb letter stands for is a mystery. Its meaning was made clear by the Military Representa- tive. It signifies the case of a man who is not utterly rejected by the Medical Board, and who is allowed to continue in civil life. He is not exactly discharged; and is still in the Army Reserve.</p>
        <p>A case maker on whose behalf application was made for exemption, came within this category, and the Tribunal had no alternative.</p>
        <p>Cotton Mill Employe. A young man, aged 18 years, employed at the Coekbedge Factory, applied for exemption. He had three brothers 'in the Army—one of whom had been discharged disabled, and three other brothers working at munitions. His father died last year and applicant had to support his mother and sister.—The application was re- fused, Alderman Tinnion remarking that the Tribunal thought a couple of the brothers could contribute to the home. Workhouse Porter. .The Warrington Board of Guardians appealed on behalf of the WorkhouSe porter, a married man aged 38 years, whose wife is also in the service of the Board. _ .</p>
        <p>' Mr. Bottomley, clerk to the Guardians, stated ' that while the man is porter at the Workhouse and deals with "casuals," &amp;c., he has other duties to perform. There is 'a military hospital iwith over 300 patients, and whenever any of these were going in or coming out of the hos- pital every day, they had to be booked. Ther. were only three men of military age in the • institution, one of whom was exempt. There were also some dangerous lunatics to deal with from time to time, and the Guardians found it very difficult to get an experienced man. ' The Chairman: Suppose the Tribunal are against you what will you do? ' Mr. Bottomley: We will have to do best we can. He further mentioned that the man had , only been passed for garrison duty. They would not have appealed at all if he had been passed for general service. The porter said it was not his wish to come before the Tribunal. He would prefer to join the Army, but the Guardians thought his ser- vices more useful there. • Mr. Bottomley, in reply to the Chairman, said the man's, wages would be made up. A month's exemption, was granted. • Exempted On Medical Grounds.</p>
        <p>A gas fitter employed by a public body appealed for exemSion on domestic grounds, and for medical reasons. He handed in a doctor's certificate to the Tribunal and re- marked that he had been passed on August 19th for Class A, general service. *The Chairman: liaa you that complaint at the time you were examined? Applieant: Yes; but I had been previously rejected. The Chairman: Did they see that certificate? Applicant: They examined me generally. Mr. Holloway: Was the examination so general that it was not particular? (Laughter.) Applicant: I don't know: it was not very long. He added that his medical man told him he would hare to undergo an operation for the complaint. Exemption was granted on medical grounds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>One of Two Brothers to Go.</head>
        <p>One of Two Brothers to Go.</p>
        <p>A furniture dealer and draper having two shops in Market-street, aged 37 years, married, applied for exemption. His brother is in part- nership with him, is 33 years, unmarried, and has been exempted. His exemption exnires in October.—The Chairman, in stating that the edse would be ailjourned for a week, said that applicant's brother, who had got exemption. was a single roan, alkl the, Tribunal were of opinion that the business -ould be managed by one of them. One would have to go, and the ease would be adjourned for a week in order to decide which should go.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>Nineteen-year-old Farm Hand.</head>
        <p>Nineteen-year-old Farm Hand.</p>
        <p>A farmer who knew that hi t 4 land is within the borough because he pays Corpotation rates, created much laughter by making the an- nouncement. But when he asked the Tribunal to exempt a teamsman nineteen years of age, from military service, the Court assumed an extremely serious aspect.—The application wa3 refused. Class " Bl."</p>
        <p>A' cabinetmaker, 34„ married, classed by the Medical Board as "El," and whose father told the Tribunal that he suffers from, chronic rheumatism, was given conditional exemption. He has two brothers in' the Army.—When asked M the man would be willing to join the V.T.C.. his father said that be was not only a Corporal in that body, but a. member of the Voluntary Fire Brigade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>Milk in the Co:oanut.</head>
        <p>The Milk in the Co:oanut.</p>
        <p>Australian Officer ( who has been watching the stormg of a hill by the Australians): "Ser- geant, call the signaller. 'There's a light flash- ing every now and again over on the hill there like a heliograph and I can't make head or tail of it." Sergeant: "Oh. that's all right, sir. It's probably only Bill Watts. He's mended the seat of his pants with a jam tin:"--" Punch."</p>
        <p>A Change of Air. Great Doctor: "Your wife, sir, needs a change of air." Mr. Tightwad: "Well, I'll get her an electric fan."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>MORE INDISPENSABLES.</head>
        <p>MORE INDISPENSABLES.</p>
        <p>Business Men's Pleas For Employes.</p>
        <p>Dr. Joseph (Deputy-Mayor), Alderman Tinnion, Messrs. W. Bradbury, F. A. Frost and J. Elwell. sat on Thursday afternoon at the Town Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>Butchers' Appeals.</head>
        <p>Butchers' Appeals.</p>
        <p>A wholesale butcher appealed on behalf of his head slaughterman, aged 23% years, and unmarried. His average kill every week was 50 beasts and 250 sheep and there- was only this man besides himself and a shopman. He could not get a slaughtermaii.—The Chairman said the Tribunal would consider their deci- sion and let him know.</p>
        <p>Another master butcher, doing an extensive business in Warrington'and Latchford, applied for exemption for two of his employes, who, in addition to working in the slaughter-house, look after branch establishments.—The Chair- man said the Tribunal thought that one of these men should go and would consider what arrangements could be made. Applicant would hear in due course what the arrangements were.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>Studying Wireless.</head>
        <p>Studying Wireless.</p>
        <p>A young man aged 22 years, employed by Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., stated in his appeal that he had only been passed for Section 2 of Class B—" Work in labour units or on garrison duty. or regimental out- door employment." Re was studying wireless telegraphy at Liverpool and his first examina- tion would come off in December and the final next February.—The Tribunal agreed to give him temporary exemption if he continued his studies, and when he had passed his examina- tions his services might be required in some department where he would be useful to the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>Machine Repairer Exempted.</head>
        <p>Machine Repairer Exempted.</p>
        <p>A. firm of velvet, cord and fustian manufac- turers, who were represented by Mr. Arthur Browne; solicitor, applied on behalf of a cutter and machine repairer, a married man, aged 35 years, Whose services were described, as in- dispensable. They have only one other male employe, and the .head • of the firm has two sons in the Army.—The Tribunal granted condi- tional exemption with the V.T.C. proviso.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>MARKET GARDENER'S COMPLAINT.</head>
        <p>MARKET GARDENER'S COMPLAINT.</p>
        <p>Conccientious Objector Gets His</p>
        <p>Job.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Mather presided at Wednesday's meeting of the Warrington Rural Military Tribunal.</p>
        <p>Before going into the list the Chairman read the following characteristic letter, received from a. Padgate market gardener': "Dear Sir,— I would like to mention a few facts as regards myself as being 'called upon on Monday next, 2nd October. When my brother appealed for me he appealed on behalf of me as a market gardener and as a tomato, cucumber and vege- table grower, I only got six weeks' exemption, final. There is- another man .who appealed at the other local Tribunal on conscientious grounds got off on condition that he gets work on a farm or market garden work: that is the only thing he can do that is of national im- portance; and this same 'man.has got my situa- tion, which I don't think is fair, and which I think the Tribunal will agree with me. I think a man who has been used to gardening and farm work ail! his life is of more national im- portanee than a man who has never done any before. It seems as if I have to go out and make room for a conscientious man, who is about- the same age as myself. He has only two children and I have five, and I am sorry 4 o say my wife is expecting the sixth shortly, and my wife is of a nervous disposition. I think. that if the Tribunal will consider .these facts they will agree with me that I would .be of more national importance, at home than the man, who has taken my situation. I think I have only done right in letting you know these facts."</p>
        <p>The Chairman said this man was before theta six or. ,eight weeks-ago—his brother appealed for him. The Tribunal said he Must go, and now it appeared that a man who had been be- fore another Tribunal and pleaded 'conseienze was told to find work on a farm and got this</p>
        <p>raan'4 place. , A. M. Mitchell: If it is as he states it is a most -unfair case. _ •</p>
        <p>Another Member: Perhaps the brother has taken this man on on purpose to make the case stronger. Mr. Ball asked if they were going to counter. balance their deeds and actions by th'e deeds and actions of another Tribunal.</p>
        <p>The ,Chairman: It is, in my opinion, far from reasonable.</p>
        <p>Rev. A. M. Mitchell: Surely a man with five children has no right to be sent out when a man with two children gets his place Mr. Backhouse said they had not seen the, certificate which was granted in the other man's case.</p>
        <p>Mr. Furness (military representative) said that a number of conscientious objectors were being dealt with by the Tribunal in Lo4don and given the option of going to farming work. , The Tribunal ultimately agreed that they could not interfere, and while expressing sym- pathy with the applicant, decided that the matter must take AS course.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <p>MEN'S WAGES FOR BOYS.</p>
        <p>Tanning Company's Appeal To</p>
        <p>Runcorn Rural Tribunal.</p>
        <p>An interesting statement with regard to the wages paid to munition workers was made at a meeting of the Runcorn Rural Tribunal, at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Thursday. Mr. C. E. Linaker, sen., presided. At the last sitting it was decided that all men aged 25 and under employed by the AStmbor Tanning Company should. present themselves for examination by, the Medical Board. Mr. Linaker, solicitor, now gave the .result of the examination. Four men had been - passed for Class A, one for Class C, and three for Class C (iii.).</p>
        <p>Mr. Moors, the works' manager, said that he employed boys instead of women. Mr. Antwis : What wages do you pay to boys?</p>
        <p>Mr. Moors reulied that boys of 16 were earn- ing from 198. 6d. to 255. per week, and youths of 18 from 425. to-455.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker submitted that the company had already released as many men as possible. Five of the men were exempted urrtil the Ist November, without leave to appeal, and the rest of the cases, numbering 34, were ad- journed for two months. Could He Get His Knee Up?</p>
        <p>Ekemption was sought on behalf of a Preston Brook teamsman,, whose employer put forward the view that he was of greater use in agri- cultural work than he would be in the Army. Exemption until Ist January.—The Chairman: Is he a member of the Volunteer Training Corps?—The Employer: No.—The Chairman: Well, then, he must join.—The Employer: I'm afraid he won't get his knee up very well after sowing wiieat.</p>
        <p>In Charge of Burial Ground.</p>
        <p>In the case of a sexton, caretaker of a chapel and insurance agent, living at Appleton, it was stated that the appellant had charge of a Nonconformist Burial Ground. It was the only Nonconformist Burial Ground just out- side Warrington and served a considerable area. Appellant, who was 35, had six children.— The appeal was disallowed.</p>
        <p>A Stockton Heath One-Man Business. Stockton Heath fish, fruit and vegetable salesman applied for exemption, through Mr. H. J. Westbrook, solicitor, Warrington. The man had been temporarily exempted to Ist September. He was 39 years of apt and had a family of two. The Medical Board lad passed him for garrison duty or in provisional units. He had no one to assist except his own son, aged 15. He put in 14 hours each day (except Sunday) and on Saturdays 18 hours. He did all the buying and his own carting and had to cut the growing vegetables and load them him- self.</p>
        <p>Mr. Westbrook supported the application by a personal appeal, saying he had known appli- cant all his life and could testify to the hard work and industry by which he had built up his small business. If ever there was a case of a one-man business which deserved consider- ation this was such a case. The applicant was a member of the Stockton Heath V.T.C.</p>
        <p>The Tribunal granted temporary exemption to Ist January, 1917.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>25% INCREASE.</head>
        <p>A 25% INCREASE.</p>
        <p>Bold Street Wesleyans Aim At Development.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Percy Meson has been pastor of Bold-street Wesleyan Church for a year, and to celebrate the fact and propagate a new scheme of development, special services were held on Thursday. At that in the evening Mr. meson thanked the people for all they had done for him, and added that although it had been a year full of difficulties for them all, they had reason for profound thanks. During the past few days there had been growing in their midst a spirit of hope and enthusiasm, and they had not been depressed. With so many away from home upon whom they could have relied they might have let ' things slide, but the workers at that church had not taken up that position, but had decided to do something more than they had yet achieved. They had pledged themselves that in every department associated with the church there should be a 25 per cent. increase by next year. Every department had met and discussed, the means by which it was to he done, and some of them had already had sub- stantial increases. The Sunday school, the Wesley Guild, the Band of Hope, and those in charge of the classes attached to the church were all concerned. On Thursday of last ‘week a united meeting of all the workers was held and they definitely committed themselves to their programme and definitely yielded them- selves to Jesus Christ. It would not be easy; it was not going to be a case of a sudden dash and mighty success, but a constant, steady effort, securing the ground as they were going for- ward and making success absolutely certain. Mr. J. G. Withinshaw, who presided, re- marked that there was nothing more difficult to move than a Church which was only half alive. Like a business, any Church which was to go ahead had to be moved from the top. If the head of a business were vigorous and enter- prising his employes were usually so; but if he were slack they were slack, too. At Bald- street they were fortunate in having an earnest and energetic pastor and a good band of workers, and he wished them every success</p>
        <p>with their ambitious scheme. Bold-street had a great name and, no doubt, they would live up to it.</p>
        <p>-Other speakers were the Rev. W. T. Derry (superintendent of the circuit) and the Rev. T. A. Turney, of Liverpool. The last-named ,+lqo preached during the afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>TO REDUCE BIG DEBT.</head>
        <p>TO REDUCE BIG DEBT.</p>
        <p>Sale Of Work Held At Brick</p>
        <p>Street Mission.</p>
        <p>To reduce a church mortgage debt of £l,OOO, a sale of work was held in the Brick-street Mission on Thursday and Friday. The opening ceremony on Thursday was per- formed by the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), and with his Worship on the platform were Councillor Henshall (chairman), Councillor Shaw and Mr. William Barton (president of the Church). .Referring to the sale .of work, the Chairman said they hoped to raise £2OO as a result of that effort. The workers had laboured very hard, and were very deserving. (Applause.) The Mayor said it was quite true that this was his first public appearance since he bad been selected Mayor for the fourth year. If a salary had been attached to his responsible position he would have refused it, but he felt that baying gone through many of the troublesi, &amp;c., brought about by the war, he thought he might be one of the fittest persons in Warring- ton to extend. the hand of help and sympathy to those cases which came under his notice. (Hear, hear.) Continuing, the Mayor said Ile was always willing to help anything in con- nectfon with the Church, and if all the people of Warrington would do the same' he would be perfectly satisfied. If there was ever a time when the Church was badly needed, it was the present time. In conclusion, the Mayor apologised for the absence of the Mayoress, wh6 had gone to Wales. .</p>
        <p>The takings at Thursday's proceedings were about £9O.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY'S OPENING CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>Councillor J. T. Locker performed the open- ing ckremony yesterday, Mr.' James Hudson presiding. Councillor Locker said it 'must lie very encouraging to the workers to find they had been able to support themselves so well. He had been conversant with their church for a great number of years, and had watched its pro- gress with pleasure and pride. He was pleased 'to hear they had done 60 well the previous day, and hoped they would do better still that day. (Applause.) Mrs. Thos. Smith proposed and Mrs. W. Barton, sen., seconded a vote of thanks TO Councillor Locker for opening the sale and to Mr. Hudson for presiding: A buttonhole was presented to Councillor Locker and Mr. Hudson by Miss A. Bibby. Entertainments and competitions were held each evening.</p>
