        <p>these with cart-grease or any other sticky sub- stance. On no account paint the stem of the tree. Examine the bands frequently, and if it is found that the grease or whatever is used Is in the least dry, smear the bands a second time. If any apples have fallen owing to the attack of the Codlin moth, all such should be gathered up .and put on the fire before the caterpillars have had time to escape from them. \ THE FRUIT CROPS. From what I have seen, ,and the reports of friends, the fruit.,crop is a very poor one, henev it will be necessary to be careful of what we have, the keeping largely depending on the gathering and the mode of storing. A very important point is the right time at which to gather the various sorts. Many people gather much too soon, with the result that the fruits shrivel,and are of no value. Choose a dry day for gathering,- and handle the fruits very care- fully so as not to bruise them in any way. Never lay ;he fruits on straw or hay, as 'these impart a bad' flavour to them and spoil them. The later the season of the apple the longer should it remain on the tree. Frost will not injure the fruit, but the heavy gales ,that" we often get at this season play havoc with it, and to guard against loss in this way one is tempted prematurely to gather the fruit. This applies both to apples and pears.—Wick, in "Farm and Home."</p>
        <p>Deaths. DENTON.—Died of wounds. Private J. Denton. dearly beloved and second son of the late Matter- son Denton and Elizabeth Denton, 21, Dixon- street, Warrington, aged 23 years. Sadly missed by all at home. A good life is too short lived, but a good name endureth for ever. In Memoriam. HARDMAN.—In loving memory of Rifleman John Hardman, of the Rifle Brigade, who was killed by a sniper in France on September 24th, 1915. Sleep on, dear son, in a foreign grave. • Your life for your country you nobly gave, No loved ones beside you to say Goo-bye, But safe in God's keeping now you he. "Duty nobly done." Deeply mourned by MOTHER. SISTERS, and only BROTHER. 47, Scott-street, Warrington. HOWELL.—In loving memory of our dear Mothei, , who passed away October Ist. 1913. oETs flee &amp;JAsCwlisy,,.. "Until the day dawns and thilßeAshßadlw RENSHAW.—In loving memory of our only dear son and brother, Private John David Renshaw (our Jack). 1619, 4th South Lancashire Regiment, who fell in action September 26th, 1915. One long, sad year has passed away, Since our great sorrow fell; Yet in our hearts we mourn the loss Of him we loved so well. . FATHER. MOTHER, SISTERS &amp; BROTHER-IN-LAW. • TAVLIN.—In loving memory of our dear, brother (Bert). Sergeant H. Tavlin, 2nd South' Lancs., who died in France, September 28th, 1914. From his loving SISTERS &amp; BROTHERS.</p>
        <p>MR. and MRS. R. BRIMELOW, 22, Piekmere- street, beg to ACKNOWLEDGE with sincere 'thanks the many expressions of sympathy they have received from friends tespecting the loss of their son, Corporal Joseph Brimelow, killed on duty.</p>
