        <p>PRIVATE P. L. ROWE. Killed.</p>
        <p>much regret to have to tell you that Private P. L. Rowe, 2nd Grenadier. Guards, died at 1.45 a.m. on the 22nd inst. He was brought to hos- pital very severely wounded in the body, and from the first there was very little hope of sav- ing his life. He was buried in the cemetery at- tached to the hospital." Private Rowe, who was. educated at St. Bene- dict's and St. Alban's Schools, was formerly employed by the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., but prior to the war was work- ing as a warder .at the Haydock Lodge Private Asylum. Enlisting in September, 1914, he had been in France 14 months. He is 22 years of age. His father was a school attenclance.officer at Widnes, and served for over 12 years with the Somerset Light Infantry. Corporal John Rowe, his brother, is serving with the Man- chester Regiment. Another brother is .. Sergeant-Major in a Volunteer Training Corps. PRIVATE J. STEWART. Missing Since Loos ; Now Reported Killed. Private John Stewart, Royal Scots Regiment, husband of Mrs. Stewart and father of six chil- dren, who reside at 16, Derby-street, having been missing since the battle of Loos in 1915, the War Office have now. notified Mrs. Stewart that they must regretfully conclude that he has died as no further news has been heard of him. Private Stewart last wrote home on September 24th, 1915, to tell his wife that the British were "going to make things hum," and that She must "look out for developments." He was a native</p>
