        <p>PRIVATE E. PYE Killed.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Heesom,, on September sth, said, "I have' sad news for - you about your dear brother, Ernest Pye. He was killed in action on the 29th August, and died without any pain. Well liked amongst his chums, he was a good chap." The cqmrade adds that a parcel of cigarettes belonging to the dead /Private was opened and distributed among his pals. Private Pye himself wrote home about a week prior to his death. After acknowledging the receipt of a; letter and some "fags," he said he was in the best of health, and in a wood near the trenches. "I am," he proceeds, "getting about sick of this weary life, but we have to make the best of it and hope mr better days to come." Private Pye, who enlisted on the outbreak of war, and had been in France twelve months, was formerly in the band at the Ragged School. Prior to enlisting he was employed by Messrs. Rylands, Bros.. Ltd., and was educated at Silver- street School. He was in his '2oth year. Private Pye's brother-in-law, Rifleman John Heesom, in the King's Royal Rifles, has been in France for 10 months.</p>
        <p>DIED OF WOUNDS. PRIVATE J. DENTON. Dies Of Wounds Which Com- rade Said Were Not Serious. Mrs'. Denton, 21, Dixon-street, has received official news that her son, Private Jim Denton,</p>
        <p>the Front ten weeks. He was educated at Heathside School and worshipped at St. Luke's Church and attended the school. A younger brother, Edward, is serving abroad with the R.F.A., having been in the Army since the outbreak of war.</p>
        <p>SERGT. BRADBURY'S CONDITION. Mr. William Bradbury, a m?mber of the War_ rington Military Tribunal, has been to see his son, Sergeant Bradbury, at the Northampton War Hospital. Although severely wounded in the stomach—the intestines being perforated in no less than nineteen, places—every hope Is entertained by the medical staff for thair patient's ultimate recovery. It is hoped that this gallant young fellow will soon be restored to health. LIEUT. S. J. PRICE. Resigns Commission Owing To 11l- Health. Many people in Warrington will hear with regret that Quartermaster and Hon. Lieutenant S. J. Price, of the South Lancashire Regiment, whose home is in Alexandra-street, relinquished his commission on the 23rd September, owing to ill-health. Mr. Pries is a son of the late Mr. Joseph Price, who served the Colours for forty-eight years, fought in the Indian Mutiny (and for many years was superior barrack sergeant at Orford. Barracks. He joined the South Lanca- shire Regiment in September, nit, and gained rapid promotion. He was gazetted honorary lieutenant and quartermaster on November 6th,</p>
